APX.us = {"country":{"name":"United States","tagline":"From mission vines and colonial vine laws to 280 AVAs and the most valuable Cabernet on earth.","classification":"Wine origin in the US is regulated federally by the Alcohol and Tobacco Tax and Trade Bureau (TTB). American Viticultural Areas (AVAs) are delimited grape-growing regions defined by geography, climate and soils, proposed by petition and approved after public comment; there are 280 as of October 2026, from Ulupalakua on Maui (about 70 acres) to Ozark Mountain (about 33 million acres). Unlike European appellations, an AVA controls only origin: there are no rules on grape varieties, yields or winemaking.\n\nLabelling rules: a wine naming an AVA must contain at least 85 percent grapes from that AVA; a state or county appellation needs 75 percent (California requires 100 percent for 'California', Oregon 95 percent for AVAs and 100 percent for the state, Washington 95 percent for AVAs). A varietal label requires at least 75 percent of the named grape (Oregon 90 percent for most varieties). A vintage date requires 95 percent from that year when an AVA is named (85 percent with a state or county). 'Estate bottled' requires the winery and vineyards to be in the same AVA. Some states require conjunctive labelling (e.g. Napa Valley must appear with any Napa sub-AVA, Sonoma County with Sonoma AVAs). Semi-generic names such as 'Champagne' and 'Chablis' can still appear on labels approved before 2006."},"regions":[{"name":"Napa Valley","slug":"napa-valley","tagline":"Fifty kilometres of valley floor and mountain that taught the world to take American Cabernet seriously.","ids":["us-atlas-peak","us-calistoga","us-chiles-valley","us-coombsville","us-crystal-springs-of-napa-valley","us-diamond-mountain-district","us-howell-mountain","us-los-carneros","us-mt-veeder","us-napa-valley","us-oak-knoll-district-of-napa-valley","us-oakville","us-rutherford","us-spring-mountain-district","us-st-helena","us-stags-leap-district","us-wild-horse-valley","us-yountville"]},{"name":"Sonoma County","slug":"sonoma-county","tagline":"Coast, fog and redwoods on one side, Zinfandel benchland and Cabernet valleys on the other: California's most varied county.","ids":["us-alexander-valley","us-bennett-valley","us-chalk-hill","us-dry-creek-valley","us-fort-ross-seaview","us-fountaingrove-district","us-green-valley-of-russian-river-valley","us-knights-valley","us-moon-mountain-district-sonoma-county","us-northern-sonoma","us-petaluma-gap","us-pine-mountain-cloverdale-peak","us-rockpile","us-russian-river-valley","us-sonoma-coast","us-sonoma-mountain","us-sonoma-valley","us-west-sonoma-coast"]},{"name":"Mendocino County","slug":"mendocino-county","tagline":"Redwoods, fog and old Italian vines: California's northernmost serious wine county, and a pioneer of organic farming.","ids":["us-anderson-valley","us-cole-ranch","us-comptche","us-covelo","us-dos-rios","us-eagle-peak-mendocino-county","us-mcdowell-valley","us-mendocino","us-mendocino-ridge","us-potter-valley","us-redwood-valley","us-yorkville-highlands"]},{"name":"Lake County","slug":"lake-county","tagline":"High-elevation volcanic country around California's largest natural lake.","ids":["us-benmore-valley","us-big-valley-district-lake-county","us-clear-lake","us-guenoc-valley","us-high-valley","us-kelsey-bench-lake-county","us-long-valley-lake-county","us-red-hills-lake-county","us-upper-lake-valley"]},{"name":"Central Coast","slug":"central-coast","tagline":"Four hundred kilometres of coast where east-west valleys pull the Pacific inland: Pinot Noir, Chardonnay, Rhône blends and mountain Cabernet.","ids":["us-arroyo-seco","us-ben-lomond-mountain","us-carmel-valley","us-central-coast","us-chalone","us-cienega-valley","us-gabilan-mountains","us-hames-valley","us-lime-kiln-valley","us-monterey","us-mt-harlan","us-paicines","us-san-antonio-valley","us-san-benito","us-san-bernabe","us-san-lucas","us-santa-cruz-mountains","us-santa-lucia-highlands"]},{"name":"Santa Barbara County","slug":"santa-barbara-county","tagline":"Where the mountains turn sideways and the Pacific pours in: California's coolest serious Pinot Noir country.","ids":["us-alisos-canyon","us-ballard-canyon","us-happy-canyon-of-santa-barbara","us-los-olivos-district","us-santa-maria-valley","us-santa-ynez-valley","us-sta-rita-hills"]},{"name":"Paso Robles","slug":"paso-robles","tagline":"A big, hot, limestone-laced district split in two by Highway 101 and subdivided into 11 AVAs.","ids":["us-adelaida-district","us-creston-district","us-el-pomar-district","us-paso-robles","us-paso-robles-estrella-district","us-paso-robles-geneseo-district","us-paso-robles-highlands-district","us-paso-robles-willow-creek-district","us-san-juan-creek","us-san-miguel-district","us-santa-margarita-ranch","us-templeton-gap-district"]},{"name":"Lodi","slug":"lodi","tagline":"Old, own-rooted Zinfandel on sandy river soils: the grower capital of California.","ids":["us-alta-mesa","us-borden-ranch","us-clements-hills","us-cosumnes-river","us-jahant","us-lodi","us-mokelumne-river","us-sloughhouse"]},{"name":"Sierra Foothills","slug":"sierra-foothills","tagline":"Gold Rush country, where miners planted Zinfandel and some of the vines are still alive.","ids":["us-california-shenandoah-valley","us-el-dorado","us-fair-play","us-fiddletown","us-north-yuba","us-sierra-foothills"]},{"name":"Southern California","slug":"southern-california","tagline":"Where California wine began, then vanished under freeways: missions, Cucamonga's old vines, Temecula's wineries and Malibu's hillsides.","ids":["us-antelope-valley-of-the-california-high-desert","us-cucamonga-valley","us-leona-valley","us-malibu-coast","us-malibu-newton-canyon","us-palos-verdes-peninsula","us-ramona-valley","us-saddle-rock-malibu","us-san-luis-rey","us-san-pasqual-valley","us-sierra-pelona-valley","us-south-coast","us-tehachapi-mountains","us-temecula-valley","us-yucaipa-valley"]},{"name":"Central Valley","slug":"central-valley","tagline":"The irrigated heartland where most California wine by volume is grown.","ids":["us-diablo-grande","us-madera","us-paulsell-valley","us-river-junction","us-salado-creek","us-squaw-valley-miramonte","us-tracy-hills"]},{"name":"Willamette Valley","slug":"willamette-valley","tagline":"A cool, green valley between the Coast Range and the Cascades where Oregon proved it could grow great Pinot Noir.","ids":["us-chehalem-mountains","us-dundee-hills","us-eola-amity-hills","us-laurelwood-district","us-lower-long-tom","us-mcminnville","us-mount-pisgah-polk-county-oregon","us-ribbon-ridge","us-tualatin-hills","us-van-duzer-corridor","us-willamette-valley","us-yamhill-carlton"]},{"name":"Southern Oregon","slug":"southern-oregon","tagline":"Warm valleys under old mountains: Tempranillo, Syrah and Bordeaux varieties alongside Pinot Noir.","ids":["us-applegate-valley","us-elkton-oregon","us-red-hill-douglas-county-oregon","us-rogue-valley","us-southern-oregon","us-umpqua-valley"]},{"name":"Washington","slug":"washington","tagline":"Desert vineyards on ice-age flood soils, irrigated by the Columbia: America's second-largest wine state.","ids":["us-puget-sound"]},{"name":"Columbia Valley","slug":"columbia-valley","tagline":"A semi-desert basin of 11 million acres that contains almost all of Washington's vines.","ids":["us-ancient-lakes-of-columbia-valley","us-beverly-washington","us-columbia-hills","us-columbia-valley","us-horse-heaven-hills","us-lake-chelan","us-naches-heights","us-rocky-reach","us-royal-slope","us-the-burn-of-columbia-valley","us-wahluke-slope","us-white-bluffs"]},{"name":"Idaho","slug":"idaho","tagline":"High-desert vineyards along the Snake River, at some of the highest elevations in the Northwest.","ids":["us-eagle-foothills","us-lewis-clark-valley","us-snake-river-valley"]},{"name":"New York","slug":"new-york","tagline":"Riesling on glacial lakes, Merlot by the sea, and America's oldest winery in the Hudson Valley.","ids":["us-champlain-valley-of-new-york","us-hudson-river-region","us-niagara-escarpment","us-upper-hudson"]},{"name":"New England","slug":"new-england","tagline":"Coastal vineyards and cold-hardy hybrids on rocky glacial soils.","ids":["us-eastern-connecticut-highlands","us-marthas-vineyard","us-nashoba-valley","us-southeastern-new-england","us-western-connecticut-highlands"]},{"name":"Virginia","slug":"virginia","tagline":"Where English colonists were ordered to plant vines in 1619, and where Jefferson failed so the state could succeed.","ids":["us-middleburg-virginia","us-monticello","us-north-fork-of-roanoke","us-northern-neck-george-washington-birthplace","us-rocky-knob","us-shenandoah-valley","us-virginia-peninsula","us-virginias-eastern-shore"]},{"name":"Mid-Atlantic","slug":"mid-atlantic","tagline":"Pennsylvania, New Jersey and Maryland: colonial wine dreams and a modern coastal-plain revival.","ids":["us-cape-may-peninsula","us-catoctin","us-central-delaware-valley","us-cumberland-valley","us-lancaster-valley","us-lehigh-valley","us-linganore","us-outer-coastal-plain","us-warren-hills"]},{"name":"Southeast","slug":"southeast","tagline":"Muscadine country, mountain vinifera and the oldest cultivated vine in America.","ids":["us-appalachian-high-country","us-crest-of-the-blue-ridge-henderson-county","us-dahlonega-plateau","us-haw-river-valley","us-mississippi-delta","us-nine-lakes-of-east-tennessee","us-swan-creek","us-tryon-foothills","us-upper-cumberland","us-upper-hiwassee-highlands","us-yadkin-valley"]},{"name":"Midwest","slug":"midwest","tagline":"Missouri's German wine country, the first AVA, and the rootstocks that saved Europe.","ids":["us-alexandria-lakes","us-altus","us-arkansas-mountain","us-augusta","us-hermann","us-indiana-uplands","us-kanawha-river-valley","us-lake-wisconsin","us-loess-hills-district","us-loramie-creek","us-ohio-river-valley","us-ozark-highlands","us-ozark-mountain","us-shawnee-hills","us-upper-mississippi-river-valley","us-wisconsin-ledge"]},{"name":"Great Lakes","slug":"great-lakes","tagline":"Lake-effect vineyards in Michigan and Ohio: Riesling on cherry-orchard peninsulas and island wineries in Lake Erie.","ids":["us-fennville","us-grand-river-valley","us-isle-st-george","us-lake-michigan-shore","us-leelanau-peninsula","us-old-mission-peninsula","us-tip-of-the-mitt"]},{"name":"Texas","slug":"texas","tagline":"Mission vines by 1650s El Paso, Munson's rootstocks, and a modern boom split between the High Plains and the Hill Country.","ids":["us-bell-mountain","us-escondido-valley","us-fredericksburg-in-the-texas-hill-country","us-texas-davis-mountains","us-texas-high-plains","us-texas-hill-country","us-texoma"]},{"name":"Southwest and Mountain West","slug":"southwest-and-mountain-west","tagline":"High-desert vineyards in Arizona, New Mexico and Colorado, and the oldest wine tradition in the United States.","ids":["us-grand-valley","us-mesilla-valley","us-middle-rio-grande-valley","us-mimbres-valley","us-sonoita","us-verde-valley","us-west-elks","us-willcox"]},{"name":"Columbia Gorge","slug":"columbia-gorge","tagline":"","ids":["us-columbia-gorge"]},{"name":"Far North California","slug":"far-north-california","tagline":"","ids":["us-inwood-valley","us-manton-valley","us-seiad-valley","us-trinity-lakes","us-willow-creek"]},{"name":"Finger Lakes","slug":"finger-lakes","tagline":"","ids":["us-cayuga-lake","us-finger-lakes","us-seneca-lake"]},{"name":"Hawaii","slug":"hawaii","tagline":"","ids":["us-ulupalakua"]},{"name":"Lake Erie","slug":"lake-erie","tagline":"","ids":["us-lake-erie"]},{"name":"Long Island","slug":"long-island","tagline":"","ids":["us-long-island","us-north-fork-of-long-island","us-the-hamptons-long-island"]},{"name":"North Coast","slug":"north-coast","tagline":"","ids":["us-north-coast","us-solano-county-green-valley","us-suisun-valley"]},{"name":"Sacramento Valley","slug":"sacramento-valley","tagline":"","ids":["us-capay-valley","us-clarksburg","us-dunnigan-hills","us-merritt-island","us-winters-highlands"]},{"name":"San Francisco Bay","slug":"san-francisco-bay","tagline":"","ids":["us-contra-costa","us-lamorinda","us-livermore-valley","us-pacheco-pass","us-san-francisco-bay","us-san-ysidro-district","us-santa-clara-valley"]},{"name":"San Luis Obispo County","slug":"san-luis-obispo-county","tagline":"","ids":["us-arroyo-grande-valley","us-edna-valley","us-san-luis-obispo-coast","us-york-mountain"]},{"name":"Walla Walla Valley","slug":"walla-walla-valley","tagline":"","ids":["us-the-rocks-district-of-milton-freewater","us-walla-walla-valley"]},{"name":"Yakima Valley","slug":"yakima-valley","tagline":"","ids":["us-candy-mountain","us-goose-gap","us-rattlesnake-hills","us-red-mountain","us-snipes-mountain","us-yakima-valley"]}],"items":{"us-adelaida-district":{"name":"Adelaida District","cat":"AVA","region":"Paso Robles","subregion":"Paso Robles","created":"2014","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Syrah, Grenache, Mourvèdre, Roussanne, Cabernet Sauvignon, Zinfandel","summary":"The northwestern hills of Paso Robles (2014), at 275 to 670 m in the Santa Lucia foothills, with calcareous shale and limestone soils, more rain than the east and cool nights. It is home to Tablas Creek, where the Perrin family of Château de Beaucastel and importer Robert Haas planted French Rhône clones from 1989, and to Justin's Isosceles and Halter Ranch.","producers":["Tablas Creek","Justin","Halter Ranch","Daou","Adelaida Vineyards","Villa Creek"],"rslug":"paso-robles"},"us-alexander-valley":{"name":"Alexander Valley","cat":"AVA","region":"Sonoma County","subregion":"Northern Sonoma","created":"1984","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot, Chardonnay, Zinfandel, Sauvignon Blanc","summary":"The upper Russian River valley (1984, expanded 1990s to 2008) from Healdsburg north to Cloverdale, named for Cyrus Alexander, who settled here in the 1840s. It is one of Sonoma's warmest areas, with less fog than the lower river, and it is the county's Cabernet Sauvignon heartland: round, plush and approachable, with soft tannin and notes of black cherry and cocoa.\n\nHillside vineyards on the Mayacamas side give darker, firmer wines. Jordan (1972) and Silver Oak's Alexander Valley Cabernet made the style famous; Simi has made wine at Healdsburg since 1876.","terroir":"Deep alluvial loams on the valley floor, gravelly benches and volcanic hillsides on the east.","producers":["Jordan","Silver Oak (Alexander Valley)","Stonestreet","Simi","Robert Young","Hawkes"],"rslug":"sonoma-county"},"us-alexandria-lakes":{"name":"Alexandria Lakes","cat":"AVA","region":"Midwest","subregion":"Minnesota","created":"2005","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Frontenac, La Crescent, Marquette","summary":"A small AVA (2005) of about 9,000 acres among the glacial lakes near Alexandria, Minnesota, created around Carlos Creek Winery and among the most northerly AVAs in the US. Cold-hardy hybrids.","rslug":"midwest"},"us-alisos-canyon":{"name":"Alisos Canyon","cat":"AVA","region":"Santa Barbara County","subregion":"Central Coast","created":"2020","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Syrah, Grenache, Sauvignon Blanc","summary":"A small AVA (2020) of about 5,800 acres in the hills between the Santa Maria and Santa Ynez valleys, near Los Alamos. A cool, foggy marine climate and sandy, calcareous soils suit Syrah and Rhône varieties.","producers":["Alisos Canyon wineries (Martian Ranch)"],"rslug":"santa-barbara-county"},"us-alta-mesa":{"name":"Alta Mesa","cat":"AVA","region":"Lodi","subregion":"Lodi","created":"2006","styles":["red"],"grapes":"Zinfandel, Syrah, Cabernet Sauvignon","summary":"A high, flat terrace (2006) in the northeastern part of Lodi, in Sacramento County, with shallow sandy loam over hardpan that limits vine vigour. It is warmer than western Lodi because it lies farther from the Delta breezes, and it grows Zinfandel and Rhône varieties.","rslug":"lodi"},"us-altus":{"name":"Altus","cat":"AVA","region":"Midwest","subregion":"Arkansas","created":"1984","styles":["white","red","sweet"],"grapes":"Muscadine, Cynthiana, Niagara, Chardonnay, Zinfandel","summary":"A small AVA (1984) around the town of Altus in western Arkansas, settled by Swiss and German immigrants in the 1880s. Wiederkehr Wine Cellars (1880) and Post Winery (1880) are among the oldest in the South; Mount Bethel and Chateau aux Arc also operate here.","producers":["Wiederkehr","Post Familie","Chateau aux Arc"],"rslug":"midwest"},"us-ancient-lakes-of-columbia-valley":{"name":"Ancient Lakes of Columbia Valley","cat":"AVA","region":"Columbia Valley","subregion":"Columbia Valley","created":"2012","styles":["white","red"],"grapes":"Riesling, Chardonnay, Pinot Gris, Sauvignon Blanc","summary":"A cool northern AVA (2012) of about 162,800 acres near Quincy, named for lakes left in coulees carved by the Missoula Floods. Caliche-rich soils and a cooler climate make it Washington's best area for crisp Riesling, Chardonnay and Pinot Gris. At approval it had 1,399 acres in six vineyards.","producers":["Cave B","White Heron","Jones of Washington"],"rslug":"columbia-valley"},"us-anderson-valley":{"name":"Anderson Valley","cat":"AVA","region":"Mendocino County","subregion":"Mendocino","created":"1983","styles":["red","white","sparkling"],"grapes":"Pinot Noir, Chardonnay, Gewürztraminer, Riesling, Pinot Gris","summary":"A narrow, 25 km valley (1983) along the Navarro River that opens toward the Pacific, so fog and cool air pour in every evening. It is one of California's coolest wine valleys and its most Alsatian: Gewürztraminer, Riesling and Pinot Gris grow beside Pinot Noir and Chardonnay, and the valley hosts an annual Alsace festival.\n\nRoederer Estate arrived from Champagne in 1982 and showed that the valley could make world-class sparkling wine; its L'Ermitage is a benchmark. Pinot Noir from the 'deep end' near Philo and Navarro is tense, floral and savoury.","terroir":"Marine sedimentary soils on benches and slopes; fog and large daily temperature swings.","producers":["Roederer Estate","Navarro Vineyards","Husch","Goldeneye","Littorai (sourcing)","Handley","Drew","Lazy Creek"],"nerd_note":"The valley had its own jargon, Boontling, invented by Boonville residents around 1900; a 'horn of Bahl' is a glass of good wine.","rslug":"mendocino-county"},"us-antelope-valley-of-the-california-high-desert":{"name":"Antelope Valley of the California High Desert","cat":"AVA","region":"Southern California","subregion":"Southern California","created":"2011","styles":["red"],"grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, Zinfandel, Syrah","summary":"A large AVA (2011) in the western Mojave Desert in Los Angeles and Kern counties, at around 700 to 900 m. Hot days, cold nights, strong winds and very low rainfall; vineyards are small and irrigated.","rslug":"southern-california"},"us-appalachian-high-country":{"name":"Appalachian High Country","cat":"AVA","region":"Southeast","subregion":"North Carolina, Tennessee, Virginia","created":"2016","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Marquette, Frontenac, La Crescent, Riesling","summary":"A high, mountainous AVA (2016) of about 2,400 square miles straddling North Carolina, Tennessee and Virginia around Boone, with vineyards from about 900 to 1,200 m, among the highest in the East. Short seasons and cold winters favour cold-hardy hybrids such as Marquette and Frontenac. At approval it had 21 vineyards (71 acres) and 10 wineries.","rslug":"southeast"},"us-applegate-valley":{"name":"Applegate Valley","cat":"AVA","region":"Southern Oregon","subregion":"Rogue Valley","created":"2000","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Syrah, Zinfandel, Viognier, Cabernet Sauvignon, Vermentino","summary":"A sub-AVA (2000) of the Rogue Valley along the Applegate River, warm and dry with decomposed granite and alluvial soils. Small family wineries grow Syrah, Zinfandel, Viognier and Rhône and Bordeaux varieties; Troon converted to biodynamic farming.","producers":["Troon","Cowhorn","Wooldridge Creek","Red Lily"],"rslug":"southern-oregon"},"us-arkansas-mountain":{"name":"Arkansas Mountain","cat":"AVA","region":"Midwest","subregion":"Arkansas","created":"1986","styles":["white","red"],"grapes":"Cynthiana, Vignoles, Chardonnay, Riesling, Muscadine","summary":"An AVA (1986) of about 2.9 million acres in the Arkansas River valley and the Boston Mountains of northwestern Arkansas, petitioned by Al Wiederkehr. Swiss and German immigrants planted here in the 1880s.","contains":["Altus"],"rslug":"midwest"},"us-arroyo-grande-valley":{"name":"Arroyo Grande Valley","cat":"AVA","region":"San Luis Obispo County","subregion":"San Luis Obispo Coast","created":"1990","styles":["red","white","sparkling"],"grapes":"Pinot Noir, Chardonnay, Zinfandel","summary":"A valley (1990) southeast of Edna Valley that grades from cool and foggy near the coast to warmer at its inland end. Talley Vineyards' Rincon and Rosemary's vineyards give fine Pinot Noir and Chardonnay; at the warmer top end, Saucelito Canyon farms Zinfandel planted in 1880.","producers":["Talley","Saucelito Canyon","Laetitia"],"rslug":"san-luis-obispo-county"},"us-arroyo-seco":{"name":"Arroyo Seco","cat":"AVA","region":"Central Coast","subregion":"Monterey","created":"1983","styles":["white","red"],"grapes":"Chardonnay, Riesling, Pinot Noir, Cabernet Sauvignon","summary":"An AVA (1983, boundaries adjusted 2006, 2018 and 2021) around the Arroyo Seco river where it emerges from the Santa Lucia Range near Greenfield. The cool eastern section is known for Riesling and Chardonnay grown on 'Greenfield potatoes', rounded river cobbles that store heat. The warmer canyon to the west grows Cabernet and Zinfandel.","producers":["J. Lohr (Arroyo Vista)","Ventana","Scheid"],"rslug":"central-coast"},"us-atlas-peak":{"name":"Atlas Peak","cat":"AVA","region":"Napa Valley","subregion":"Napa Valley","created":"1992","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot, Cabernet Franc, Sangiovese, Chardonnay","summary":"High mountain AVA (1992) in the Vaca Range northeast of Napa city, with vineyards from about 230 to 800 m. The elevation keeps summer afternoons cooler than on the valley floor, nights are cold, and the red volcanic soils are thin and rocky. Cabernet Sauvignon is fresh, dark-fruited and firmly built.\n\nAtlas Peak Vineyards was founded in the 1980s by a partnership that included Piero Antinori, who planted Sangiovese here before it was fashionable; Antinori's Antica estate continues the link. On 8 October 2017 the Atlas Fire started on these slopes and burned through vineyards and homes across the district.","terroir":"Shallow volcanic soils, red and rocky, on a high plateau and slopes; above the fog with cool nights.","producers":["Antica Napa Valley (Antinori)","Stagecoach Vineyard (grower)","Acumen","Elan"],"nerd_note":"Atlas Peak was one of the early AVAs defined largely by elevation and is a source of Sangiovese that pre-dates the 1990s 'Cal-Ital' boom.","rslug":"napa-valley"},"us-augusta":{"name":"Augusta","cat":"AVA","region":"Midwest","subregion":"Missouri","created":"1980","styles":["red","white","fortified"],"grapes":"Norton, Chambourcin, Vignoles, Chardonel, Vidal","summary":"The first American Viticultural Area, approved on 20 June 1980, eight months before Napa Valley. It covers about 15,000 acres around the town of Augusta on the Missouri River west of St. Louis, founded by German settlers in the 1830s; before Prohibition it had a dozen wineries. Loess-capped bluffs and a valley moderated by the river grow Norton, Chambourcin, Vignoles and Chardonel. Mount Pleasant Estates (1859) is the anchor winery.\n\nAugusta's 19th-century winemakers were mostly German immigrants, many of them liberal refugees of the failed revolutions of 1848. The Missouri River Valley was dubbed the 'Missouri Rhineland'. After Prohibition the industry vanished until Lucian Dressel bought Mount Pleasant in 1966 and replanted. Norton, a native grape that thrives in humid summers, makes deep, inky, high-acid reds that are the region's calling card.","producers":["Mount Pleasant Estates","Augusta Winery","Balducci","Noboleis"],"nerd_note":"Augusta won the race to be first partly because the 1978 rules were brand new and its petition was simple: a small, well-defined valley with a clear historical name.","rslug":"midwest"},"us-ballard-canyon":{"name":"Ballard Canyon","cat":"AVA","region":"Santa Barbara County","subregion":"Santa Ynez Valley","created":"2013","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Syrah, Grenache, Sauvignon Blanc","summary":"A small, north-south canyon AVA (2013) between Buellton and Los Olivos, with about 7,800 acres. Its growers decided to focus on Syrah, which shows dark fruit and savoury, peppery depth on the canyon's calcareous and sandy soils. Grenache and Sauvignon Blanc also grow well.","producers":["Stolpman","Beckmen (Purisima Mountain)","Larner","Jonata","Saarloos"],"rslug":"santa-barbara-county"},"us-bell-mountain":{"name":"Bell Mountain","cat":"AVA","region":"Texas","subregion":"Texas Hill Country","created":"1986","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, Chardonnay","summary":"Texas's first AVA (1986), a single granite-and-sand hill north of Fredericksburg owned by the Oberhelman family. It would be enclosed by the proposed Llano Uplift AVA.","rslug":"texas"},"us-ben-lomond-mountain":{"name":"Ben Lomond Mountain","cat":"AVA","region":"Central Coast","subregion":"Santa Cruz Mountains","created":"1987","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Pinot Noir, Chardonnay, Cabernet Sauvignon","summary":"A small mountain AVA (1987) inside the Santa Cruz Mountains, on a ridge northwest of Santa Cruz above the fog line, at 120 to 800 m. Sandy soils from granite and limestone; cool Pinot Noir, Chardonnay and Cabernet.","rslug":"central-coast"},"us-benmore-valley":{"name":"Benmore Valley","cat":"AVA","region":"Lake County","subregion":"North Coast","created":"1991","styles":["white"],"grapes":"Chardonnay","summary":"A small, high valley (1991) in the Mayacamas on the western edge of Lake County, at about 730 m. Once planted mainly to Chardonnay for large brands, it now has few vines.","rslug":"lake-county"},"us-bennett-valley":{"name":"Bennett Valley","cat":"AVA","region":"Sonoma County","subregion":"Sonoma Valley","created":"2003","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Merlot, Syrah, Sauvignon Blanc, Pinot Noir, Chardonnay","summary":"A small AVA (2003) in the hills southeast of Santa Rosa, cooled by marine air that flows through the Crane Canyon gap from the Petaluma side. That coolness suits Merlot, Syrah, Sauvignon Blanc and Pinot Noir. Matanzas Creek (1977), known for Merlot and Sauvignon Blanc and its lavender fields, is the best-known winery.","producers":["Matanzas Creek","Bennett Valley Cellars"],"rslug":"sonoma-county"},"us-beverly-washington":{"name":"Beverly, Washington","cat":"AVA","region":"Columbia Valley","subregion":"Columbia Valley","created":"2024","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot, Chardonnay","summary":"A small AVA (2024) of about 2,415 acres on terraces above the Columbia River at the hamlet of Beverly, just north of Sentinel Gap. It was proposed as 'Wanapum Village', but the petitioner asked for a new name after members of the Wanapum tribe objected to the use of their name, and TTB approved it as 'Beverly, Washington'.","rslug":"columbia-valley"},"us-big-valley-district-lake-county":{"name":"Big Valley District-Lake County","cat":"AVA","region":"Lake County","subregion":"Clear Lake","created":"2013","styles":["white","red"],"grapes":"Sauvignon Blanc, Cabernet Sauvignon, Zinfandel","summary":"A flat basin (2013) on the southwest shore of Clear Lake around Kelseyville, with about 1,800 acres of vines at approval. High water tables and cold-air pooling give a cooler, frostier site than the surrounding hills; Sauvignon Blanc is the signature.","rslug":"lake-county"},"us-borden-ranch":{"name":"Borden Ranch","cat":"AVA","region":"Lodi","subregion":"Lodi","created":"2006","styles":["red"],"grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, Zinfandel, Petite Sirah","summary":"Hilly eastern Lodi AVA (2006) of cobbled, gravelly old river terraces at the foot of the Sierra, on the old Borden ranch. Bordeaux varieties and Zinfandel.","rslug":"lodi"},"us-california-shenandoah-valley":{"name":"California Shenandoah Valley","cat":"AVA","region":"Sierra Foothills","subregion":"Sierra Foothills","created":"1982","styles":["red"],"grapes":"Zinfandel, Barbera, Syrah, Primitivo, Sangiovese","summary":"The core of Amador County wine (1983), a valley of rolling hills around Plymouth at 300 to 600 m, with decomposed granite and volcanic soils. It holds some of the oldest Zinfandel vines in California, including the Original Grandpère vineyard claimed to date from before 1869, and Deaver Vineyard, whose fruit made Sutter Home's 1968 Amador Zinfandel. Barbera and Rhône varieties also do well.\n\nThe name includes 'California' to separate it from the older Shenandoah Valley AVA of Virginia and West Virginia.","producers":["Sobon Estate","Terre Rouge","Turley (Amador)","Andis","Vino Noceto","Easton"],"rslug":"sierra-foothills"},"us-calistoga":{"name":"Calistoga","cat":"AVA","region":"Napa Valley","subregion":"Napa Valley","created":"2009","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, Zinfandel, Petite Sirah, Charbono, Cabernet Franc, Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc","summary":"Napa's northernmost and warmest valley-floor AVA, approved in late 2009 after a long fight over the use of the name by a brand that sourced grapes elsewhere. It sits at the head of the valley under Mount St. Helena, where the valley floor narrows and is ringed by the Mayacamas and Vaca ranges.\n\nDays are hot, but cool air spills in at night through the Knights Valley gap from the Russian River side, so the daily temperature swing is among the largest in Napa. The wines are bold and dark: Cabernet Sauvignon with real tannic grip, plus Zinfandel, Petite Sirah and Charbono from old vineyards that never got replanted to Cabernet. Chateau Montelena, whose 1973 Chardonnay won the white half of the 1976 Paris tasting, is the most famous estate; Araujo's former Eisele Vineyard, now Eisele Vineyard estate, is its cult Cabernet.","terroir":"Volcanic soils derived from Mount St. Helena and the Sonoma Volcanics, plus alluvial fans; hot afternoons and cold nights.","producers":["Chateau Montelena","Eisele Vineyard (formerly Araujo)","Larkmead","Tofanelli","Storybook Mountain"],"nerd_note":"The town's name is a slip of the tongue: founder Sam Brannan meant to promise 'the Saratoga of California' and is said to have blurted 'the Calistoga of Sarafornia'. In 2024 TTB adjusted Calistoga's boundary when it created the neighbouring Crystal Springs of Napa Valley AVA.","rslug":"napa-valley"},"us-candy-mountain":{"name":"Candy Mountain","cat":"AVA","region":"Yakima Valley","subregion":"Yakima Valley","created":"2020","styles":["red"],"grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, Syrah, Merlot","summary":"One of Washington's smallest AVAs (2020), about 815 acres on the southwest-facing slope of Candy Mountain near Richland, close to Red Mountain. Steep slopes, shallow loess and wind give dense Cabernet. TTB modified the Yakima Valley boundary to include it.","rslug":"yakima-valley"},"us-capay-valley":{"name":"Capay Valley","cat":"AVA","region":"Sacramento Valley","subregion":"Yolo County","created":"2002","styles":["red"],"grapes":"Syrah, Tempranillo, Grenache","summary":"A long valley (2002) in western Yolo County along Cache Creek, between the Blue Ridge and the Capay Hills. Warm and dry, it grows Rhône and Mediterranean varieties on a small scale, and the area is known more widely for organic farms.","rslug":"sacramento-valley"},"us-cape-may-peninsula":{"name":"Cape May Peninsula","cat":"AVA","region":"Mid-Atlantic","subregion":"Outer Coastal Plain","created":"2018","styles":["white","red"],"grapes":"Albariño, Chardonnay, Merlot, Cabernet Franc","summary":"A peninsula AVA (2018) at New Jersey's southern tip, surrounded by the Atlantic and Delaware Bay. The petition stressed its warmth: water keeps autumns mild and the frost-free season long. Sandy soils; at approval six vineyards on about 115 acres. Albariño, Merlot and Chardonnay.","producers":["Cape May Winery","Hawk Haven","Willow Creek"],"rslug":"mid-atlantic"},"us-carmel-valley":{"name":"Carmel Valley","cat":"AVA","region":"Central Coast","subregion":"Monterey","created":"1982","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot, Chardonnay","summary":"A narrow valley (1983) running inland from Carmel-by-the-Sea, sheltered from fog at its upper end by the Santa Lucia Range, where vineyards sit at 200 to 600 m. It is warm enough for Cabernet Sauvignon and Merlot; Durney Vineyards (later Heller Estate) planted the first commercial vineyard in 1968.","producers":["Heller Estate","Boekenoogen","Joullian"],"rslug":"central-coast"},"us-catoctin":{"name":"Catoctin","cat":"AVA","region":"Mid-Atlantic","subregion":"Maryland","created":"1983","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Cabernet Franc, Merlot, Chardonnay, Albariño","summary":"A Piedmont AVA (1983) in Frederick and Washington counties, Maryland, at the foot of the Catoctin Mountains. Black Ankle and Sugarloaf Mountain are leading wineries nearby.","producers":["Catoctin Breeze","Sugarloaf Mountain"],"rslug":"mid-atlantic"},"us-cayuga-lake":{"name":"Cayuga Lake","cat":"AVA","region":"Finger Lakes","subregion":"Finger Lakes","created":"1988","styles":["white","red","sparkling"],"grapes":"Riesling, Pinot Noir, Cabernet Franc, Chardonnay, Cayuga White","summary":"The first Finger Lakes sub-AVA (1988), on the slopes of the longest Finger Lake. Cayuga is deep enough to moderate the climate, though slightly cooler than Seneca; the Cayuga Wine Trail (1983) was the first organised wine trail in America. Riesling and sparkling wines lead.","producers":["Heart & Hands","Hosmer","Bloomer Creek","Sheldrake Point","Lucas"],"nerd_note":"Cayuga White, a hybrid bred at Cornell's Geneva station and released in 1972, is named for the lake.","rslug":"finger-lakes"},"us-central-coast":{"name":"Central Coast","cat":"AVA","region":"Central Coast","subregion":"California","created":"1985","styles":["red","white","sparkling"],"grapes":"Chardonnay, Pinot Noir, Cabernet Sauvignon, Syrah, Zinfandel","summary":"Umbrella AVA (1985, adjusted 1999, 2006 and 2024) of about 6.8 million acres covering the coastal counties from Contra Costa and Alameda south to Santa Barbara. It is used for multi-county blends and contains famous AVAs from the Santa Cruz Mountains to Sta. Rita Hills.","rslug":"central-coast"},"us-central-delaware-valley":{"name":"Central Delaware Valley","cat":"AVA","region":"Mid-Atlantic","subregion":"New Jersey and Pennsylvania","created":"1984","styles":["white","red"],"grapes":"Chardonnay, Cabernet Franc, Chambourcin","summary":"An AVA (1984) along both banks of the Delaware River in Pennsylvania (Bucks County) and New Jersey (Hunterdon and Mercer), sheltered by the valley; Unionville Vineyards and others make Chardonnay and Bordeaux varieties.","producers":["Unionville Vineyards"],"rslug":"mid-atlantic"},"us-chalk-hill":{"name":"Chalk Hill","cat":"AVA","region":"Sonoma County","subregion":"Russian River Valley","created":"1983","styles":["white","red"],"grapes":"Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Merlot, Cabernet Sauvignon","summary":"A warm corner of the Russian River Valley (1983) east of Windsor, in hills above the fog. The 'chalk' is a misnomer: the pale soils are volcanic ash from Mount St. Helena's eruptions, mixed with river sediments. It is warmer than the rest of the Russian River Valley and better suited to Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc and Bordeaux varieties than to Pinot Noir.","producers":["Chalk Hill Estate","Verité (part sourced)","Rodney Strong"],"rslug":"sonoma-county"},"us-chalone":{"name":"Chalone","cat":"AVA","region":"Central Coast","subregion":"Gabilan Mountains","created":"1982","styles":["white","red"],"grapes":"Chardonnay, Pinot Noir, Chenin Blanc, Pinot Blanc","summary":"A remote, high bench (1982) at about 500 m in the Gavilan (Gabilan) Mountains, under the volcanic spires of Pinnacles National Park. Its limestone and granite soils are rare in California, and vines were first planted here around 1919. Dick Graff bought the property in 1965 and made Chalone's Burgundian Chardonnay and Pinot Noir; its 1974 Chardonnay was third among the whites in Paris in 1976.","producers":["Chalone Vineyard"],"nerd_note":"Chalone's water had to be trucked up the mountain for years; the Chenin Blanc vines planted in 1919 are among the oldest in California.","rslug":"central-coast"},"us-champlain-valley-of-new-york":{"name":"Champlain Valley of New York","cat":"AVA","region":"New York","subregion":"New York","created":"2016","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Marquette, Frontenac, La Crescent","summary":"A cold AVA (2016) of about 500 square miles in Clinton and Essex counties on the western shore of Lake Champlain. TTB accepted that its short season suits cold-hardy hybrids (Frontenac, La Crescent, Marquette) rather than vinifera. At approval it had six wineries. A Champlain Valley of Vermont AVA across the lake was proposed in 2026.","rslug":"new-york"},"us-chehalem-mountains":{"name":"Chehalem Mountains","cat":"AVA","region":"Willamette Valley","subregion":"Willamette Valley","created":"2006","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Pinot Noir, Chardonnay, Pinot Gris, Pinot Blanc","summary":"The highest landform in the northern Willamette Valley (2006), a 30 km ridge southwest of Portland rising to about 500 m at Bald Peak. It has all three Willamette soil types: volcanic Jory, marine sediments and wind-blown Laurelwood loess. Adelsheim (1971) and Ponzi were early settlers; the AVA contains Ribbon Ridge and the Laurelwood District.","producers":["Adelsheim","Ponzi","Bergström","Rex Hill","Penner-Ash (nearby)"],"contains":["Ribbon Ridge","Laurelwood District"],"rslug":"willamette-valley"},"us-chiles-valley":{"name":"Chiles Valley","cat":"AVA","region":"Napa Valley","subregion":"Napa Valley","created":"1999","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, Zinfandel, Merlot, Sauvignon Blanc","summary":"A small, high side valley (1999) tucked into the Vaca Range east of the main Napa Valley, at about 250 to 370 m. Cold air drains into it at night, so it is cooler than the valley floor, with a long season and a later harvest. Cabernet Sauvignon and Zinfandel are firm and fresh.\n\nThe valley takes its name from Joseph Ballinger Chiles, an early overland pioneer who received the Catacula land grant in 1844. Volker Eisele, who farmed here organically from the 1970s, was one of the leaders who defended Napa's Agricultural Preserve.","terroir":"Alluvial valley floor and surrounding slopes, cooler nights from cold-air pooling.","producers":["Brown Estate","Volker Eisele Family Estate","Kuleto Estate","RustRidge"],"nerd_note":"Brown Estate, known for Zinfandel, is widely cited as the first Black-owned estate winery in Napa Valley.","rslug":"napa-valley"},"us-cienega-valley":{"name":"Cienega Valley","cat":"AVA","region":"Central Coast","subregion":"San Benito","created":"1982","styles":["red"],"grapes":"Zinfandel, Pinot Noir, Cabernet Pfeffer, Négrette","summary":"Historic valley (1982) in San Benito County along the San Andreas fault, where vines were planted in the 1850s. William Palmtag and later Almaden made wine here; DeRose Vineyards farms old, own-rooted Zinfandel and Négrette. Limestone and granite soils.","producers":["DeRose","Eden Rift"],"nerd_note":"The vineyard sits directly on the San Andreas fault; the creep of the fault slowly bends rows and has cracked the old winery walls.","contains":["Lime Kiln Valley"],"rslug":"central-coast"},"us-clarksburg":{"name":"Clarksburg","cat":"AVA","region":"Sacramento Valley","subregion":"Sacramento-San Joaquin Delta","created":"1984","styles":["white","red"],"grapes":"Chenin Blanc, Petite Sirah, Chardonnay, Albariño","summary":"Delta country (1984, expanded 2022) south of Sacramento, where vineyards grow on reclaimed river islands behind levees. Cool afternoon breezes through the Carquinez Strait make it cooler than Lodi. Chenin Blanc is the historic speciality, plus Petite Sirah and Albariño; much fruit is sold to wineries elsewhere.","producers":["Bogle","Dry Creek Vineyard (Clarksburg Chenin)","Heringer"],"contains":["Merritt Island"],"rslug":"sacramento-valley"},"us-clear-lake":{"name":"Clear Lake","cat":"AVA","region":"Lake County","subregion":"North Coast","created":"1984","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Sauvignon Blanc, Cabernet Sauvignon, Petite Sirah, Zinfandel, Merlot","summary":"The large umbrella AVA (1984) around Clear Lake, covering most of Lake County's vineyards. The lake moderates temperatures, while altitudes of 400 m and more give cool nights. Sauvignon Blanc and Cabernet Sauvignon lead, with Petite Sirah and Zinfandel from older plantings.","producers":["Steele","Shannon Ridge","Brassfield","Six Sigma"],"contains":["Red Hills Lake County","Big Valley District-Lake County","Kelsey Bench-Lake County","High Valley","Upper Lake Valley"],"rslug":"lake-county"},"us-clements-hills":{"name":"Clements Hills","cat":"AVA","region":"Lodi","subregion":"Lodi","created":"2006","styles":["red"],"grapes":"Zinfandel, Cabernet Sauvignon, Tempranillo, Albariño","summary":"Rolling hills (2006) in the eastern part of Lodi, with red, cobbly clay-loam soils on old river terraces; slightly warmer than the west. Cabernet Sauvignon, Zinfandel and Iberian varieties.","producers":["Bokisch","Klinker Brick"],"rslug":"lodi"},"us-cole-ranch":{"name":"Cole Ranch","cat":"AVA","region":"Mendocino County","subregion":"Mendocino","created":"1983","styles":["white","red"],"grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot, Riesling, Chardonnay","summary":"Once the smallest AVA in the United States (1983) at about 150 to 190 acres, a single ranch in a narrow valley in the hills between Ukiah and Boonville, owned by the Cole family. It grows Riesling, Cabernet Sauvignon and Merlot. Hawaii's Ulupalakua (2021) has since taken the 'smallest AVA' title.","nerd_note":"Cole Ranch is essentially a one-owner AVA, which is legal as long as the petition proves the area is geographically distinct.","rslug":"mendocino-county"},"us-columbia-gorge":{"name":"Columbia Gorge","cat":"AVA","region":"Columbia Gorge","subregion":"Oregon and Washington","created":"2004","styles":["red","white","sparkling"],"grapes":"Pinot Noir, Chardonnay, Gewürztraminer, Riesling, Syrah, Zinfandel","summary":"A dramatic AVA (2004) straddling the Columbia River where it cuts through the Cascade Range, on both the Oregon (Hood River, Wasco) and Washington (Klickitat, Skamania) sides. In about 65 km, rainfall falls from more than 90 cm a year in the west to under 25 cm in the east, so the gorge grows everything from cool-climate Pinot Noir, Chardonnay and Gewürztraminer near Hood River to Zinfandel, Syrah and Cabernet near The Dalles. Old Zinfandel planted by Italian immigrants survives at The Pines 1852 vineyard.","producers":["Analemma","Syncline","Phelps Creek","Cathedral Ridge","The Pines 1852"],"rslug":"columbia-gorge"},"us-columbia-hills":{"name":"Columbia Hills","cat":"AVA","region":"Columbia Valley","subregion":"Columbia Valley","created":"2026","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Syrah, Cabernet Sauvignon, Grenache, Viognier","summary":"Washington's 22nd AVA (effective September 2026), about 29,387 acres in Klickitat County along the north bank of the Columbia River opposite The Dalles and around Maryhill. Eighty percent of the land lies within a mile of the river, which acts as a heat reservoir; soils are coarse basalt colluvium and wind-blown sand and gravel bars left by the ice-age floods, much coarser than the loess common elsewhere. At approval there were about 338 acres of vines owned by 19 landowners and four wineries.\n\nThe name comes from the Columbia Hills Natural Area Preserve and Columbia Hills Historical State Park, home to the petroglyph 'She Who Watches'.","rslug":"columbia-valley"},"us-columbia-valley":{"name":"Columbia Valley","cat":"AVA","region":"Columbia Valley","subregion":"Washington and Oregon","created":"1984","styles":["red","white","sweet"],"grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot, Syrah, Chardonnay, Riesling, Cabernet Franc","summary":"Washington's giant umbrella AVA (1984) of about 11.5 million acres covering most of eastern Washington and a strip of northern Oregon. Low rainfall, long sunny days, cold nights and Missoula flood soils give wines of ripe fruit and fresh acidity. Most Washington wine, including the biggest brands, carries this name. Inside it are 19 sub-AVAs.\n\nThe scale is hard to grasp: Columbia Valley covers about a quarter of Washington State, yet vines occupy only a small fraction, clustered on south-facing slopes and benches near the rivers where cold air drains away in winter. Growing degree days range from cool (Ancient Lakes) to very warm (Wahluke Slope, Red Mountain). Most vineyards are own-rooted on sandy, phylloxera-hostile soils, which lets growers replant quickly after freezes. The AVA also crosses into Oregon around Milton-Freewater and the Columbia Gorge, so some Oregon wineries use the name.","producers":["Chateau Ste. Michelle","Columbia Crest","Quilceda Creek (sources)","L'Ecole No 41","Andrew Will","Gramercy Cellars"],"contains":["Yakima Valley","Walla Walla Valley","Horse Heaven Hills","Wahluke Slope","Ancient Lakes of Columbia Valley","Lake Chelan","Naches Heights","Royal Slope","White Bluffs","Rocky Reach","The Burn of Columbia Valley","Beverly, Washington","Columbia Hills"],"rslug":"columbia-valley"},"us-comptche":{"name":"Comptche","cat":"AVA","region":"Mendocino County","subregion":"Mendocino County coast","created":"2024","styles":["red","white","sparkling"],"grapes":"Pinot Noir, Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc","summary":"A small AVA (2024) of about 1,422 acres around the hamlet of Comptche, a few kilometres inland from Mendocino town. Unusually, TTB excluded it from the North Coast AVA because its foggy, cold, coastal climate and soils differ so sharply from the larger area. It grows Pinot Noir, Chardonnay and sparkling-wine grapes on a handful of small vineyards.","nerd_note":"Comptche is one of very few AVAs that is surrounded by, but not part of, a larger AVA.","rslug":"mendocino-county"},"us-contra-costa":{"name":"Contra Costa","cat":"AVA","region":"San Francisco Bay","subregion":"San Francisco Bay","created":"2024","styles":["red"],"grapes":"Zinfandel, Mourvèdre, Carignan, Petite Sirah","summary":"A new AVA (2024) of about 167,100 acres in eastern Contra Costa County, around Oakley, Brentwood and Antioch, with at least 60 vineyards on about 1,700 acres. Its treasure is ungrafted, century-old Zinfandel, Mourvèdre (Mataro), Carignan and Petite Sirah on deep, sandy soils where phylloxera never took hold, much of it bought by producers such as Bedrock, Cline, Turley and Ridge.\n\nCreating it required TTB to enlarge both San Francisco Bay and Central Coast so that Contra Costa sits fully inside them.","producers":["Cline (origins)","Bedrock (Evangelho)","Turley (sourcing)","Ridge (sourcing)"],"nerd_note":"The Evangelho Vineyard in Antioch, planted in the 1890s, grows own-rooted vines in deep sand dunes left by an ancient river delta.","rslug":"san-francisco-bay"},"us-coombsville":{"name":"Coombsville","cat":"AVA","region":"Napa Valley","subregion":"Napa Valley","created":"2011","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot, Cabernet Franc, Syrah, Chardonnay, Pinot Noir","summary":"Coombsville (2011) is a horseshoe of hills east of the city of Napa, often described as the eroded rim of an ancient volcanic caldera. Close to the bay, it is one of Napa's coolest Cabernet areas, with fog and afternoon wind; harvest comes later and the wines show fresh acidity, red and black fruit and less overt alcohol.\n\nGrowers such as Tom Farella planted here in the 1970s, but the area was a secret source for valley wineries until the AVA gave it its own name. Syrah, Chardonnay and Pinot Noir also grow well.","terroir":"Volcanic tuff and ash on the slopes, alluvial fans below; cool marine influence from San Pablo Bay.","producers":["Farella","Favia","Palmaz","Ancien","Arietta"],"nerd_note":"The name honours Nathan Coombs, who laid out the town of Napa in 1847.","rslug":"napa-valley"},"us-cosumnes-river":{"name":"Cosumnes River","cat":"AVA","region":"Lodi","subregion":"Lodi","created":"2006","styles":["white","red"],"grapes":"Chardonnay, Pinot Grigio, Zinfandel","summary":"The northwestern Lodi sub-AVA (2006) on the Cosumnes River, close to the Delta and among the coolest, with heavy clay and alluvial soils; Pinot Grigio, Chardonnay and Zinfandel.","rslug":"lodi"},"us-covelo":{"name":"Covelo","cat":"AVA","region":"Mendocino County","subregion":"Mendocino","created":"2006","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, Zinfandel, Chardonnay","summary":"A remote, high valley (2006) of about 38,000 acres in northeastern Mendocino County, at about 425 m, surrounded by mountains and the Round Valley Indian Reservation. Cold winters and warm summers; plantings are very small.","rslug":"mendocino-county"},"us-crest-of-the-blue-ridge-henderson-county":{"name":"Crest of the Blue Ridge Henderson County","cat":"AVA","region":"Southeast","subregion":"North Carolina","created":"2019","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Cabernet Franc, Chardonnay, Petit Manseng, Riesling","summary":"A mountain AVA (2019) of about 215 square miles in Henderson County near Hendersonville, defined by elevation (most land at 640 to 960 m) and a cooler climate. At approval it had 14 vineyards on about 70 acres and two bonded wineries.","producers":["Burntshirt","Saint Paul Mountain"],"rslug":"southeast"},"us-creston-district":{"name":"Creston District","cat":"AVA","region":"Paso Robles","subregion":"Paso Robles","created":"2014","styles":["red"],"grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, Zinfandel, Syrah","summary":"A district (2014) in the southeast of Paso Robles around the hamlet of Creston, in foothills of the La Panza Range at 300 to 600 m. Mixed alluvial and granitic soils, low rainfall and a warm, dry climate grow Cabernet Sauvignon, Zinfandel and Syrah.","rslug":"paso-robles"},"us-crystal-springs-of-napa-valley":{"name":"Crystal Springs of Napa Valley","cat":"AVA","region":"Napa Valley","subregion":"Napa Valley","created":"2024","styles":["red"],"grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot, Cabernet Franc","summary":"Napa's newest sub-AVA (effective November 2024) covers about 4,000 acres of the western face of the Vaca Range between St. Helena and Calistoga. TTB describes it as an all-hillside region: every slope is between 15 and 40 percent, mostly facing west to southwest, and the boundary runs from the 400-foot contour (where the foothills meet the frost-prone valley floor) up to the 1,400-foot contour where Howell Mountain begins.\n\nAbout 30 vineyards cover some 230 acres. Afternoon sun and warm air draining off the slopes make it a warm Cabernet Sauvignon site with less frost risk than the floor below. The name comes from Crystal Springs Road and the springs that gave the road its name.","terroir":"All hillside, 120 to 430 m, volcanic soils on west and southwest slopes; less frost than the valley floor and less elevation cooling than Howell Mountain above.","nerd_note":"Creating it also moved Calistoga's boundary, after a grower showed that one 17-acre vineyard on North Fork Crystal Springs Road would otherwise have been split between two AVAs.","rslug":"napa-valley"},"us-cucamonga-valley":{"name":"Cucamonga Valley","cat":"AVA","region":"Southern California","subregion":"Southern California","created":"1995","styles":["red","fortified"],"grapes":"Zinfandel, Mission, Grenache, Mourvèdre, Palomino","summary":"A historic AVA (1995) in San Bernardino and Riverside counties, east of Los Angeles. In 1900 Secondo Guasti founded the Italian Vineyard Company here and planted about 5,000 acres, then billed as the largest vineyard in the world; by the 1920s the valley had more than 20,000 acres of vines. Warehouses, freeways and Ontario airport have replaced most of them, but scattered old, dry-farmed Zinfandel and Mission vines survive, some more than a century old, and Galleano Winery (1927) still makes wine from them.","producers":["Galleano","Joseph Filippi","Bedrock (Cucamonga Zinfandel)"],"rslug":"southern-california"},"us-cumberland-valley":{"name":"Cumberland Valley","cat":"AVA","region":"Mid-Atlantic","subregion":"Pennsylvania and Maryland","created":"1985","styles":["white","red"],"grapes":"Chardonnay, Vidal, Cabernet Franc","summary":"An AVA (1985) in the limestone Cumberland Valley of south-central Pennsylvania and western Maryland, part of the Great Appalachian Valley that runs from New York to Alabama. The valley's sheltered floor and well-drained soils grow hybrids and vinifera for a handful of small wineries.","rslug":"mid-atlantic"},"us-dahlonega-plateau":{"name":"Dahlonega Plateau","cat":"AVA","region":"Southeast","subregion":"Georgia","created":"2018","styles":["red","white","sparkling"],"grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, Cabernet Franc, Petit Manseng, Chardonnay","summary":"Georgia's first wholly in-state AVA (2018), about 133 square miles on a plateau in Lumpkin and White counties, the site of the 1829 Georgia gold rush. Elevation and slope give better air drainage than the surrounding land. At approval it had seven wineries and about 110 acres; Wolf Mountain, Frogtown and Three Sisters grow Cabernet, Petit Manseng and sparkling-wine grapes.","producers":["Wolf Mountain","Frogtown Cellars","Three Sisters","Montaluce"],"rslug":"southeast"},"us-diablo-grande":{"name":"Diablo Grande","cat":"AVA","region":"Central Valley","subregion":"Central Valley","created":"1998","styles":["red"],"grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, Syrah","summary":"A single-development AVA (1998) in the Diablo Range foothills west of Patterson, Stanislaus County, created alongside a planned resort and golf community. It is cooler than the San Joaquin Valley floor below, but its vineyards are small and few wines use the name.","rslug":"central-valley"},"us-diamond-mountain-district":{"name":"Diamond Mountain District","cat":"AVA","region":"Napa Valley","subregion":"Napa Valley","created":"2001","styles":["red","sparkling"],"grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, Cabernet Franc, Merlot, Petit Verdot","summary":"A steep, small mountain AVA (2001) on the Mayacamas slopes just southwest of Calistoga, rising from about 120 to 670 m. The soils are volcanic ash and weathered lava, light and fast-draining, and the vines sit above much of the valley's heat. Cabernet Sauvignon here is dense, mineral and tannic in youth, and it is among the longest-lived in Napa.\n\nDiamond Creek defined the place. Al Brounstein planted it in 1968 and from 1972 bottled separate Cabernets from neighbouring blocks with different soils (Volcanic Hill, Red Rock Terrace, Gravelly Meadow), an early and very Burgundian statement that site matters in California. Schramsberg's caves are dug into the same mountainside.","terroir":"Volcanic ash, tuff and red lava soils on steep, mostly northeast-facing slopes; cooler and less foggy than the valley floor.","producers":["Diamond Creek","Schramsberg","Von Strasser","Reverie"],"nerd_note":"Brounstein reportedly brought his first Bordeaux cuttings in by way of Mexico, and Diamond Creek's block-by-block bottlings were among the first vineyard-parcel Cabernets in the United States.","rslug":"napa-valley"},"us-dos-rios":{"name":"Dos Rios","cat":"AVA","region":"Mendocino County","subregion":"Mendocino","created":"2005","styles":["red"],"grapes":"Zinfandel, Syrah, Cabernet Sauvignon","summary":"A tiny, remote AVA (2005) of about 15,500 acres where the Middle Fork and main stem of the Eel River meet ('two rivers'), in rugged northern Mendocino County. It has only a few acres of vines.","rslug":"mendocino-county"},"us-dry-creek-valley":{"name":"Dry Creek Valley","cat":"AVA","region":"Sonoma County","subregion":"Northern Sonoma","created":"1983","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Zinfandel, Sauvignon Blanc, Cabernet Sauvignon, Petite Sirah, Syrah, Grenache","summary":"A narrow valley (1983) running 25 km northwest of Healdsburg, now dammed at its head by Lake Sonoma. Italian immigrant families planted Zinfandel and field blends here in the late 19th century, and many of those head-trained, dry-farmed vines survive on the gravelly benches. Dry Creek Zinfandel is the archetype: brambly blackberry, black pepper and spice, ripe but rarely jammy.\n\nThe valley floor's deep alluvial loam suits Sauvignon Blanc; David Stare's Dry Creek Vineyard (1972) was the first new winery here after Prohibition and championed the grape. Rhône varieties and Cabernet are increasingly important.","terroir":"Gravelly, iron-rich red benchland on both sides of a deep alluvial valley floor; warm days, with fog reaching the southern end.","producers":["Ridge (Lytton Springs)","Quivira","Preston","Dry Creek Vineyard","Pedroncelli","Unti","A. Rafanelli","Nalle"],"nerd_note":"Ridge labels its Lytton Springs Zinfandel blend as Dry Creek Valley; the old vines there were planted around 1900 and include Petite Sirah, Carignan and Mataro.","rslug":"sonoma-county"},"us-dundee-hills":{"name":"Dundee Hills","cat":"AVA","region":"Willamette Valley","subregion":"Willamette Valley","created":"2004","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Pinot Noir, Chardonnay, Pinot Gris","summary":"The birthplace of Oregon Pinot Noir and still its most famous address (2004). The Dundee Hills are a compact block of hills above the town of Dundee, and the AVA boundary follows the 200-foot contour, roughly where red volcanic Jory soil begins. Jory is deep, iron-rich, red clay loam weathered from Columbia River basalt that flowed here about 15 million years ago; it drains well and holds enough water for dry farming.\n\nDavid and Diana Lett planted the first Pinot Noir in the Willamette Valley at their Eyrie Vineyards site in the Dundee Hills in 1965 to 1966, and Erath, Sokol Blosser, Domaine Drouhin Oregon (1988), Archery Summit, Domaine Serene and Lingua Franca followed. Dundee Hills Pinot Noir is classically red-fruited and perfumed, with silky tannin and a hint of earth and spice.","terroir":"Volcanic Jory soils on southeast-facing slopes above 200 feet; protected from the worst Pacific winds by the Coast Range.","producers":["The Eyrie Vineyards","Domaine Drouhin Oregon","Sokol Blosser","Erath","Archery Summit","Domaine Serene","Lingua Franca","Maison Joseph Drouhin","Winderlea"],"nerd_note":"Jory soil is Oregon's official state soil, named after Jory Hill near Salem.","rslug":"willamette-valley"},"us-dunnigan-hills":{"name":"Dunnigan Hills","cat":"AVA","region":"Sacramento Valley","subregion":"Yolo County","created":"1993","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Syrah, Viognier, Chardonnay, Cabernet Sauvignon","summary":"Rolling hills (1993) in northern Yolo County beside Interstate 5, created at the request of R.H. Phillips, which planted large vineyards here in the 1980s. Warm days and Delta breezes; Syrah, Viognier and Chardonnay.","producers":["R.H. Phillips (EXP)"],"rslug":"sacramento-valley"},"us-eagle-foothills":{"name":"Eagle Foothills","cat":"AVA","region":"Idaho","subregion":"Snake River Valley","created":"2015","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Syrah, Riesling, Cabernet Sauvignon, Pinot Gris","summary":"A sub-AVA (2015) of about 49,800 acres in the foothills north of Eagle, west of Boise, slightly higher and cooler than the surrounding Snake River Valley, with nine vineyards (67 acres) at approval. Bitner Vineyards and 3 Horse Ranch are known producers.","producers":["3 Horse Ranch","Bitner"],"rslug":"idaho"},"us-eagle-peak-mendocino-county":{"name":"Eagle Peak Mendocino County","cat":"AVA","region":"Mendocino County","subregion":"Mendocino","created":"2014","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Pinot Noir, Syrah, Merlot","summary":"A mountainous AVA (2014) of about 26,260 acres northwest of Redwood Valley, with about 120 acres in 16 vineyards at the time of approval. The petition cited a marine-influenced climate, strong breezes, steep slopes and shallow soils with little water-holding capacity, which keep yields low for Pinot Noir and Rhône varieties.","rslug":"mendocino-county"},"us-eastern-connecticut-highlands":{"name":"Eastern Connecticut Highlands","cat":"AVA","region":"New England","subregion":"Connecticut","created":"2019","styles":["white","red"],"grapes":"Seyval Blanc, Cayuga White, Chardonnay, St. Croix","summary":"An AVA (2019) of about 1,250 square miles in the hills of eastern Connecticut, with 16 vineyards (about 115 acres) and six wineries at approval.","rslug":"new-england"},"us-edna-valley":{"name":"Edna Valley","cat":"AVA","region":"San Luis Obispo County","subregion":"San Luis Obispo Coast","created":"1982","styles":["white","red"],"grapes":"Chardonnay, Pinot Noir, Syrah, Albariño","summary":"A cool, east-west valley (1982) just south of San Luis Obispo city, open to the Pacific through Morro Bay so that fog and wind flow straight in. It has one of the longest growing seasons in California, ideal for Chardonnay with crisp acidity and Pinot Noir. Soils include volcanic plugs (the Nine Sisters) and marine sediments.\n\nParagon Vineyard, planted by the Niven family from 1973, was the valley's pioneer and still supplies many wineries; the Nivens later founded Baileyana and Tangent, and Edna Valley Vineyard was a joint venture with Chalone. Because the valley points straight at the Pacific through Morro Bay, the afternoon breeze arrives early and the growing season can stretch from February budbreak to November harvest, giving Chardonnay its hallmark combination of ripeness and acidity. Since 2022 Edna Valley has sat inside the San Luis Obispo Coast AVA.","producers":["Edna Valley Vineyard","Chamisal","Tolosa","Alban (Rhône pioneer)"],"nerd_note":"John Alban planted Viognier, Roussanne and Grenache here around 1990; Alban Vineyards bills itself as the first American winery founded exclusively for Rhône varieties.","rslug":"san-luis-obispo-county"},"us-el-dorado":{"name":"El Dorado","cat":"AVA","region":"Sierra Foothills","subregion":"Sierra Foothills","created":"1983","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Zinfandel, Syrah, Barbera, Grenache, Cabernet Franc","summary":"A high AVA (1983) in El Dorado County, near where gold was found at Coloma in 1848, with vineyards from about 365 to 1,070 m, among the highest in California. Volcanic and granitic soils and cool nights suit Rhône varieties, Barbera, Zinfandel and even Riesling.","producers":["Lava Cap","Boeger","Madroña","Holly's Hill"],"contains":["Fair Play"],"rslug":"sierra-foothills"},"us-el-pomar-district":{"name":"El Pomar District","cat":"AVA","region":"Paso Robles","subregion":"Paso Robles","created":"2014","styles":["red"],"grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, Zinfandel, Merlot","summary":"A district (2014) southeast of Templeton on high, old alluvial terraces with calcareous patches, where some marine air from the Templeton Gap still reaches. Moderate temperatures suit Zinfandel, Cabernet Sauvignon and Merlot; the name recalls the El Pomar ('the orchard') land grant.","rslug":"paso-robles"},"us-elkton-oregon":{"name":"Elkton Oregon","cat":"AVA","region":"Southern Oregon","subregion":"Umpqua Valley","created":"2013","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Pinot Noir, Riesling, Gewürztraminer, Chardonnay","summary":"The coolest and wettest part of the Umpqua (2013), about 74,900 acres along the Umpqua River near Elkton, only about 50 km from the Pacific, so marine air flows straight up the river. It is cool-climate Pinot Noir, Riesling and Gewürztraminer country, cooler than much of the Willamette.","producers":["Brandborg","Bradley","River's Edge"],"rslug":"southern-oregon"},"us-eola-amity-hills":{"name":"Eola-Amity Hills","cat":"AVA","region":"Willamette Valley","subregion":"Willamette Valley","created":"2006","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Pinot Noir, Chardonnay, Pinot Gris","summary":"A north-south ridge (2006) northwest of Salem, directly in line with the Van Duzer Corridor, so cold ocean wind blows across it most summer afternoons. The wind keeps acidity high and skins thick; the soils are thin volcanic basalt with some marine sediment. Eola-Amity Pinot Noir is dark-fruited, firm and taut, and Chardonnay is among Oregon's best. Bethel Heights (1977) and Cristom were pioneers; Evening Land's Seven Springs Vineyard is famous.","producers":["Bethel Heights","Cristom","Evening Land (Seven Springs)","St. Innocent","Lingua Franca (Eola sourcing)","Walter Scott","Brick House (nearby)"],"rslug":"willamette-valley"},"us-escondido-valley":{"name":"Escondido Valley","cat":"AVA","region":"Texas","subregion":"Texas","created":"1992","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, Chenin Blanc","summary":"A small AVA (1992) in Pecos County, West Texas, where large vineyards were planted on University of Texas land near Fort Stockton in the 1980s, at about 900 m. The desert setting means intense heat and irrigation; the vineyards have long supplied grapes to Texas wineries.","rslug":"texas"},"us-fair-play":{"name":"Fair Play","cat":"AVA","region":"Sierra Foothills","subregion":"El Dorado","created":"2001","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Syrah, Grenache, Tempranillo, Zinfandel, Barbera","summary":"A high sub-AVA (2001, expanded 2015) around the hamlet of Fair Play in southern El Dorado County, at about 600 to 900 m on decomposed granite. Small family wineries make Rhône and Spanish varieties and Zinfandel.","producers":["Skinner","Cedarville","Miraflores","Fitzpatrick"],"rslug":"sierra-foothills"},"us-fennville":{"name":"Fennville","cat":"AVA","region":"Great Lakes","subregion":"Lake Michigan Shore","created":"1981","styles":["white","red"],"grapes":"Riesling, Chardonnay, Pinot Gris, Chambourcin","summary":"One of the first AVAs (1981), a small area near Lake Michigan in Allegan County around Fenn Valley Vineyards, which petitioned for it. Sandy soils and lake moderation.","producers":["Fenn Valley"],"rslug":"great-lakes"},"us-fiddletown":{"name":"Fiddletown","cat":"AVA","region":"Sierra Foothills","subregion":"Sierra Foothills","created":"1983","styles":["red"],"grapes":"Zinfandel","summary":"A small AVA (1983) in Amador County above the Shenandoah Valley, at about 450 to 760 m, with old Zinfandel vineyards such as Eschen, planted around 1910.","producers":["Easton (Eschen Vineyard)","Ridge (historic Fiddletown Zinfandel)"],"rslug":"sierra-foothills"},"us-finger-lakes":{"name":"Finger Lakes","cat":"AVA","region":"Finger Lakes","subregion":"New York","created":"1982","styles":["white","red","sparkling","sweet"],"grapes":"Riesling, Cabernet Franc, Pinot Noir, Chardonnay, Gewürztraminer, Lemberger, Saperavi, Seyval Blanc, Vidal","summary":"One of the great Riesling regions outside Europe (AVA 1982), an area of more than 2 million acres around eleven long, narrow glacial lakes in upstate New York. The deepest lakes (Seneca reaches about 190 m, Cayuga about 130 m) store summer heat and release it in winter, protecting vines on the slopes from the worst cold and delaying spring budbreak until frost danger has passed. Soils are shale, limestone and glacial gravels.\n\nDr. Konstantin Frank proved at Keuka Lake in the late 1950s that vinifera could survive here, and his winery's first vintage came in 1962. Today Riesling is the signature, from bone-dry to ice wine, with tense acidity and slate-and-citrus flavours, followed by Cabernet Franc, Pinot Noir, Lemberger, Gewürztraminer, Grüner Veltliner and Saperavi. The region has more than 100 wineries.","terroir":"Glacial lakes moderate temperatures; slopes of shale, limestone and gravel; cold winters with snow cover.","producers":["Dr. Konstantin Frank","Hermann J. Wiemer","Ravines","Red Newt","Fox Run","Boundary Breaks","Heart & Hands","Bloomer Creek","Kelby James Russell","Nathan Kendall"],"contains":["Seneca Lake","Cayuga Lake"],"rslug":"finger-lakes"},"us-fort-ross-seaview":{"name":"Fort Ross-Seaview","cat":"AVA","region":"Sonoma County","subregion":"West Sonoma Coast","created":"2011","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Pinot Noir, Chardonnay","summary":"A ridgetop AVA (2011) of about 27,500 acres just inland from the ruins of the Russian colony at Fort Ross. The vineyards mostly sit above about 280 m (920 feet), above the marine layer, so they get sun while the valleys below are blanketed in fog, and cold air drains away at night. Yields are low and berries small.\n\nDavid Hirsch planted here in 1980, Walt and Joan Flowers in 1989, and Helen Turley and John Wetlaufer's Marcassin estate followed. The Pinot Noirs combine coastal brightness with surprising concentration.","terroir":"Steep ridges of Franciscan sandstone and shale along the San Andreas fault zone; above the fog line with large diurnal swings.","producers":["Hirsch","Flowers","Marcassin","Fort Ross Vineyard","Wild Hog"],"nerd_note":"The San Andreas fault runs right along the base of the ridges here, and Hirsch Vineyards straddles terrain on both tectonic plates.","rslug":"sonoma-county"},"us-fountaingrove-district":{"name":"Fountaingrove District","cat":"AVA","region":"Sonoma County","subregion":"North Coast","created":"2015","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot, Syrah, Zinfandel, Chardonnay","summary":"A hilly AVA (2015) of about 38,000 acres in the Mayacamas foothills northeast of Santa Rosa, with around 500 acres of vines. It is named for Fountaingrove, the utopian Brotherhood of the New Life founded by Thomas Lake Harris in 1875. Kanaye Nagasawa, a young Japanese samurai who came with Harris, ran its winery and became one of California's leading winemakers before Prohibition.\n\nVolcanic soils and elevation suit Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot and Syrah. The October 2017 Tubbs Fire swept through the district, destroying Fountaingrove's famous 1899 Round Barn and hundreds of homes.","producers":["Paradise Ridge","Pride Mountain (part)","Fisher (part)"],"nerd_note":"Nagasawa (1852 to 1934) is often called California's 'Wine King'; land laws against Japanese ownership meant his estate was lost after his death.","rslug":"sonoma-county"},"us-fredericksburg-in-the-texas-hill-country":{"name":"Fredericksburg in the Texas Hill Country","cat":"AVA","region":"Texas","subregion":"Texas Hill Country","created":"1988","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Tempranillo, Cabernet Sauvignon, Viognier, Mourvèdre","summary":"A small AVA (1988) of about 66,000 acres (roughly 100 square miles) around the German-founded town of Fredericksburg (1846). Sandy, granite-derived soils and the town's tourist trade have made it the epicentre of Hill Country wine; Pedernales Cellars petitioned for it.","producers":["Pedernales","Grape Creek","Becker (nearby)"],"rslug":"texas"},"us-gabilan-mountains":{"name":"Gabilan Mountains","cat":"AVA","region":"Central Coast","subregion":"Central Coast","created":"2022","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Pinot Noir, Chardonnay, Syrah","summary":"A large mountain AVA (2022) of about 98,000 acres along the Gabilan (Gavilan) Range between the Salinas Valley and San Benito County, defined by elevation, a cooler climate and granitic and limestone soils. It encloses Chalone and Mt. Harlan, but TTB kept those older AVAs as they are.","contains":["Chalone","Mt. Harlan"],"rslug":"central-coast"},"us-goose-gap":{"name":"Goose Gap","cat":"AVA","region":"Yakima Valley","subregion":"Yakima Valley","created":"2021","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot, Chardonnay","summary":"A small AVA (2021) of about 8,129 acres in the hills south of Red Mountain near Richland. At approval it had two vineyards covering more than 1,800 acres and one winery; the petition stressed geology and soils on the hill's folded basalt.","rslug":"yakima-valley"},"us-grand-river-valley":{"name":"Grand River Valley","cat":"AVA","region":"Great Lakes","subregion":"Ohio","created":"1983","styles":["white","red","sweet"],"grapes":"Riesling, Chardonnay, Pinot Gris, Cabernet Franc, Vidal","summary":"An AVA (1983) in northeast Ohio along the Grand River near Lake Erie, where lake moderation and gravelly soils allow vinifera. Debonné Vineyards and Ferrante are leading producers of Riesling and ice wine.","producers":["Debonné","Ferrante","Laurello","M Cellars"],"rslug":"great-lakes"},"us-grand-valley":{"name":"Grand Valley","cat":"AVA","region":"Southwest and Mountain West","subregion":"Colorado","created":"1991","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Riesling, Cabernet Franc, Syrah, Merlot, Cabernet Sauvignon","summary":"Colorado's main wine area (1991), along the Colorado River around Palisade and Grand Junction at about 1,400 m. Cold air drains down the river canyon at night and warm air off the Book Cliffs reduces frost, making it fruit country (Palisade peaches) as well as wine country. Riesling, Cabernet Franc, Syrah and Merlot.","producers":["Colterris","Carlson","Plum Creek","Colorado Cellars","Sauvage Spectrum"],"rslug":"southwest-and-mountain-west"},"us-green-valley-of-russian-river-valley":{"name":"Green Valley of Russian River Valley","cat":"AVA","region":"Sonoma County","subregion":"Russian River Valley","created":"1983","styles":["sparkling","white","red"],"grapes":"Pinot Noir, Chardonnay","summary":"A small, foggy AVA (1983) in the southwestern corner of the Russian River Valley around Forestville, Graton and Occidental, one of the coolest parts of the larger valley. The soil is almost all Goldridge sandy loam. It is classic ground for sparkling wine and taut Chardonnay and Pinot Noir.\n\nIron Horse, founded by Barry and Audrey Sterling in 1976, made sparkling wines that were poured at the Reagan-Gorbachev summits from 1985 and at many White House dinners since.","producers":["Iron Horse","Marimar Estate","Dutton-Goldfield","Hartford Family"],"nerd_note":"Until 2007 the AVA was called 'Sonoma County Green Valley'; TTB renamed it to avoid confusion with Solano County Green Valley.","rslug":"sonoma-county"},"us-guenoc-valley":{"name":"Guenoc Valley","cat":"AVA","region":"Lake County","subregion":"North Coast","created":"1981","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Petite Sirah, Cabernet Sauvignon, Sauvignon Blanc","summary":"An early single-proprietor AVA (1981) in southern Lake County, on the old Guenoc Ranch. The English actress Lillie Langtry, a friend of the future Edward VII, bought the ranch in 1888 and hired a Bordeaux winemaker; her portrait was later used on the labels of Langtry Estate. Warm and dry, it grows Petite Sirah, Cabernet Sauvignon and Sauvignon Blanc.","producers":["Langtry Farms"],"rslug":"lake-county"},"us-hames-valley":{"name":"Hames Valley","cat":"AVA","region":"Central Coast","subregion":"Monterey","created":"1994","styles":["red"],"grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, Syrah, Merlot","summary":"The southernmost Monterey AVA (1994), a warm inland valley near the San Luis Obispo County line with wide day-night temperature swings. Shallow, rocky soils grow Bordeaux and Rhône varieties, mostly sold to wineries elsewhere in the Central Coast.","rslug":"central-coast"},"us-happy-canyon-of-santa-barbara":{"name":"Happy Canyon of Santa Barbara","cat":"AVA","region":"Santa Barbara County","subregion":"Santa Ynez Valley","created":"2009","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, Cabernet Franc, Sauvignon Blanc, Merlot","summary":"The eastern and warmest part of the Santa Ynez Valley (2009), a 23,900-acre canyon area with serpentine and rocky soils. Bordeaux varieties ripen here, and Star Lane, Grassini and Happy Canyon Vineyard make Cabernet Sauvignon, Cabernet Franc and Sauvignon Blanc. The name supposedly dates from Prohibition, when moonshine was made in the canyon.","producers":["Star Lane","Grassini","Happy Canyon Vineyard","Vogelzang"],"rslug":"santa-barbara-county"},"us-haw-river-valley":{"name":"Haw River Valley","cat":"AVA","region":"Southeast","subregion":"North Carolina","created":"2009","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Cabernet Franc, Chambourcin, Merlot, Viognier","summary":"A Piedmont AVA (2009) of about 868 square miles along the Haw River in central North Carolina, named for the Sissipahaw people. At approval it had about 60 acres and six wineries.","producers":["Iron Gate","Grove Winery"],"rslug":"southeast"},"us-hermann":{"name":"Hermann","cat":"AVA","region":"Midwest","subregion":"Missouri","created":"1983","styles":["red","white","fortified"],"grapes":"Norton, Chambourcin, Vignoles, Catawba, Vidal","summary":"An AVA (1983) of about 51,000 acres (80 square miles) along the Missouri River around the German-founded town of Hermann (1837). By the 1880s Stone Hill Winery was one of the largest in the country, winning medals at world fairs, and the town's Norton, known locally as Cynthiana, became famous; Missouri adopted Norton as its official state grape. Stone Hill and Hermannhof restored the 19th-century cellars after Prohibition.","producers":["Stone Hill","Hermannhof","Adam Puchta","Dierberg Star Lane (Hermann)"],"nerd_note":"Adam Puchta Winery (1855) claims to be the oldest continuously family-owned winery in the US.","rslug":"midwest"},"us-high-valley":{"name":"High Valley","cat":"AVA","region":"Lake County","subregion":"Clear Lake","created":"2005","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Sauvignon Blanc, Cabernet Sauvignon, Syrah","summary":"A narrow, east-west valley (2005) in northeastern Lake County above Clear Lake, at about 500 to 920 m. Cool air drains down from the surrounding ridges, giving a long season for Sauvignon Blanc, Cabernet and Syrah. Its boundary was trimmed in 2023 when the neighbouring Long Valley-Lake County AVA was created.","producers":["Brassfield"],"rslug":"lake-county"},"us-horse-heaven-hills":{"name":"Horse Heaven Hills","cat":"AVA","region":"Columbia Valley","subregion":"Columbia Valley","created":"2005","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot, Syrah, Chardonnay, Riesling","summary":"A big AVA (2005) of about 570,000 acres on the south-facing slopes above the Columbia River, south of the Yakima Valley. Steady wind from the river reduces frost and disease. Champoux Vineyard (planted 1972 as Mercer Ranch) has given perfect-score Cabernets to Quilceda Creek and Andrew Will; Columbia Crest is based here. At approval there were 6,040 acres of vines.","producers":["Columbia Crest","Champoux (grower)","Andrew Will","Mercer Estates","Double Canyon"],"rslug":"columbia-valley"},"us-howell-mountain":{"name":"Howell Mountain","cat":"AVA","region":"Napa Valley","subregion":"Napa Valley","created":"1983","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot, Cabernet Franc, Zinfandel, Petite Sirah, Viognier","summary":"The first sub-AVA lying wholly inside Napa Valley, approved in late 1983 and effective in January 1984, and still one of the most distinctive. It is a high plateau in the Vaca Range above St. Helena, and the boundary follows the 1,400-foot (about 430 m) contour, roughly the top of the summer fog layer. Vines above the fog get longer sun and cooler nights, in thin red volcanic soils and white volcanic ash (tufa).\n\nThe result is mountain Cabernet at its most monumental: deep colour, blue and black fruit, iron and graphite, and tannin that can take a decade to unwind. Randy Dunn's Howell Mountain Cabernet set the style from 1979. The stone wineries of Ladera (built 1886 for Brun and Chaix) and La Jota recall a 19th-century reputation for Zinfandel.","terroir":"Above 430 m: red volcanic soils and white tufa ash, low fertility, sunny days above the fog and cool nights.","producers":["Dunn Vineyards","La Jota","Ladera","O'Shaughnessy","Outpost","Cade","Robert Craig"],"nerd_note":"Howell Mountain was one of the first AVAs anywhere defined by elevation; the 2024 Crystal Springs of Napa Valley AVA was drawn to stop exactly at Howell Mountain's 1,400-foot line.","rslug":"napa-valley"},"us-hudson-river-region":{"name":"Hudson River Region","cat":"AVA","region":"New York","subregion":"New York","created":"1982","styles":["white","red","sparkling"],"grapes":"Seyval Blanc, Chardonnay, Cabernet Franc, Baco Noir, Riesling","summary":"The oldest wine region in the United States still making wine (AVA 1982), along the Hudson valley north of New York City. French Huguenots planted vines in Ulster County in the 1670s, Croton Point had a commercial vineyard by 1827, and Brotherhood Winery has operated at Washingtonville since its first commercial vintage in 1839. The region pioneered hybrids after Prohibition; Benmarl, Millbrook and Whitecliff grow Chardonnay, Cabernet Franc and Seyval Blanc.","producers":["Brotherhood","Millbrook","Benmarl","Whitecliff","Fjord"],"nerd_note":"Benmarl's vineyard at Marlboro is often cited as one of the oldest continuously planted vineyards in the US, dating to the 1770s.","rslug":"new-york"},"us-indiana-uplands":{"name":"Indiana Uplands","cat":"AVA","region":"Midwest","subregion":"Indiana","created":"2013","styles":["white","red","sweet"],"grapes":"Traminette, Chambourcin, Vidal, Cabernet Franc","summary":"An AVA (2013) of about 4,600 square miles in the hills of southern Indiana, carved out of the Ohio River Valley AVA. At approval it had 19 vineyards (about 200 acres) and 17 wineries, including Oliver Winery, the state's largest, and Huber's Orchard. Limestone karst geology; Traminette, Chambourcin and Vidal.","producers":["Oliver Winery","Huber's","Turtle Run"],"rslug":"midwest"},"us-inwood-valley":{"name":"Inwood Valley","cat":"AVA","region":"Far North California","subregion":"Shasta County","created":"2012","styles":["red"],"grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, Syrah, Merlot","summary":"A small valley (2012) of about 28,400 acres in eastern Shasta County, in the foothills of the southern Cascades east of Redding. At approval it had about 60 acres of vines; volcanic soils and cool nights from the Cascades suit Bordeaux and Rhône varieties.","rslug":"far-north-california"},"us-isle-st-george":{"name":"Isle St. George","cat":"AVA","region":"Great Lakes","subregion":"Ohio","created":"1982","styles":["white","red"],"grapes":"Catawba, Riesling, Chardonnay, Pinot Gris","summary":"An island AVA (1982) of about 700 acres covering North Bass Island in Lake Erie, close to the Canadian border. The lake keeps winters mild and autumns long, and vines have grown here since the 1850s. Meier's Wine Cellars, which petitioned for the AVA, farmed much of the island's land in Catawba, Riesling and Chardonnay.","rslug":"great-lakes"},"us-jahant":{"name":"Jahant","cat":"AVA","region":"Lodi","subregion":"Lodi","created":"2006","styles":["red"],"grapes":"Zinfandel, Cabernet Sauvignon, Petite Sirah","summary":"A small Lodi sub-AVA (2006) between the Mokelumne and Cosumnes rivers, close to the Delta, with clay-loam soils and cool afternoon winds. Zinfandel, Cabernet and Petite Sirah.","rslug":"lodi"},"us-kanawha-river-valley":{"name":"Kanawha River Valley","cat":"AVA","region":"Midwest","subregion":"West Virginia","created":"1986","styles":["white","red"],"grapes":"Seyval Blanc, Chambourcin, Vidal","summary":"An AVA (1986) in the Kanawha River valley of West Virginia, around Charleston, within the Ohio River Valley AVA. A small number of wineries grow hybrids.","rslug":"midwest"},"us-kelsey-bench-lake-county":{"name":"Kelsey Bench-Lake County","cat":"AVA","region":"Lake County","subregion":"Clear Lake","created":"2013","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, Petite Sirah, Sauvignon Blanc","summary":"A gently sloping bench (2013) between Big Valley and the Red Hills, at about 410 to 610 m, with about 900 acres of vines at approval. Better air drainage than Big Valley and deep, well-drained alluvial soils suit Cabernet Sauvignon, Petite Sirah and Sauvignon Blanc.","rslug":"lake-county"},"us-knights-valley":{"name":"Knights Valley","cat":"AVA","region":"Sonoma County","subregion":"Northern Sonoma","created":"1983","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, Sauvignon Blanc, Chardonnay","summary":"A small, elevated valley (1983) between Alexander Valley and Calistoga, at the southeast corner of northern Sonoma. Volcanic soils from Mount St. Helena and warm, sunny days give Cabernet Sauvignon of structure and depth, and Sauvignon Blanc. Beringer owns a large share of the vineyards; Peter Michael Winery, founded by British electronics entrepreneur Sir Peter Michael in 1982, is the star estate.","producers":["Peter Michael","Beringer (Knights Valley)","Kendall-Jackson (Knights Valley vineyards)"],"rslug":"sonoma-county"},"us-lake-chelan":{"name":"Lake Chelan","cat":"AVA","region":"Columbia Valley","subregion":"Columbia Valley","created":"2009","styles":["white","red"],"grapes":"Riesling, Pinot Gris, Syrah, Malbec, Pinot Noir","summary":"A glacial-lake AVA (2009) of about 24,000 acres around the southern end of Lake Chelan, about 80 km long and one of the deepest lakes in the United States. The lake moderates temperatures and extends the season. At approval it had 13 bonded wineries and 140 acres; Riesling, Pinot Noir, Syrah and Malbec now grow among apple orchards.","producers":["Tsillan Cellars","Karma","Vin du Lac","Benson"],"rslug":"columbia-valley"},"us-lake-erie":{"name":"Lake Erie","cat":"AVA","region":"Lake Erie","subregion":"New York, Pennsylvania, Ohio","created":"1983","styles":["white","red","sweet"],"grapes":"Concord, Niagara, Riesling, Vidal, Chardonnay, Catawba","summary":"A three-state AVA (1983) along the south shore of Lake Erie in New York, Pennsylvania and Ohio, about 2.2 million acres. The lake keeps autumn mild, and the narrow strip between lake and escarpment is the largest Concord grape-growing area in the world, supplying Welch's juice; Welch's founder Charles Welch moved his operations to Westfield, New York in 1897. Wineries also grow Riesling, Vidal and hybrids. TTB proposed a Conneaut Creek sub-AVA in Ohio in 2024.","producers":["Johnson Estate","Presque Isle","Mazza"],"contains":["Grand River Valley","Isle St. George"],"rslug":"lake-erie"},"us-lake-michigan-shore":{"name":"Lake Michigan Shore","cat":"AVA","region":"Great Lakes","subregion":"Michigan","created":"1983","styles":["white","red","sweet"],"grapes":"Riesling, Chardonnay, Cabernet Franc, Vidal, Concord","summary":"An AVA (1983) in southwestern Michigan near Lake Michigan, where lake winds protect vines from cold. It is Michigan's most productive area, with St. Julian (1921) and Tabor Hill (1968) among its wineries.","producers":["St. Julian","Tabor Hill","Domaine Berrien"],"contains":["Fennville"],"rslug":"great-lakes"},"us-lake-wisconsin":{"name":"Lake Wisconsin","cat":"AVA","region":"Midwest","subregion":"Wisconsin","created":"1994","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Marquette, Maréchal Foch, St. Pepin, La Crescent","summary":"A small AVA (1994) of about 32,000 acres in Columbia and Dane counties, Wisconsin, on the Wisconsin River near Prairie du Sac. Wollersheim Winery occupies the site where the Hungarian Agoston Haraszthy planted vines in the 1840s before he left for California; its hillside caves were dug in 1858.","producers":["Wollersheim"],"rslug":"midwest"},"us-lamorinda":{"name":"Lamorinda","cat":"AVA","region":"San Francisco Bay","subregion":"San Francisco Bay","created":"2016","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot, Pinot Noir, Chardonnay","summary":"A small, hilly AVA (2016) in western Contra Costa County named for the towns of Lafayette, Moraga and Orinda. Many tiny vineyards (46 covering 139 acres at approval) on the slopes of the Berkeley Hills grow Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot, Pinot Noir and Chardonnay, cooled by bay fog.","rslug":"san-francisco-bay"},"us-lancaster-valley":{"name":"Lancaster Valley","cat":"AVA","region":"Mid-Atlantic","subregion":"Pennsylvania","created":"1982","styles":["white","red"],"grapes":"Vidal, Chambourcin, Chardonnay, Cabernet Franc","summary":"An AVA (1982) in Pennsylvania's Lancaster and Chester counties, in Pennsylvania Dutch farm country with deep, fertile limestone soils. Conestoga Vineyards petitioned for it; today wineries such as Waltz Vineyards grow Chardonnay and Bordeaux varieties alongside hybrids.","producers":["Nissley","Waltz Vineyards"],"rslug":"mid-atlantic"},"us-laurelwood-district":{"name":"Laurelwood District","cat":"AVA","region":"Willamette Valley","subregion":"Chehalem Mountains","created":"2020","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Pinot Noir, Chardonnay, Pinot Gris","summary":"A sub-AVA (2020) of about 33,600 acres on the northern and eastern slopes of the Chehalem Mountains, defined by Laurelwood soil: wind-blown loess deposited after the Missoula Floods over basalt. At approval it had about 70 vineyards (975 acres) and 25 wineries. Laurelwood Pinot Noir tends to be lifted and spicy.","producers":["Ponzi","Adelsheim (part)","Alloro"],"rslug":"willamette-valley"},"us-leelanau-peninsula":{"name":"Leelanau Peninsula","cat":"AVA","region":"Great Lakes","subregion":"Michigan","created":"1982","styles":["white","sparkling","red"],"grapes":"Riesling, Pinot Noir, Chardonnay, Pinot Blanc, Gewürztraminer","summary":"A peninsula AVA (1982) between Lake Michigan and Grand Traverse Bay, home to dozens of wineries. Larry Mawby, who petitioned for the AVA, is known for sparkling wine; Riesling, Pinot Noir and Gewürztraminer are the main grapes.","producers":["L. Mawby","Black Star Farms","Blustone","Shady Lane"],"rslug":"great-lakes"},"us-lehigh-valley":{"name":"Lehigh Valley","cat":"AVA","region":"Mid-Atlantic","subregion":"Pennsylvania","created":"2008","styles":["white","red","sparkling"],"grapes":"Riesling, Grüner Veltliner, Chardonnay, Cabernet Franc, Chambourcin","summary":"An AVA (2008) of about a million acres in eastern Pennsylvania along the Lehigh River, named from a Lenape word for river forks. Rolling hills; at approval nine wineries and 220 acres. Galen Glen and Fox & Hound are known for Grüner Veltliner and Riesling.","producers":["Galen Glen","Clover Hill","Pinnacle Ridge","Vynecrest"],"rslug":"mid-atlantic"},"us-leona-valley":{"name":"Leona Valley","cat":"AVA","region":"Southern California","subregion":"Southern California","created":"2008","styles":["red"],"grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, Syrah, Merlot","summary":"A narrow valley (2008) in northern Los Angeles County that follows the San Andreas fault, at about 900 m, with cold winters and hot summers. About 20 acres of vines at approval.","rslug":"southern-california"},"us-lewis-clark-valley":{"name":"Lewis-Clark Valley","cat":"AVA","region":"Idaho","subregion":"Idaho and Washington","created":"2016","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Syrah, Cabernet Sauvignon, Riesling, Merlot","summary":"A low, deep river canyon (2016) of about 307,000 acres where the Snake and Clearwater rivers meet at Lewiston, Idaho and Clarkston, Washington, far lower and warmer than the Snake River Valley to the south. Before Prohibition it was a thriving wine region. At approval it had 16 vineyards (81 acres) and three wineries. TTB realigned the Columbia Valley to avoid overlap.","rslug":"idaho"},"us-lime-kiln-valley":{"name":"Lime Kiln Valley","cat":"AVA","region":"Central Coast","subregion":"Cienega Valley","created":"1982","styles":["red"],"grapes":"Zinfandel, Mourvèdre","summary":"A tiny valley (1982) within Cienega Valley, named for lime kilns that once burned local limestone. Old vineyards grow Zinfandel and Mourvèdre on limestone-rich soils.","producers":["Enz Vineyards (historic)"],"rslug":"central-coast"},"us-linganore":{"name":"Linganore","cat":"AVA","region":"Mid-Atlantic","subregion":"Maryland","created":"1983","styles":["white","red"],"grapes":"Chardonnay, Vidal, Chambourcin","summary":"An AVA (1983) in Frederick and Carroll counties, Maryland, around Linganore Creek in the Piedmont. Linganore Winecellars (Berrywine Plantations), founded in 1976, petitioned for it and makes grape and fruit wines; the area grows hybrids and vinifera.","producers":["Linganore Winecellars"],"rslug":"mid-atlantic"},"us-livermore-valley":{"name":"Livermore Valley","cat":"AVA","region":"San Francisco Bay","subregion":"San Francisco Bay","created":"1982","styles":["white","red","sweet"],"grapes":"Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Sémillon, Cabernet Sauvignon, Petite Sirah","summary":"One of California's oldest wine districts (AVA 1982, expanded 2006), an east-west valley in Alameda County where cool air from San Francisco Bay flows in each afternoon. Gravelly soils reminded early growers of Graves; Charles Wetmore planted Sémillon and Sauvignon Blanc from Château d'Yquem cuttings in the 1880s, and his Cresta Blanca white won a gold medal at the 1889 Paris Exposition.\n\nCarl Wente (1883) and James Concannon (1883) founded wineries that are still family-run. The Wente clone of Chardonnay, propagated from cuttings Ernest Wente obtained in 1912, is the ancestor of most Chardonnay planted in California. Concannon's Petite Sirah was the first varietal bottling of that grape (1961).","producers":["Wente","Concannon","Darcie Kent","Steven Kent","Murrieta's Well","Retzlaff"],"nerd_note":"Most Chardonnay in California descends from the 'Wente clone', making Livermore the grandmother of the state's most planted white.","rslug":"san-francisco-bay"},"us-lodi":{"name":"Lodi","cat":"AVA","region":"Lodi","subregion":"Central Valley","created":"1986","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Zinfandel, Cabernet Sauvignon, Cinsault, Carignan, Petite Sirah, Chardonnay","summary":"Lodi (1986, expanded 2002) is a grower's appellation of about 540,000 acres and roughly 100,000 acres of vines in San Joaquin and Sacramento counties. It is California's Zinfandel capital, with hundreds of acres of vines more than 50 and some more than 100 years old, often own-rooted in sand. Cooling Delta breezes keep it fresher than the rest of the Central Valley.\n\nThe 'Lodi Native' project has bottled single-vineyard old-vine Zinfandels with minimal intervention to show site differences, and producers from Turley to Bedrock and Michael David buy Lodi old vines.\n\nLodi's sandy soils matter for more than texture: phylloxera cannot move easily through sand, so many old vineyards were never grafted, and some own-rooted Zinfandel and Cinsault vines are more than 130 years old. Growers here developed the Lodi Rules sustainability standard in 2005, now used outside the region. The style ranges from big, ripe, oaky Zinfandel to lighter, fresher bottlings from young producers who pick earlier.","producers":["Michael David","Bokisch","Klinker Brick","m2","St. Amant","Turley (sourcing)","Bedrock (sourcing)"],"contains":["Alta Mesa","Borden Ranch","Clements Hills","Cosumnes River","Jahant","Mokelumne River","Sloughhouse"],"rslug":"lodi"},"us-loess-hills-district":{"name":"Loess Hills District","cat":"AVA","region":"Midwest","subregion":"Iowa and Missouri","created":"2016","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Marquette, La Crescent, Frontenac, Edelweiss","summary":"An AVA (2016) of about 4.6 million acres (some 7,200 square miles) along the Missouri River in western Iowa and northwestern Missouri, defined by its extraordinarily deep wind-blown loess soils, which form steep bluffs. At approval it had 66 vineyards on 112 acres and 13 wineries.","rslug":"midwest"},"us-long-island":{"name":"Long Island","cat":"AVA","region":"Long Island","subregion":"New York","created":"2001","styles":["red","white","rose","sparkling"],"grapes":"Merlot, Cabernet Franc, Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Cabernet Sauvignon","summary":"The AVA (2001) covering Nassau and Suffolk counties, surrounding the North Fork and Hamptons AVAs. Long Island's maritime climate gives a growing season of about 220 days, longer than upstate, and sandy glacial soils. Merlot, Cabernet Franc, Chardonnay and rosé are its strengths.","contains":["North Fork of Long Island","The Hamptons, Long Island"],"rslug":"long-island"},"us-long-valley-lake-county":{"name":"Long Valley-Lake County","cat":"AVA","region":"Lake County","subregion":"North Coast","created":"2023","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, Sauvignon Blanc, Petite Sirah","summary":"A small, high valley (2023) of about 7,605 acres in northeastern Lake County, northeast of Clear Lake, with five vineyards (about 149 acres) and three wineries at approval. Elevation and cold winters distinguish it from High Valley, part of which it took over. Unusually, it lies outside the Clear Lake AVA; TTB adjusted the North Coast boundary to include it.","rslug":"lake-county"},"us-loramie-creek":{"name":"Loramie Creek","cat":"AVA","region":"Midwest","subregion":"Ohio","created":"1982","styles":["white"],"grapes":"Seyval Blanc","summary":"A tiny AVA (1982) in Shelby County, western Ohio, created for a single farm winery; no longer commercially active, but still on the TTB list.","rslug":"midwest"},"us-los-carneros":{"name":"Los Carneros","cat":"AVA","region":"Napa Valley","subregion":"Napa Valley and Sonoma County","created":"1983","styles":["red","white","sparkling"],"grapes":"Chardonnay, Pinot Noir, Merlot, Syrah","summary":"Los Carneros (1983) straddles the southern ends of Napa and Sonoma counties along San Pablo Bay, and it was one of the first AVAs drawn by climate rather than county lines. Its name means 'the rams', for the sheep that once grazed its low, rolling hills. Fog and a near-daily wind off the bay keep it cool, and the shallow, dense clay soils restrain vigour.\n\nThat made it the natural home for Pinot Noir and Chardonnay, first planted seriously by Louis M. Martini and Beaulieu (with Tchelistcheff) in the 1930s and 1940s, and later by Saintsbury (1981). Champagne and Cava houses followed: Domaine Carneros (Taittinger), Gloria Ferrer (Freixenet) and Artesa (Codorníu). Hyde and Hudson are its most famous grower vineyards; Larry Hyde's partnership with Aubert de Villaine of Domaine de la Romanée-Conti produces HdV.","terroir":"Shallow, heavy clay loams over hardpan; persistent wind and fog from San Pablo Bay; one of the coolest parts of the North Coast.","producers":["Domaine Carneros","Saintsbury","Gloria Ferrer","Artesa","Hyde de Villaine (HdV)","Truchard","Etude"],"nerd_note":"Carneros wines can be labeled with Napa Valley or Sonoma Valley as well as Los Carneros, depending on which side of the county line the grapes grew.","rslug":"napa-valley"},"us-los-olivos-district":{"name":"Los Olivos District","cat":"AVA","region":"Santa Barbara County","subregion":"Santa Ynez Valley","created":"2016","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Sauvignon Blanc, Syrah, Grenache, Cabernet Franc","summary":"An alluvial plain (2016) of about 22,800 acres around the town of Los Olivos, between Ballard Canyon and Happy Canyon, with 47 vineyards (about 1,120 acres) at approval. Moderate temperatures and well-drained soils suit Sauvignon Blanc, Rhône and Bordeaux varieties.","producers":["Beckmen","Andrew Murray","Brander"],"rslug":"santa-barbara-county"},"us-lower-long-tom":{"name":"Lower Long Tom","cat":"AVA","region":"Willamette Valley","subregion":"Willamette Valley","created":"2021","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Pinot Noir, Pinot Gris, Chardonnay","summary":"The southernmost Willamette sub-AVA (2021), about 25,000 acres in the hills west of the Long Tom River near Monroe, between Eugene and Corvallis. Thin, uplifted marine sedimentary soils (Bellpine and Bellpine/Jory) and a slightly warmer climate. At approval it had 22 vineyards (492 acres) and 10 wineries.","producers":["Benton-Lane","High Pass","Brigadoon"],"rslug":"willamette-valley"},"us-madera":{"name":"Madera","cat":"AVA","region":"Central Valley","subregion":"Central Valley","created":"1984","styles":["fortified","sweet","red","white"],"grapes":"Touriga Nacional, Tinta Cão, Orange Muscat, Black Muscat, French Colombard","summary":"A warm Central Valley AVA (1985) in Madera and Fresno counties, known for fortified and sweet wines: Ficklin pioneered port-style wines from Portuguese varieties in 1946, and Andrew Quady's Essensia (orange Muscat) and Elysium (black Muscat) are dessert-wine classics.","producers":["Quady","Ficklin"],"rslug":"central-valley"},"us-malibu-coast":{"name":"Malibu Coast","cat":"AVA","region":"Southern California","subregion":"Southern California","created":"2014","styles":["red","white","rose"],"grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, Syrah, Chardonnay, Pinot Noir","summary":"An AVA (2014) of about 44,590 acres in the Santa Monica Mountains of Los Angeles and Ventura counties, with about 52 small vineyards on 198 acres at approval. Ocean views and marine air, but also the constant threat of wildfire: the 2018 Woolsey Fire burned through many vineyards. Los Angeles County has since restricted new vineyard planting in the Santa Monica Mountains.","contains":["Saddle Rock-Malibu","Malibu-Newton Canyon"],"rslug":"southern-california"},"us-malibu-newton-canyon":{"name":"Malibu-Newton Canyon","cat":"AVA","region":"Southern California","subregion":"Malibu Coast","created":"1996","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, Chardonnay","summary":"A bowl-shaped AVA (1996) of about 850 acres high in the Santa Monica Mountains, created around Rosenthal's vineyard, which was planted in 1987. Above the marine layer, it grows Cabernet Sauvignon and Chardonnay.","producers":["Rosenthal"],"rslug":"southern-california"},"us-manton-valley":{"name":"Manton Valley","cat":"AVA","region":"Far North California","subregion":"Shasta and Tehama counties","created":"2014","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Syrah, Cabernet Sauvignon, Petite Sirah","summary":"A small valley (2014) of about 11,200 acres on the western slope of Mount Lassen in Shasta and Tehama counties. Volcanic soils from Lassen and elevations around 600 m give cool nights; a few small wineries.","rslug":"far-north-california"},"us-marthas-vineyard":{"name":"Martha's Vineyard","cat":"AVA","region":"New England","subregion":"Southeastern New England","created":"1985","styles":["white","red"],"grapes":"Chardonnay, Riesling, Cabernet Sauvignon","summary":"An island AVA (1985) covering Martha's Vineyard, petitioned by Chicama Vineyards, which planted the island's first vinifera in 1971. Not to be confused with Heitz's famous Martha's Vineyard Cabernet in Napa. Little commercial production remains.","rslug":"new-england"},"us-mcdowell-valley":{"name":"McDowell Valley","cat":"AVA","region":"Mendocino County","subregion":"Mendocino","created":"1981","styles":["red","rose"],"grapes":"Syrah, Grenache, Petite Sirah","summary":"A tiny bench valley (1982) east of Hopland at around 300 m. It is best known for old Syrah and Grenache, among California's oldest Rhône plantings (some vines date from 1919), which McDowell Valley Vineyards bottled in the early days of the Rhône Rangers movement.","producers":["McDowell Valley Vineyards"],"rslug":"mendocino-county"},"us-mcminnville":{"name":"McMinnville","cat":"AVA","region":"Willamette Valley","subregion":"Willamette Valley","created":"2005","styles":["red"],"grapes":"Pinot Noir, Pinot Gris, Chardonnay","summary":"A sub-AVA (2005) in the Coast Range foothills west and southwest of McMinnville, limited to elevations of 200 to 1,000 feet, with uplifted marine sediments and basalt. Close to the Van Duzer Corridor, it is cool and windy, and the Pinot Noirs are dark and firmly structured. Eyrie's winery is in town, though its vineyards are in the Dundee Hills.","producers":["Brittan","Youngberg Hill","Maysara"],"rslug":"willamette-valley"},"us-mendocino":{"name":"Mendocino","cat":"AVA","region":"Mendocino County","subregion":"North Coast","created":"1984","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Zinfandel, Petite Sirah, Carignan, Cabernet Sauvignon, Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc","summary":"The main AVA (1984) for the southern and central valleys of Mendocino County, including Anderson, Ukiah, Redwood and Potter valleys and McDowell Valley. It is used for blends from across those valleys; inland it means warm-climate Zinfandel, Petite Sirah, Carignan and Cabernet.","producers":["Parducci","Fetzer","Bonterra","McNab Ridge"],"rslug":"mendocino-county"},"us-mendocino-ridge":{"name":"Mendocino Ridge","cat":"AVA","region":"Mendocino County","subregion":"Mendocino","created":"1997","styles":["red"],"grapes":"Zinfandel, Pinot Noir, Syrah","summary":"The only non-contiguous AVA in the United States (1997): it consists of every piece of land above 1,200 feet (366 m) in a coastal strip of southwestern Mendocino County, so its map looks like an archipelago of 'islands in the sky' poking above the marine layer. Old Zinfandel vineyards such as Zeni and Ciapusci, planted by Italian families, grow on these ridges alongside newer Pinot Noir.\n\nIn January 2024 TTB proposed renaming it 'Mendocino Coast Ridge' to stress its coastal identity; as of late 2026 that proposal had not been finalised.","producers":["Edmeades (Zeni Vineyard)","Greenwood Ridge","Drew","Mariah"],"nerd_note":"Because only land above 1,200 feet counts, the AVA covers about 87,000 acres on paper but has only a few hundred acres of vines.","rslug":"mendocino-county"},"us-merritt-island":{"name":"Merritt Island","cat":"AVA","region":"Sacramento Valley","subregion":"Clarksburg","created":"1983","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Petite Sirah, Chenin Blanc, Chardonnay","summary":"A Delta island (1983) of about 5,000 acres inside Clarksburg, bounded by the Sacramento River and Elk Slough. Sandy loam over peat and cool Delta breezes; the Bogle family petitioned for it.","producers":["Bogle"],"rslug":"sacramento-valley"},"us-mesilla-valley":{"name":"Mesilla Valley","cat":"AVA","region":"Southwest and Mountain West","subregion":"New Mexico and Texas","created":"1985","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Mission, Cabernet Sauvignon, Zinfandel, Muscat","summary":"An AVA (1985) along the Rio Grande from Las Cruces, New Mexico into far West Texas near El Paso, where Spanish friars planted Mission vines in the 17th century. Hot desert summers; St. Clair and La Viña are among its wineries.","rslug":"southwest-and-mountain-west"},"us-middle-rio-grande-valley":{"name":"Middle Rio Grande Valley","cat":"AVA","region":"Southwest and Mountain West","subregion":"New Mexico","created":"1988","styles":["sparkling","red","white"],"grapes":"Chardonnay, Pinot Noir, Mission, Cabernet Sauvignon","summary":"An AVA (1988) along the Rio Grande from Santa Fe's southern edge through Albuquerque to Socorro. Spanish friars planted Mission vines near Socorro around 1629, the first in what became the United States. Gruet, founded by a Champagne family from Bethon in 1984, makes traditional-method sparkling wine here.","producers":["Gruet","Casa Rondeña","Milagro"],"rslug":"southwest-and-mountain-west"},"us-middleburg-virginia":{"name":"Middleburg Virginia","cat":"AVA","region":"Virginia","subregion":"Virginia","created":"2012","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Cabernet Franc, Petit Verdot, Merlot, Chardonnay, Viognier","summary":"An AVA (2012) of about 190 square miles in Loudoun and Fauquier counties, horse country an hour from Washington, D.C. At approval it had 10 vineyards and 14 wineries. Cabernet Franc, Petit Verdot and Chardonnay; Boxwood and RdV (founded by Rutger de Vink) make some of Virginia's most serious Bordeaux blends nearby.","producers":["Boxwood","Greenhill","Chrysalis (nearby)","Cana"],"rslug":"virginia"},"us-mimbres-valley":{"name":"Mimbres Valley","cat":"AVA","region":"Southwest and Mountain West","subregion":"New Mexico","created":"1985","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, Zinfandel, Chardonnay","summary":"An AVA (1985) in the Mimbres River valley of southwestern New Mexico around Deming. Large vineyards here, many owned by European investors in the 1980s, supply much of New Mexico's wine grapes.","producers":["St. Clair"],"rslug":"southwest-and-mountain-west"},"us-mississippi-delta":{"name":"Mississippi Delta","cat":"AVA","region":"Southeast","subregion":"Mississippi, Louisiana, Tennessee","created":"1984","styles":["white","red","sweet"],"grapes":"Muscadine, Scuppernong","summary":"A large alluvial AVA (1984) of about 4.1 million acres covering the Mississippi Delta in Mississippi, Louisiana and Tennessee. The Rushing Winery petitioned for it; muscadines dominate the little wine made here.","rslug":"southeast"},"us-mokelumne-river":{"name":"Mokelumne River","cat":"AVA","region":"Lodi","subregion":"Lodi","created":"2006","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Zinfandel, Cinsault, Carignan, Cabernet Sauvignon","summary":"The largest and most important Lodi sub-AVA (2006), on the deep, sandy alluvial fan of the Mokelumne River west of the foothills. It holds most of Lodi's oldest Zinfandel and Cinsault, own-rooted because phylloxera cannot thrive in such sand. Bechthold, Soucie and Royal Tee are famous old-vine sites.","producers":["Turley (Kirschenmann)","Bedrock","St. Amant","Michael David"],"rslug":"lodi"},"us-monterey":{"name":"Monterey","cat":"AVA","region":"Central Coast","subregion":"Central Coast","created":"1984","styles":["white","red"],"grapes":"Chardonnay, Pinot Noir, Riesling, Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot","summary":"Large AVA (1984) covering the Salinas Valley and its side valleys. Cold wind blows up the valley from Monterey Bay every afternoon, so the north is among the coolest places in California and yields are high on the flat valley floor; the south around King City is warmer. Monterey is a major source of Chardonnay, Pinot Noir and Riesling for big brands, with a small number of exceptional sites on the benches.","contains":["Santa Lucia Highlands","Arroyo Seco","Chalone","Carmel Valley","San Bernabe","San Lucas","Hames Valley","San Antonio Valley","Gabilan Mountains"],"rslug":"central-coast"},"us-monticello":{"name":"Monticello","cat":"AVA","region":"Virginia","subregion":"Virginia","created":"1984","styles":["red","white","sparkling"],"grapes":"Cabernet Franc, Petit Verdot, Merlot, Viognier, Petit Manseng, Chardonnay","summary":"Virginia's most important AVA (1984, expanded 2018), around Charlottesville in the Piedmont foothills of the Blue Ridge, named for Thomas Jefferson's home. Jefferson's vinifera failed, but modern growers on well-drained, clay-rich slopes make Virginia's best Cabernet Franc, Petit Verdot, Viognier and Bordeaux blends. Barboursville, Early Mountain, King Family, Jefferson Vineyards and Michael Shaps are leading producers.\n\nCharlottesville's wine scene grew from Gabriele Rausse's 1976 plantings at Barboursville and Horton Vineyards' bet on Viognier (from 1991), which led Virginia to adopt Viognier as its signature white. Petit Manseng, whose thick skins resist humidity, has become another local success. Summer rain and hurricanes remain the main threats, so growers favour well-drained slopes in the foothills (around 150 to 450 m), open canopies and early-ripening clones.","producers":["Barboursville","Early Mountain","King Family","Jefferson Vineyards","Michael Shaps","Keswick","Veritas"],"nerd_note":"Jefferson Vineyards grows on land that Jefferson gave to Philip Mazzei for his 1774 vineyard experiment.","rslug":"virginia"},"us-moon-mountain-district-sonoma-county":{"name":"Moon Mountain District Sonoma County","cat":"AVA","region":"Sonoma County","subregion":"Sonoma Valley","created":"2013","styles":["red"],"grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, Zinfandel, Merlot, Syrah","summary":"A steep mountain AVA (2013) of about 17,600 acres on the western face of the Mayacamas above the Sonoma Valley, with 40 commercial vineyards at the time of the petition. Volcanic rock and red soils on west-facing slopes give dense Cabernet Sauvignon and Zinfandel.\n\nMonte Rosso, planted in the 1880s by Emmanuel Goldstein and bought by Louis M. Martini in 1938, is its most famous vineyard, with Zinfandel and Cabernet vines more than a century old.","producers":["Louis M. Martini (Monte Rosso)","Kamen","Repris","Hanzell (Moon Mountain parcels)","Bedrock (Monte Rosso)"],"nerd_note":"'Sonoma County' is part of the official AVA name to keep it from being confused with other Moon Mountains.","rslug":"sonoma-county"},"us-mount-pisgah-polk-county-oregon":{"name":"Mount Pisgah, Polk County, Oregon","cat":"AVA","region":"Willamette Valley","subregion":"Willamette Valley","created":"2022","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Pinot Noir, Chardonnay","summary":"A small AVA (2022) of about 5,850 acres around Mount Pisgah, a hill west of Salem near the town of Dallas. Its soils come from Siletz River volcanics, basalt about 40 to 60 million years old and much older than the Jory of the Dundee Hills. At approval it had 10 vineyards (531 acres) and two wineries, including Freedom Hill Vineyard, a famous source of Pinot Noir.","producers":["Freedom Hill (grower)","Ken Wright (Freedom Hill)","Bethel Heights (sourcing)"],"rslug":"willamette-valley"},"us-mt-harlan":{"name":"Mt. Harlan","cat":"AVA","region":"Central Coast","subregion":"Gabilan Mountains","created":"1990","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Pinot Noir, Chardonnay, Viognier","summary":"A one-producer mountain AVA (1990) high in the Gabilan Range, about 670 to 790 m, created for Josh Jensen's Calera. After studying in Burgundy, Jensen searched California for limestone and found it on an old lime kiln site here; he planted Pinot Noir from 1975 in named parcels (Selleck, Reed, Jensen, Mills, de Villiers, Ryan).\n\nThe Pinots are lean, mineral and long-lived, and Calera's Viognier was one of the first in America.\n\nJensen planted his first three Pinot Noir parcels in 1975 at about 670 m, far from any town and with scarce water. Yields are very low because of the thin soils and drought. Calera's Pinots are known for long ageing: they start firm and savoury and open into mushroom, rose and red-fruit perfume after a decade. Jensen sold the winery to Duckhorn in 2017, and the estate continues to bottle each named parcel separately.","producers":["Calera"],"nerd_note":"Calera means lime kiln in Spanish; the old kiln Jensen found on the mountain told him the limestone he wanted was there.","rslug":"central-coast"},"us-mt-veeder":{"name":"Mt. Veeder","cat":"AVA","region":"Napa Valley","subregion":"Napa Valley","created":"1990","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, Malbec, Merlot, Syrah, Zinfandel, Chardonnay","summary":"Mountain AVA (1990) in the southern Mayacamas, west of the city of Napa, with vineyards from about 150 to 750 m on steep, forested slopes. Unlike the volcanic north, much of Mt. Veeder is uplifted ancient seabed: shallow, acidic sandstone and shale soils that keep yields tiny. The wines are taut and powerful, Cabernet Sauvignon and Malbec with firm acid and dusty tannin that need years in bottle.\n\nMayacamas Vineyards, built around an 1889 stone winery, is the reference. Its 1971 Cabernet was in the 1976 Paris red flight and tied for third place at the 30th-anniversary rematch in 2006. The Hess Collection pairs mountain Cabernet with a museum of contemporary art.","terroir":"Shallow, sedimentary marine soils (sandstone, shale) with some volcanic intrusions; steep slopes, cooler marine-influenced climate.","producers":["Mayacamas Vineyards","The Hess Collection","Mount Veeder Winery","Lokoya","Mount Brave"],"nerd_note":"Mayacamas's 1970s Cabernets were famously hard in youth; bottles from the Travers era (1968 to 2013) are now prized for how slowly they evolved.","rslug":"napa-valley"},"us-naches-heights":{"name":"Naches Heights","cat":"AVA","region":"Columbia Valley","subregion":"Columbia Valley","created":"2011","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Syrah, Riesling, Tempranillo, Pinot Gris","summary":"A volcanic plateau (2011) of about 13,250 acres northwest of Yakima, at 365 to 640 m, formed by the Tieton andesite lava flow; its soils are wind-blown loess over that volcanic rock. At approval it had 105 acres of vines.","rslug":"columbia-valley"},"us-napa-valley":{"name":"Napa Valley","cat":"AVA","region":"Napa Valley","subregion":"North Coast","created":"1981","styles":["red","white","sparkling","sweet"],"grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot, Cabernet Franc, Petit Verdot, Malbec, Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Pinot Noir, Zinfandel","summary":"The most famous wine address in the Americas, approved in 1981 as California's first AVA and the second in the country after Augusta, Missouri. The AVA covers almost all of Napa County apart from the area around Lake Berryessa, so it includes the valley floor, both mountain ranges and the side valleys, and it contains 17 smaller AVAs.\n\nNapa's reputation rests on Cabernet Sauvignon. The style that made it famous is ripe and dense, with blackcurrant, plum, cocoa and sweet tannin, often lifted by new French oak. Thoughtful producers have moved toward earlier picking and less oak since the 2010s, but generosity of fruit is the house signature. A Napa Valley label alone usually means a blend of sites, while the sub-AVAs and named vineyards tell you where on the map the wine comes from.\n\nThe valley is also a lesson in land politics. The Agricultural Preserve of 1968 forbade subdivision of the valley floor into lots smaller than 20 acres (later 40), which is why the valley still looks like farmland rather than the Silicon Valley sprawl a few miles south. Land values are now among the highest for any vineyard outside Burgundy and Champagne, and a ton of Napa Cabernet costs several times the California average.","terroir":"Mediterranean climate with a strong north-south temperature gradient: cool and foggy near San Pablo Bay, warm toward Calistoga. Soils mix volcanic material from the Sonoma Volcanics, uplifted marine sediments and gravelly alluvial fans. Mountain vineyards above the fog line give smaller berries and firmer tannin.","producers":["Robert Mondavi Winery","Stag's Leap Wine Cellars","Chateau Montelena","Inglenook","Beaulieu Vineyard","Heitz Cellar","Opus One","Harlan Estate","Screaming Eagle","Dominus Estate","Spottswoode","Corison"],"nerd_note":"Napa Valley was the first AVA approved in California (1981). Wines from any of its 17 sub-AVAs must also show 'Napa Valley' on the label under California's conjunctive labeling law.","contains":["Calistoga","Diamond Mountain District","Spring Mountain District","St. Helena","Rutherford","Oakville","Yountville","Stags Leap District","Oak Knoll District of Napa Valley","Mt. Veeder","Atlas Peak","Howell Mountain","Chiles Valley","Coombsville","Wild Horse Valley","Los Carneros","Crystal Springs of Napa Valley"],"rslug":"napa-valley"},"us-nashoba-valley":{"name":"Nashoba Valley","cat":"AVA","region":"New England","subregion":"Massachusetts","created":"2026","styles":["white","red"],"grapes":"Albariño, Cabernet Franc, Chardonnay, Riesling, St. Croix","summary":"A new AVA (effective April 2026) of about 18,367 acres in Worcester County, Massachusetts, around Bolton, Harvard and Stow, an old apple-orchard area west of Boston. The soils formed in glacial till and outwash; the most common series, Paxton, is a well-drained, deep loam. TTB accepted about 1,697 growing degree days (Celsius base), placing it in Winkler Region III. At approval it had three commercial vineyards on about 16 acres, growing Albariño, Cabernet Franc, Chardonnay, Riesling and St. Croix. Nashoba Valley Winery (1978), known for fruit wines, is the best-known producer.","producers":["Nashoba Valley Winery"],"rslug":"new-england"},"us-niagara-escarpment":{"name":"Niagara Escarpment","cat":"AVA","region":"New York","subregion":"New York","created":"2005","styles":["white","red","sweet"],"grapes":"Riesling, Cabernet Franc, Vidal, Chardonnay","summary":"An AVA (2005) on the bench below the Niagara Escarpment in Niagara County, the US counterpart of Ontario's Niagara benchlands across the river. Lake Ontario's moderating effect and limestone-rich soils suit Riesling, Cabernet Franc and ice wine. At approval it had about 400 acres of vineyards.","producers":["Freedom Run","Arrowhead Spring","Leonard Oakes"],"rslug":"new-york"},"us-nine-lakes-of-east-tennessee":{"name":"Nine Lakes of East Tennessee","cat":"AVA","region":"Southeast","subregion":"Tennessee","created":"2026","styles":["red","white","sweet"],"grapes":"Chambourcin, Cabernet Franc, Muscadine, Vidal, Traminette","summary":"A large AVA (effective April 2026) of about 4,064 square miles in the Valley and Ridge Province of East Tennessee, named for the nine reservoirs created by Tennessee Valley Authority dams in the 14 counties around Knoxville. Parallel ridges (about 330 to 460 m) and valleys let growers choose slopes where morning sun dries the dew. At approval it had about 232 acres of vines and 29 wineries, many of them busy tourist wineries around the Great Smoky Mountains gateway towns.","rslug":"southeast"},"us-north-coast":{"name":"North Coast","cat":"AVA","region":"North Coast","subregion":"California","created":"1983","styles":["red","white","sparkling"],"grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, Chardonnay, Pinot Noir, Zinfandel, Sauvignon Blanc, Merlot","summary":"A vast umbrella AVA (1983) of about 3.3 million acres covering the wine country north of San Francisco: most of Napa, Sonoma, Mendocino and Lake counties plus parts of Solano and Marin. It contains dozens of smaller AVAs and is mostly used for blends that draw on several counties, much as 'Central Coast' is used farther south.","nerd_note":"TTB modified the North Coast boundary when it created the Petaluma Gap AVA (2017) and again for Long Valley-Lake County (2023); the 2024 Comptche AVA was deliberately left outside it.","rslug":"north-coast"},"us-north-fork-of-long-island":{"name":"North Fork of Long Island","cat":"AVA","region":"Long Island","subregion":"Long Island","created":"1986","styles":["red","white","rose"],"grapes":"Merlot, Cabernet Franc, Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc","summary":"The northern prong (1986) of eastern Long Island, between Long Island Sound and Peconic Bay, with most of the island's vineyards and about 40 wineries. Water on three sides moderates temperatures; well-drained sandy loam suits Merlot, Cabernet Franc and Chardonnay in a fresh, Bordeaux-like style. Alex and Louisa Hargrave planted the first vineyard at Cutchogue in 1973.","producers":["Paumanok","Bedell","Macari","Shinn Estate","Lieb","Castello di Borghese"],"rslug":"long-island"},"us-north-fork-of-roanoke":{"name":"North Fork of Roanoke","cat":"AVA","region":"Virginia","subregion":"Virginia","created":"1983","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Chardonnay, Cabernet Sauvignon","summary":"A small AVA (1983) in the valley of the North Fork of the Roanoke River in Montgomery and Roanoke counties, in the Ridge and Valley region of southwestern Virginia. Very few vineyards today.","rslug":"virginia"},"us-north-yuba":{"name":"North Yuba","cat":"AVA","region":"Sierra Foothills","subregion":"Sierra Foothills","created":"1985","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, Riesling, Sauvignon Blanc","summary":"A small, high foothill AVA (1985) in Yuba County, created around Renaissance Vineyard, a winery founded by a spiritual community in the 1970s and known for long-aged Cabernet and late-harvest whites.","producers":["Renaissance Vineyard"],"rslug":"sierra-foothills"},"us-northern-neck-george-washington-birthplace":{"name":"Northern Neck George Washington Birthplace","cat":"AVA","region":"Virginia","subregion":"Virginia","created":"1987","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Cabernet Franc, Chardonnay, Petit Verdot","summary":"An AVA (1987) on the Northern Neck peninsula between the Potomac and Rappahannock rivers, where George Washington was born in 1732. Rivers and the Chesapeake moderate the climate; Ingleside Vineyards, which petitioned for the AVA, is the leading winery.","producers":["Ingleside"],"rslug":"virginia"},"us-northern-sonoma":{"name":"Northern Sonoma","cat":"AVA","region":"Sonoma County","subregion":"North Coast","created":"1985","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, Chardonnay, Pinot Noir, Zinfandel","summary":"An umbrella AVA (1985) covering most of northern Sonoma County, including the Russian River, Dry Creek, Alexander and Knights valleys, Chalk Hill and Rockpile. It was petitioned by E. & J. Gallo, whose estate vineyards span several of those valleys, and it is used mainly by producers blending across them.","producers":["Gallo Family Vineyards","Jordan (some bottlings)"],"rslug":"sonoma-county"},"us-oak-knoll-district-of-napa-valley":{"name":"Oak Knoll District of Napa Valley","cat":"AVA","region":"Napa Valley","subregion":"Napa Valley","created":"2004","styles":["white","red"],"grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot, Chardonnay, Riesling, Sauvignon Blanc","summary":"The southernmost valley-floor AVA (2004) apart from Carneros, between the city of Napa and Yountville. Cool, foggy mornings and a long, even season suit Chardonnay, Riesling and Merlot as well as a more restrained, savoury style of Cabernet Sauvignon.\n\nTrefethen is the anchor. Its 1886 Eshcol winery, one of Napa's surviving 19th-century wooden 'ghost wineries', was badly damaged in the magnitude 6.0 South Napa earthquake of 2014 and rebuilt. Trefethen's 1976 Chardonnay was judged best Chardonnay at the 1979 Gault-Millau 'Wine Olympics' in Paris.","terroir":"Alluvial fans from the Mayacamas and Dry Creek, deep gravelly and clay loams; marine-cooled.","producers":["Trefethen Family Vineyards","Silver Trident","Robert Biale (Black Chicken Zinfandel sourced nearby)"],"nerd_note":"Oak Knoll is one of the few Napa sub-AVAs whose official name includes 'of Napa Valley', a compromise to avoid clashing with other Oak Knolls.","rslug":"napa-valley"},"us-oakville":{"name":"Oakville","cat":"AVA","region":"Napa Valley","subregion":"Napa Valley","created":"1993","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, Cabernet Franc, Merlot, Petit Verdot, Sauvignon Blanc","summary":"Oakville (1993) is the richest square mile in American wine. The western bench below the Mayacamas holds To Kalon, the vineyard H.W. Crabb planted in the 1860s and named from the Greek for 'the highest beauty'; Robert Mondavi built his winery on it in 1966, the first major new winery in Napa since Prohibition. Opus One stands across Highway 29, Harlan Estate is on the western hills above, and Screaming Eagle, Dalla Valle and the old Heitz Martha's Vineyard are minutes away.\n\nOakville Cabernet combines the depth of Rutherford with a little more freshness and polish: cassis, violets, graphite and long, fine-grained tannin. Vineyards on the western alluvial fans give the most perfumed wines; the eastern side toward the Silverado Trail has heavier soils and more muscular reds.","terroir":"Gravelly alluvial fans from the Mayacamas on the west; volcanic-derived soils from the Vaca Range on the east; moderately warm, with fog burning off by late morning.","producers":["Robert Mondavi Winery","Opus One","Harlan Estate","Screaming Eagle","Dalla Valle","Far Niente","Heitz Cellar (Martha's Vineyard)","Silver Oak","Groth","PlumpJack"],"nerd_note":"To Kalon today is divided among several owners (Mondavi/Constellation, Beckstoffer, Opus One, the Detert and MacDonald families), and Constellation's trademark on the name has been the subject of lawsuits.","rslug":"napa-valley"},"us-ohio-river-valley":{"name":"Ohio River Valley","cat":"AVA","region":"Midwest","subregion":"Indiana, Kentucky, Ohio, West Virginia","created":"1983","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Chambourcin, Vidal, Catawba, Traminette, Cabernet Franc","summary":"A huge AVA (1983) of about 16 million acres along the Ohio River in Indiana, Kentucky, Ohio and West Virginia. This was the scene of America's first commercial wine boom: Swiss settlers at Vevay, Indiana planted vines in the early 1800s, and Nicholas Longworth's Catawba at Cincinnati made 'sparkling Catawba' famous in the 1840s and 1850s. Today it is a broad umbrella for scattered wineries.","contains":["Kanawha River Valley"],"rslug":"midwest"},"us-old-mission-peninsula":{"name":"Old Mission Peninsula","cat":"AVA","region":"Great Lakes","subregion":"Michigan","created":"1987","styles":["white","red","sparkling","sweet"],"grapes":"Riesling, Pinot Gris, Chardonnay, Pinot Noir, Gewürztraminer","summary":"A narrow, 30 km peninsula (1987) jutting into Grand Traverse Bay north of Traverse City. Lake Michigan surrounds it on three sides, delaying spring and extending autumn; cherry orchards share the slopes with Riesling, Pinot Gris, Pinot Noir and Chardonnay. Edward O'Keefe planted vinifera at Chateau Grand Traverse in 1974, Michigan's first commercial vinifera vineyard.","producers":["Chateau Grand Traverse","Brys Estate","Mari Vineyards","Bowers Harbor","2 Lads"],"rslug":"great-lakes"},"us-outer-coastal-plain":{"name":"Outer Coastal Plain","cat":"AVA","region":"Mid-Atlantic","subregion":"New Jersey","created":"2007","styles":["red","white","sparkling"],"grapes":"Cabernet Franc, Merlot, Chardonnay, Albariño, Chambourcin","summary":"New Jersey's largest AVA (2007, expanded 2017), about 2.25 million acres of sandy coastal plain in the southern half of the state. The Atlantic and Delaware Bay moderate the climate and give a long season; sandy, well-drained soils suit Cabernet Franc, Merlot, Chardonnay, Albariño and Chambourcin. Renault Winery at Egg Harbor City has operated since 1864.","producers":["Renault","Bellview","Sharrott","Tomasello"],"contains":["Cape May Peninsula"],"rslug":"mid-atlantic"},"us-ozark-highlands":{"name":"Ozark Highlands","cat":"AVA","region":"Midwest","subregion":"Missouri","created":"1987","styles":["red","white","sweet"],"grapes":"Norton, Catawba, Concord, Vignoles, Chambourcin","summary":"A large AVA (1987) of about 1.85 million acres on the Ozark Plateau of south-central Missouri, around St. James, with chert and limestone soils. Italian immigrants at Rosati and St. James grew Concord and Catawba; St. James Winery is one of Missouri's largest.","producers":["St. James Winery"],"rslug":"midwest"},"us-ozark-mountain":{"name":"Ozark Mountain","cat":"AVA","region":"Midwest","subregion":"Missouri, Arkansas, Oklahoma","created":"1986","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Norton, Chambourcin, Vignoles, Catawba","summary":"By area the largest AVA in the United States: about 33 million acres (around 52,000 square miles) of the Ozark Plateau in Missouri, Arkansas and Oklahoma, approved in 1986. It contains the Hermann, Ozark Highlands, Altus and Arkansas Mountain AVAs and is used mostly as a catch-all for wines from the Ozarks.","contains":["Hermann","Ozark Highlands","Arkansas Mountain","Altus"],"rslug":"midwest"},"us-pacheco-pass":{"name":"Pacheco Pass","cat":"AVA","region":"San Francisco Bay","subregion":"San Francisco Bay","created":"1984","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Zinfandel, Cabernet Sauvignon","summary":"A small AVA (1984) at the pass through the Diablo Range between Santa Clara and San Benito counties. Strong marine winds; a few vineyards on the historic route to the San Joaquin Valley.","rslug":"san-francisco-bay"},"us-paicines":{"name":"Paicines","cat":"AVA","region":"Central Coast","subregion":"San Benito","created":"1982","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, Chardonnay, Merlot","summary":"A small AVA (1982) in San Benito County south of Hollister, petitioned by Almaden, which once planted large vineyards here. Cool breezes; mostly grape-growing for other wineries.","rslug":"central-coast"},"us-palos-verdes-peninsula":{"name":"Palos Verdes Peninsula","cat":"AVA","region":"Southern California","subregion":"Southern California","created":"2021","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Syrah, Cabernet Sauvignon, Chardonnay","summary":"A small coastal AVA (2021) of about 15,900 acres on the hilly peninsula south of Los Angeles, with only about 7 acres of vines at approval. Uplifted marine terraces and constant ocean breezes.","rslug":"southern-california"},"us-paso-robles":{"name":"Paso Robles","cat":"AVA","region":"Paso Robles","subregion":"Central Coast","created":"1983","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, Zinfandel, Syrah, Grenache, Mourvèdre, Petite Sirah, Merlot","summary":"The large Paso Robles AVA (1983, expanded 2009) covers about 610,000 acres and more than 40,000 acres of vines in northern San Luis Obispo County. Hot days, very cold nights and limestone-rich hills on the west side give powerful Cabernet Sauvignon, Zinfandel and Rhône blends. Since 2014 it has been divided into 11 district AVAs.\n\nThe 2014 division into 11 districts came after a decade of debate and studies by the Paso Robles AVA Committee showing large differences in rainfall (from under 250 mm in the east to over 760 mm in the west), soils and temperature. Paso is also one of California's most ambitious Rhône regions: the Rhône Rangers movement of the 1980s and 1990s found its home here, and Saxum's James Berry Vineyard bottlings have been among the most highly scored American Rhône blends. Water is the key political issue, since the Paso Robles groundwater basin is overdrawn and the county has limited new plantings.","producers":["Tablas Creek","Saxum","L'Aventure","Justin","J. Lohr","Daou","Turley (Pesenti)","Linne Calodo"],"contains":["Adelaida District","Creston District","El Pomar District","Paso Robles Estrella District","Paso Robles Geneseo District","Paso Robles Highlands District","Paso Robles Willow Creek District","San Juan Creek","San Miguel District","Santa Margarita Ranch","Templeton Gap District"],"rslug":"paso-robles"},"us-paso-robles-estrella-district":{"name":"Paso Robles Estrella District","cat":"AVA","region":"Paso Robles","subregion":"Paso Robles","created":"2014","styles":["red"],"grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, Syrah, Merlot","summary":"Rolling plains (2014) northeast of Paso Robles along the Estrella River, warm and dry with alluvial terraces. Gary Eberle planted Syrah here in the 1970s, among California's first.","producers":["Eberle","J. Lohr (Estrella)","Vina Robles"],"rslug":"paso-robles"},"us-paso-robles-geneseo-district":{"name":"Paso Robles Geneseo District","cat":"AVA","region":"Paso Robles","subregion":"Paso Robles","created":"2014","styles":["red"],"grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, Syrah, Petite Sirah","summary":"An area of low hills (2014) east of Paso Robles city, with old alluvial terraces, some calcareous soils and moderate warmth, rainfall slightly higher than further east. Bordeaux and Rhône varieties do well, and Cass and Tobin James are among its wineries.","producers":["Cass","Tobin James"],"rslug":"paso-robles"},"us-paso-robles-highlands-district":{"name":"Paso Robles Highlands District","cat":"AVA","region":"Paso Robles","subregion":"Paso Robles","created":"2014","styles":["red"],"grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, Zinfandel, Syrah, Petite Sirah","summary":"The high, remote southeastern district (2014), at 365 to 610 m, with the largest day-night temperature swings in Paso Robles, low rainfall and sandy alluvial soils. Large vineyards grow Cabernet, Zinfandel and Rhône varieties.","rslug":"paso-robles"},"us-paso-robles-willow-creek-district":{"name":"Paso Robles Willow Creek District","cat":"AVA","region":"Paso Robles","subregion":"Paso Robles","created":"2014","styles":["red"],"grapes":"Syrah, Grenache, Mourvèdre, Zinfandel, Cabernet Sauvignon","summary":"Rolling hills (2014) west of Paso Robles town, with calcareous soils, cool marine air from the Templeton Gap and some of the region's most famous estates. Saxum's James Berry Vineyard, Linne Calodo and L'Aventure make dense, ripe Rhône and Cabernet blends here.","producers":["Saxum","L'Aventure","Linne Calodo","Booker"],"rslug":"paso-robles"},"us-paulsell-valley":{"name":"Paulsell Valley","cat":"AVA","region":"Central Valley","subregion":"Central Valley","created":"2022","styles":["red"],"grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, Zinfandel","summary":"A foothill AVA (2022) of about 34,155 acres in eastern Stanislaus County, in rolling grazing land at the edge of the Sierra foothills east of Modesto. The petition stressed its rolling topography and soils, distinct from the flat, irrigated valley floor to the west.","rslug":"central-valley"},"us-petaluma-gap":{"name":"Petaluma Gap","cat":"AVA","region":"Sonoma County","subregion":"Sonoma Coast","created":"2017","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Pinot Noir, Chardonnay, Syrah","summary":"One of the only AVAs defined by wind. Petaluma Gap (approved December 2017, effective 2018) covers about 202,500 acres in southern Sonoma and northern Marin, where a break in the coastal hills between Bodega Bay and San Pablo Bay funnels cold ocean air inland every afternoon. The petition cited afternoon wind speeds well above those of the surrounding areas.\n\nWind makes grape skins thicker and slows ripening while keeping acidity high, so the 4,000-odd acres of vines give Pinot Noir with deep colour and firm structure, and peppery cool-climate Syrah.","producers":["Keller Estate","Gap's Crown (vineyard)","Sun Chase (vineyard)","Kastania"],"nerd_note":"Wind was the main distinguishing feature in the petition, which made Petaluma Gap a test case for TTB's idea of what counts as terroir.","rslug":"sonoma-county"},"us-pine-mountain-cloverdale-peak":{"name":"Pine Mountain-Cloverdale Peak","cat":"AVA","region":"Sonoma County","subregion":"Northern Sonoma","created":"2011","styles":["red"],"grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, Cabernet Franc, Merlot","summary":"A small mountain AVA (2011) of about 4,570 acres around Pine Mountain, a 900 m peak on the western flank of the Mayacamas above Cloverdale, straddling the Sonoma-Mendocino line. Vineyards sit roughly from 490 to 900 m, above the Alexander Valley heat and fog, in thin, rocky volcanic and sedimentary soils. Cabernet Sauvignon and Cabernet Franc are dark, firm and mineral.","producers":["Miro (sourcing)","Silver Oak (Pine Mountain sourcing)","Sonoma Coast Vineyards (sourcing)"],"nerd_note":"The petitioners first asked for 'Pine Mountain-Mayacmas'; the name was changed after objections that it could be confused with Mayacamas Vineyards on Mt. Veeder.","rslug":"sonoma-county"},"us-potter-valley":{"name":"Potter Valley","cat":"AVA","region":"Mendocino County","subregion":"Mendocino","created":"1983","styles":["white","red","sweet"],"grapes":"Sauvignon Blanc, Riesling, Pinot Noir, Chardonnay","summary":"A high, cool valley (1983) northeast of Ukiah, ringed by mountains and fed by water diverted from the Eel River into the Russian River. Cold nights and a long season favour aromatic whites (Riesling, Sauvignon Blanc) and botrytised dessert wines, as well as Pinot Noir.","rslug":"mendocino-county"},"us-puget-sound":{"name":"Puget Sound","cat":"AVA","region":"Washington","subregion":"Washington","created":"1995","styles":["white","red"],"grapes":"Madeleine Angevine, Siegerrebe, Müller-Thurgau, Pinot Noir, Pinot Gris","summary":"A large AVA (1995) of about 4.5 million acres west of the Cascades, around Seattle and the islands of Puget Sound. Cool, cloudy and wet, it suits early-ripening grapes such as Madeleine Angevine, Siegerrebe, Müller-Thurgau and Pinot Noir. It is home to many wineries that buy grapes from eastern Washington, so the AVA name is used on only a small share of their wines.","producers":["Bainbridge Vineyards","Lopez Island Vineyards","Spoiled Dog"],"rslug":"washington"},"us-ramona-valley":{"name":"Ramona Valley","cat":"AVA","region":"Southern California","subregion":"South Coast","created":"2005","styles":["red"],"grapes":"Syrah, Cabernet Sauvignon, Tempranillo","summary":"An upland AVA (2006) of about 89,000 acres in San Diego County, at 300 to 900 m around the town of Ramona. At approval it had 17 vineyards on about 45 acres; many small wineries now grow Rhône and Bordeaux varieties.","rslug":"southern-california"},"us-rattlesnake-hills":{"name":"Rattlesnake Hills","cat":"AVA","region":"Yakima Valley","subregion":"Yakima Valley","created":"2006","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot, Syrah, Riesling","summary":"A sub-AVA (2006) of about 68,500 acres on the south-facing slopes of the Rattlesnake Hills north of the Yakima River, at 260 to 900 m, with many small wineries. Higher elevation and good air drainage reduce frost risk.","rslug":"yakima-valley"},"us-red-hill-douglas-county-oregon":{"name":"Red Hill Douglas County, Oregon","cat":"AVA","region":"Southern Oregon","subregion":"Umpqua Valley","created":"2005","styles":["red"],"grapes":"Pinot Noir","summary":"One of Oregon's smallest AVAs (2005), about 5,500 acres around a single hill near Yoncalla in the northern Umpqua, with red, Jory-type volcanic soil similar to the Dundee Hills. Essentially one large vineyard; Pinot Noir.","rslug":"southern-oregon"},"us-red-hills-lake-county":{"name":"Red Hills Lake County","cat":"AVA","region":"Lake County","subregion":"Clear Lake","created":"2004","styles":["red"],"grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot, Syrah, Petite Sirah","summary":"Volcanic hills (2004, expanded 2024) on the flanks of Mount Konocti south of Clear Lake, at about 430 to 900 m. The red soils are full of obsidian and the light is intense, so Cabernet Sauvignon develops thick skins, dark colour and firm tannin, often with surprising freshness. Andy Beckstoffer's Amber Knolls and Obsidian Ridge are among its best-known vineyards.","producers":["Obsidian Ridge","Beckstoffer (Amber Knolls)","Brassfield","Shannon Ridge"],"nerd_note":"The 2004 petition reported about 3,100 acres of vineyards; obsidian chips glitter in the soil after rain.","rslug":"lake-county"},"us-red-mountain":{"name":"Red Mountain","cat":"AVA","region":"Yakima Valley","subregion":"Yakima Valley","created":"2001","styles":["red"],"grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot, Syrah, Cabernet Franc, Petit Verdot","summary":"Washington's smallest AVA from 2001 until Candy Mountain arrived in 2020, about 4,000 acres on the southwest-facing slope of a low ridge near Benton City, at the east end of the Yakima Valley. It is one of the warmest spots in the state, with constant wind and very low rainfall; sandy, calcareous, rocky soils and wind stress produce small berries with thick skins.\n\nThe result is Washington's most tannic, concentrated Cabernet Sauvignon. Jim Holmes and John Williams planted Kiona and Ciel du Cheval in 1975; Hedges, Col Solare (Ste. Michelle and Antinori), Klipsun and Tapteil followed.\n\nThe sandy, calcareous soils over basalt are low in organic matter and very free-draining, so growers control vigour tightly with drip irrigation; vines are often own-rooted. Afternoon wind keeps the canopy dry and stresses the berries, shrinking them and thickening their skins, which is why the wines carry Washington's firmest tannins. The 2001 AVA was one of the first Washington appellations defined on such a small scale. Col Solare (founded by Antinori and Ste. Michelle), Duckhorn's Canvasback and Quilceda Creek's Galitzine Vineyard show how outside investment followed the reputation.","producers":["Hedges","Col Solare","Kiona","Fidélitas","Cadence","Quilceda Creek (Galitzine Vineyard)"],"nerd_note":"Despite the name, the mountain is not red; it is named for the cheatgrass (downy brome) that turns the slopes reddish in spring.","rslug":"yakima-valley"},"us-redwood-valley":{"name":"Redwood Valley","cat":"AVA","region":"Mendocino County","subregion":"Mendocino","created":"1996","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Zinfandel, Carignan, Petite Sirah, Cabernet Sauvignon","summary":"A warm valley (1997) north of Ukiah, at the head of the Russian River watershed, with red, iron-rich soils. Italian families planted Zinfandel, Carignan and Petite Sirah here early in the 20th century, and Fetzer was founded here in 1968. Old vines still give some of Mendocino's best Zinfandel and Carignan.","producers":["Fetzer (origins)","Frey","Barra","Golden Vineyards"],"rslug":"mendocino-county"},"us-ribbon-ridge":{"name":"Ribbon Ridge","cat":"AVA","region":"Willamette Valley","subregion":"Chehalem Mountains","created":"2005","styles":["red"],"grapes":"Pinot Noir, Chardonnay","summary":"A tiny ridge (2005) about 5.6 km long within the Chehalem Mountains, made of uplifted ocean sediments (Willakenzie soils) and sheltered by the larger mountains. At approval it had 14 vineyards on 286 acres. Beaux Frères (founded in 1986 by Mike Etzel with his brother-in-law Robert Parker) and Patricia Green made it famous for dark, dense Pinot Noir.","producers":["Beaux Frères","Patricia Green","Brick House","Trisaetum"],"rslug":"willamette-valley"},"us-river-junction":{"name":"River Junction","cat":"AVA","region":"Central Valley","subregion":"Central Valley","created":"2001","styles":["white","red"],"grapes":"Chardonnay, Merlot","summary":"A small AVA (2001) at the confluence of the Stanislaus and San Joaquin rivers in San Joaquin County, defined by river-borne alluvial soils and Delta breezes. It was created around a single large vineyard, and the name is rarely seen on labels.","rslug":"central-valley"},"us-rockpile":{"name":"Rockpile","cat":"AVA","region":"Sonoma County","subregion":"Northern Sonoma","created":"2002","styles":["red"],"grapes":"Zinfandel, Petite Sirah, Cabernet Sauvignon, Syrah","summary":"A rugged, remote AVA (2002) of ridges above the west arm of Lake Sonoma, limited to land above about 240 m (800 feet). Thin, rocky soils, wind and sun above the fog give concentrated, spicy Zinfandel and Petite Sirah with firm structure. The name comes from Rockpile Ranch, homesteaded in the 1850s by an ancestor of the Mauritson family.","producers":["Mauritson","Rockpile Vineyard (grower)","Carol Shelton","Paradise Ridge"],"rslug":"sonoma-county"},"us-rocky-knob":{"name":"Rocky Knob","cat":"AVA","region":"Virginia","subregion":"Virginia","created":"1983","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Cabernet Franc, Sangiovese, Pinot Grigio","summary":"A small, high AVA (1983) on the Blue Ridge plateau in Floyd and Patrick counties along the Blue Ridge Parkway, near Rocky Knob. Cool, elevated and wind-swept; Villa Appalaccia makes Italian varieties here.","producers":["Villa Appalaccia"],"rslug":"virginia"},"us-rocky-reach":{"name":"Rocky Reach","cat":"AVA","region":"Columbia Valley","subregion":"Columbia Valley","created":"2022","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Syrah, Cabernet Sauvignon, Riesling","summary":"A small AVA (2022) of about 32,000 acres on terraces along the Columbia River north of Wenatchee, near the Rocky Reach Dam. Its gravelly, rocky soils were deposited when the Missoula Floods and glacial outburst floods reshaped the canyon.","rslug":"columbia-valley"},"us-rogue-valley":{"name":"Rogue Valley","cat":"AVA","region":"Southern Oregon","subregion":"Southern Oregon","created":"1991","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Syrah, Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot, Tempranillo, Viognier, Chardonnay, Pinot Noir","summary":"A large, warm valley system (1991) around Medford and Grants Pass along the Rogue River and its tributaries (Applegate, Illinois, Bear Creek). The eastern part around Ashland is high and continental; the west is wetter. Syrah, Tempranillo, Cabernet, Merlot and Viognier thrive; Peter Britt's Valley View (1852) at Jacksonville was Oregon's first recorded winery.","producers":["Valley View","Troon (Applegate)","Weisinger","RoxyAnn","Kriselle"],"contains":["Applegate Valley"],"rslug":"southern-oregon"},"us-royal-slope":{"name":"Royal Slope","cat":"AVA","region":"Columbia Valley","subregion":"Columbia Valley","created":"2020","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot, Syrah, Chardonnay","summary":"A large AVA (2020) of 156,389 acres on the gentle, south-facing slopes of the Frenchman Hills, north of the Wahluke Slope. At approval 13 vineyards covered about 14,100 acres, many of them very large; the elevation and slope keep cold air draining away.","rslug":"columbia-valley"},"us-russian-river-valley":{"name":"Russian River Valley","cat":"AVA","region":"Sonoma County","subregion":"Northern Sonoma","created":"1983","styles":["red","white","sparkling"],"grapes":"Pinot Noir, Chardonnay, Zinfandel, Syrah, Sauvignon Blanc","summary":"California's most celebrated cool-climate Pinot Noir appellation (1983, expanded in 2005 and 2011), about 171,000 acres around the lower Russian River from Healdsburg south to Sebastopol. Fog rolls up the river and through the Petaluma Gap most summer nights and mornings, so days start cold, warm by mid-afternoon and cool again by evening. That pattern gives ripe red-berry and cola Pinot Noir with bright acidity, and rich, citrusy Chardonnay.\n\nThe Burgundian pioneers were Joseph Swan (first vintage 1969), the Rochioli family (Pinot from the late 1960s), Williams Selyem (first vintage 1981), Dehlinger and Kistler. Westside Road, along the river south of Healdsburg, is the Pinot Noir 'Golden Mile'. Old-vine Zinfandel and sparkling wine also thrive.","terroir":"Goldridge sandy loam (ancient marine sandstone), alluvial river terraces and some clay; fog-driven daily temperature swings.","producers":["Williams Selyem","Rochioli","Kistler","Dehlinger","Gary Farrell","Merry Edwards","Joseph Swan","Marimar Estate","DuMOL"],"nerd_note":"Burt Williams and Ed Selyem started as weekend garagistes; their 1987 Rochioli Vineyard Pinot Noir won the California State Fair's top prize and made the label a mailing-list sensation.","contains":["Green Valley of Russian River Valley","Chalk Hill"],"rslug":"sonoma-county"},"us-rutherford":{"name":"Rutherford","cat":"AVA","region":"Napa Valley","subregion":"Napa Valley","created":"1993","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot, Cabernet Franc, Petit Verdot, Sauvignon Blanc","summary":"Rutherford (1993) is classic Napa Cabernet country, a band across the valley floor between Oakville and St. Helena. Its fame rests on the Rutherford Bench, gravelly alluvial fans on the west side where Beaulieu and Inglenook built the reputation of California Cabernet before and after Prohibition. André Tchelistcheff, Beaulieu's winemaker from 1938, summed up the place in a line every Napa guide quotes: it takes Rutherford dust to grow great Cabernet.\n\nThe 'dust' is a texture as much as a taste: a fine, cocoa-powder tannin and a savoury, dusty, dried-herb note over ripe blackcurrant. Inglenook's 1941 Cabernet and Beaulieu's Georges de Latour Private Reserve (from the 1936 vintage) are touchstones of American wine history.","terroir":"Deep, well-drained gravelly loam on the western bench; heavier, more volcanic soils toward the Silverado Trail. Moderately warm with morning fog.","producers":["Inglenook","Beaulieu Vineyard","Caymus","Quintessa","Staglin Family","Frog's Leap","Hall Rutherford"],"nerd_note":"Gustave Niebaum, a Finnish sea captain who made his fortune in the Alaska fur trade, founded Inglenook around 1880. Francis Ford Coppola bought the estate in 1975 and, in 2011, bought back the Inglenook name and restored it.","rslug":"napa-valley"},"us-saddle-rock-malibu":{"name":"Saddle Rock-Malibu","cat":"AVA","region":"Southern California","subregion":"Malibu Coast","created":"2006","styles":["red","rose"],"grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, Syrah, Merlot","summary":"A tiny AVA (2006) of about 2,090 acres in the Santa Monica Mountains above Malibu, at 520 to 680 m, essentially the Semler family's Malibu Family Wines vineyard (planted 1997). Its hilltop vines produce Bordeaux and Rhône reds.","producers":["Malibu Family Wines (Saddlerock)"],"rslug":"southern-california"},"us-salado-creek":{"name":"Salado Creek","cat":"AVA","region":"Central Valley","subregion":"Central Valley","created":"2004","styles":["red"],"grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot","summary":"A small AVA (2004) of about 2,940 acres at the foot of the Diablo Range in western Stanislaus County, with 44 acres of vines at approval. Spanish explorer Gabriel Moraga named Salado ('salty') Creek.","rslug":"central-valley"},"us-san-antonio-valley":{"name":"San Antonio Valley","cat":"AVA","region":"Central Coast","subregion":"Monterey","created":"2006","styles":["red"],"grapes":"Syrah, Cabernet Sauvignon, Zinfandel","summary":"A remote valley (2006) west of the Salinas Valley in southern Monterey County, near Mission San Antonio de Padua (1771) and the Fort Hunter Liggett army base. Warm days and cold nights; Rhône and Bordeaux varieties and Zinfandel.","producers":["Pierce Ranch"],"rslug":"central-coast"},"us-san-benito":{"name":"San Benito","cat":"AVA","region":"Central Coast","subregion":"Central Coast","created":"1987","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Pinot Noir, Chardonnay, Zinfandel, Cabernet Sauvignon","summary":"Umbrella AVA (1987) for the winegrowing valleys of San Benito County, east of Monterey across the Gabilan Range, including Cienega Valley, Paicines and Lime Kiln Valley. Almaden once farmed thousands of acres here.","contains":["Cienega Valley","Paicines","Lime Kiln Valley"],"rslug":"central-coast"},"us-san-bernabe":{"name":"San Bernabe","cat":"AVA","region":"Central Coast","subregion":"Monterey","created":"2004","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot, Chardonnay, Syrah","summary":"An AVA (2004) defined by one enormous vineyard south of King City in the Salinas Valley, at one time described as the largest contiguous vineyard in the world (more than 5,000 acres, owned by Delicato). Sandy, wind-deposited soils grow a range of varieties for large brands.","producers":["Delicato"],"rslug":"central-coast"},"us-san-francisco-bay":{"name":"San Francisco Bay","cat":"AVA","region":"San Francisco Bay","subregion":"Central Coast","created":"1999","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, Chardonnay, Zinfandel, Petite Sirah","summary":"An umbrella AVA (1999) covering the counties around San Francisco Bay, roughly 2,450 square miles at creation, expanded in 2006, 2008 and 2024 (to take in all of the new Contra Costa AVA). It contains Livermore Valley, Santa Clara Valley, Lamorinda, Contra Costa and others and is used for blends from around the Bay.","rslug":"san-francisco-bay"},"us-san-juan-creek":{"name":"San Juan Creek","cat":"AVA","region":"Paso Robles","subregion":"Paso Robles","created":"2014","styles":["red"],"grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot, Syrah","summary":"The eastern, hottest and driest of the Paso Robles districts (2014), in the upper Cholame and San Juan Creek valleys, with low rainfall and big vineyards.","rslug":"paso-robles"},"us-san-lucas":{"name":"San Lucas","cat":"AVA","region":"Central Coast","subregion":"Monterey","created":"1987","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot, Chardonnay","summary":"A warm AVA (1987) in the southern Salinas Valley around San Lucas, beyond the reach of most of Monterey Bay's cold afternoon wind. Large vineyards on alluvial benches, some owned by Delicato, which petitioned for it, supply Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot and Chardonnay to big producers.","rslug":"central-coast"},"us-san-luis-obispo-coast":{"name":"San Luis Obispo Coast","cat":"AVA","region":"San Luis Obispo County","subregion":"Central Coast","created":"2022","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Chardonnay, Pinot Noir, Syrah, Albariño","summary":"A coastal AVA (2022) of about 408,500 acres stretching the length of San Luis Obispo County west of the Santa Lucia Range, from the Monterey line to the Santa Maria River. It is cooled directly by the Pacific and encloses the Edna Valley and Arroyo Grande Valley AVAs. Chardonnay, Pinot Noir and Syrah are the focus.","producers":["Stolo","Talley","Tolosa","Center of Effort"],"contains":["Edna Valley","Arroyo Grande Valley"],"rslug":"san-luis-obispo-county"},"us-san-luis-rey":{"name":"San Luis Rey","cat":"AVA","region":"Southern California","subregion":"South Coast","created":"2024","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Syrah, Cabernet Sauvignon, Grenache, Sangiovese","summary":"A new AVA (2024) of about 97,700 acres in northern San Diego County around Fallbrook, Bonsall, Pala and Pauma Valley, named for the San Luis Rey River and Mission San Luis Rey de Francia (1798). At approval it had 44 vineyards on about 256 acres and 23 wineries. Marine air reaches the valleys through the river corridor.","rslug":"southern-california"},"us-san-miguel-district":{"name":"San Miguel District","cat":"AVA","region":"Paso Robles","subregion":"Paso Robles","created":"2014","styles":["red"],"grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot, Syrah","summary":"The northern district (2014) around the town and mission of San Miguel Arcángel (1797), warm with alluvial soils along the Salinas and Estrella rivers. Large vineyards for Cabernet and Merlot.","rslug":"paso-robles"},"us-san-pasqual-valley":{"name":"San Pasqual Valley","cat":"AVA","region":"Southern California","subregion":"South Coast","created":"1981","styles":["red"],"grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, Sauvignon Blanc","summary":"One of the earliest AVAs (1981), a small valley near Escondido in San Diego County. Its vineyards have mostly disappeared, but it remains on the TTB list. The proposed Rancho Guejito AVA would partly overlap it.","rslug":"southern-california"},"us-san-ysidro-district":{"name":"San Ysidro District","cat":"AVA","region":"San Francisco Bay","subregion":"Santa Clara Valley","created":"1990","styles":["white","red"],"grapes":"Chardonnay, Pinot Noir, Merlot","summary":"A small district (1990) on the eastern side of the Santa Clara Valley near Gilroy, cooled by marine air through the Pajaro Gap. It mostly supplies Chardonnay and Pinot Noir to wineries elsewhere.","rslug":"san-francisco-bay"},"us-santa-clara-valley":{"name":"Santa Clara Valley","cat":"AVA","region":"San Francisco Bay","subregion":"San Francisco Bay","created":"1989","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, Zinfandel, Chardonnay, Grenache","summary":"The historic valley (1989) south of San Francisco Bay, now Silicon Valley. In the 19th century it had dozens of wineries, including Paul Masson's and Almaden (founded 1852 by Charles Lefranc), but urban growth erased most vineyards. Vines remain in the south around Gilroy, Morgan Hill and the Hecker Pass.","producers":["J. Lohr (origins)","Guglielmo","Sarah's Vineyard"],"contains":["San Ysidro District"],"rslug":"san-francisco-bay"},"us-santa-cruz-mountains":{"name":"Santa Cruz Mountains","cat":"AVA","region":"Central Coast","subregion":"Central Coast","created":"1981","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, Pinot Noir, Chardonnay, Merlot, Zinfandel","summary":"One of the first AVAs defined by its mountains (1982): the boundary follows elevation lines on both sides of the range, roughly above 400 feet on the ocean side and 800 feet on the bay side, to take in the slopes rather than the valleys. The range between Silicon Valley and the Pacific has vineyards scattered through redwood and oak forest, many above the fog.\n\nIts history is outsized. Paul Masson made sparkling wine at Saratoga from the 1890s; Martin Ray founded Mount Eden in the 1940s and preached 100 percent varietal wines; Ridge's Monte Bello Cabernet (made under Paul Draper from 1969) placed fifth in the red flight at the 1976 Paris tasting and won the 30th-anniversary rematch outright in 2006. Rhys, Mount Eden and others make thrilling Pinot Noir and Chardonnay.","terroir":"Steep slopes of fractured Franciscan shale, sandstone and some limestone (Monte Bello); marine influence, fog in the valleys, and the San Andreas fault running through the range.","producers":["Ridge Vineyards","Mount Eden","Rhys","Thomas Fogarty","Bonny Doon (origins)","David Bruce","Hallcrest"],"nerd_note":"Ridge has printed full ingredient lists on its labels since 2011, a direct challenge to secretive winemaking.","contains":["Ben Lomond Mountain"],"rslug":"central-coast"},"us-santa-lucia-highlands":{"name":"Santa Lucia Highlands","cat":"AVA","region":"Central Coast","subregion":"Monterey","created":"1992","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Pinot Noir, Chardonnay, Syrah","summary":"Terraced benches (1992) on the eastern flank of the Santa Lucia Range, facing east across the Salinas Valley, from about 30 to 370 m. Morning fog and fierce afternoon winds from Monterey Bay give a long, cool season, while the southeast-facing slopes catch the morning sun. The result is dense, dark-fruited, deeply coloured Pinot Noir and rich Chardonnay and Syrah.\n\nThe Pisoni family (Gary Pisoni planted from 1982) and Garys' Vineyard (with Gary Franscioni), Rosella's and Sleepy Hollow are the famous names.","terroir":"Gravelly, well-drained alluvial terraces of granitic origin; strong afternoon winds.","producers":["Pisoni","Lucia","Roar","Talbott","Morgan","Siduri (sourcing)"],"rslug":"central-coast"},"us-santa-margarita-ranch":{"name":"Santa Margarita Ranch","cat":"AVA","region":"Paso Robles","subregion":"Paso Robles","created":"2014","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, Chardonnay, Syrah","summary":"The southernmost Paso Robles district (2014), a single historic ranch near Santa Margarita that was part of the mission lands of San Luis Obispo. Cooler and wetter than most of Paso Robles; large vineyards of Cabernet and Chardonnay.","rslug":"paso-robles"},"us-santa-maria-valley":{"name":"Santa Maria Valley","cat":"AVA","region":"Santa Barbara County","subregion":"Central Coast","created":"1981","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Pinot Noir, Chardonnay, Syrah","summary":"A wide, funnel-shaped valley (1981, expanded 2010) that opens west to the Pacific, among the first AVAs in California. Morning fog, afternoon wind and a long, cool season (harvests can run into November) give Pinot Noir and Chardonnay with bright acidity and spice.\n\nBien Nacido Vineyard, planted by the Miller family from 1973 on benches above the Santa Maria River, has supplied scores of producers with vineyard-designated fruit; Au Bon Climat and Qupé share a winery on the property.","producers":["Au Bon Climat","Bien Nacido Estate","Cambria","Foxen","Byron","Qupé"],"rslug":"santa-barbara-county"},"us-santa-ynez-valley":{"name":"Santa Ynez Valley","cat":"AVA","region":"Santa Barbara County","subregion":"Central Coast","created":"1983","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Pinot Noir, Chardonnay, Syrah, Sauvignon Blanc, Grenache, Cabernet Sauvignon","summary":"The broad valley AVA (1983) along the Santa Ynez River, running from Lompoc east toward Lake Cachuma, with a strong west-to-east warming gradient. It contains Sta. Rita Hills, Ballard Canyon, Los Olivos District and Happy Canyon, and is used for blends across them.\n\nThe valley is a textbook of how distance from the ocean controls style. At the western end, Sta. Rita Hills grows taut Pinot Noir and Chardonnay; in the middle, Ballard Canyon and Los Olivos ripen Syrah and Grenache; at the eastern end, Happy Canyon is much warmer on summer afternoons and grows Cabernet. The town of Los Olivos and Solvang, a Danish-themed village, are the tourist hubs, and the 2004 film Sideways was largely shot along this valley's roads.","producers":["Firestone","Gainey","Buttonwood"],"contains":["Sta. Rita Hills","Ballard Canyon","Los Olivos District","Happy Canyon of Santa Barbara"],"rslug":"santa-barbara-county"},"us-seiad-valley":{"name":"Seiad Valley","cat":"AVA","region":"Far North California","subregion":"Siskiyou County","created":"1994","styles":["red"],"grapes":"Pinot Noir, Cabernet Sauvignon","summary":"One of California's northernmost AVAs (1994), a tiny valley on the Klamath River in Siskiyou County near the Oregon border. It was established for a single grower and has almost no commercial vineyards today.","rslug":"far-north-california"},"us-seneca-lake":{"name":"Seneca Lake","cat":"AVA","region":"Finger Lakes","subregion":"Finger Lakes","created":"2003","styles":["white","red","sparkling","sweet"],"grapes":"Riesling, Cabernet Franc, Pinot Noir, Gewürztraminer, Lemberger","summary":"The largest and deepest Finger Lake gives its name to a sub-AVA (2003) with more than 50 wineries on its slopes. The lake rarely freezes, so the 'banana belt' on its southeastern shore is the warmest part of the Finger Lakes, good for Riesling, Cabernet Franc and even Merlot. Hermann J. Wiemer, Red Newt, Ravines and Fox Run are here.","producers":["Hermann J. Wiemer","Ravines","Red Newt","Fox Run","Boundary Breaks","Anthony Road"],"rslug":"finger-lakes"},"us-shawnee-hills":{"name":"Shawnee Hills","cat":"AVA","region":"Midwest","subregion":"Illinois","created":"2006","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Chambourcin, Norton, Traminette, Vidal","summary":"An AVA (2006) in the unglaciated hills of southern Illinois, between the Ohio and Mississippi rivers. The region had about 1,250 acres of vineyards in 1890; today about a dozen wineries grow Chambourcin, Norton and Traminette.","rslug":"midwest"},"us-shenandoah-valley":{"name":"Shenandoah Valley","cat":"AVA","region":"Virginia","subregion":"Virginia and West Virginia","created":"1982","styles":["white","red"],"grapes":"Chardonnay, Riesling, Cabernet Franc, Vidal, Chambourcin","summary":"A long limestone valley AVA (1982) between the Blue Ridge and the Allegheny Mountains in Virginia and West Virginia, drier than the Piedmont thanks to the rain shadow of the mountains. Riesling, Chardonnay, Cabernet Franc and hybrids; Bluestone and Barren Ridge are among its wineries. Not to be confused with California Shenandoah Valley.","producers":["Bluestone","Barren Ridge","Muse"],"rslug":"virginia"},"us-sierra-foothills":{"name":"Sierra Foothills","cat":"AVA","region":"Sierra Foothills","subregion":"California","created":"1987","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Zinfandel, Barbera, Syrah, Petite Sirah, Sangiovese","summary":"A long umbrella AVA (1987) of about 2.6 million acres following the Sierra Nevada foothills through eight counties. Gold Rush vineyards of Zinfandel survive here, and the higher reaches grow Rhône varieties and Barbera.","contains":["El Dorado","Fair Play","California Shenandoah Valley","Fiddletown","North Yuba"],"rslug":"sierra-foothills"},"us-sierra-pelona-valley":{"name":"Sierra Pelona Valley","cat":"AVA","region":"Southern California","subregion":"Southern California","created":"2010","styles":["red"],"grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, Syrah, Zinfandel","summary":"A small valley (2010) in northern Los Angeles County near Agua Dulce, at about 760 to 1,000 m, with about 96 acres of vines at approval. A high-desert-edge climate with big temperature swings.","rslug":"southern-california"},"us-sloughhouse":{"name":"Sloughhouse","cat":"AVA","region":"Lodi","subregion":"Lodi","created":"2006","styles":["red"],"grapes":"Zinfandel, Syrah, Petite Sirah","summary":"The northeastern and warmest Lodi sub-AVA (2006), in Sacramento County in the Sierra foothills along the Cosumnes River, with red, cobbly soils. Zinfandel and Rhône varieties.","rslug":"lodi"},"us-snake-river-valley":{"name":"Snake River Valley","cat":"AVA","region":"Idaho","subregion":"Idaho and Oregon","created":"2007","styles":["white","red"],"grapes":"Riesling, Chardonnay, Syrah, Cabernet Sauvignon, Tempranillo, Malbec","summary":"A huge AVA (2007) of about 5.2 million acres along the Snake River across southwestern Idaho and two Oregon counties. Vineyards mostly sit at 600 to 900 m on volcanic and old lake-bed soils (from Pliocene Lake Idaho), with hot days, cold nights and frequent winter freeze risk. At approval it had 15 wineries and 1,107 acres of vines.","producers":["Ste. Chapelle","Koenig","Sawtooth","Cinder","Hells Canyon"],"contains":["Eagle Foothills"],"rslug":"idaho"},"us-snipes-mountain":{"name":"Snipes Mountain","cat":"AVA","region":"Yakima Valley","subregion":"Yakima Valley","created":"2009","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot, Muscat, Syrah","summary":"A small AVA (2009) of 4,145 acres on a single uplift rising from the floor of the Yakima Valley near Sunnyside. Its rocky soils include rounded cobbles carried by the ancestral Columbia River. William B. Bridgman planted vinifera on its slopes around 1917 and founded Upland Winery in 1934, and Harrison Hill Vineyard has Cabernet Sauvignon planted in 1963, among the oldest in the state. Named for cattle baron Ben Snipes, who settled at its base in the 1850s.","producers":["Upland Estates","Harrison Hill (DeLille)"],"rslug":"yakima-valley"},"us-solano-county-green-valley":{"name":"Solano County Green Valley","cat":"AVA","region":"North Coast","subregion":"North Coast","created":"1982","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Merlot, Cabernet Sauvignon, Chardonnay","summary":"A small valley (1983) in the hills of western Solano County, next door to Napa's Wild Horse Valley and east of Coombsville, cooled by air from San Pablo Bay through the Carquinez Strait. Merlot, Cabernet Sauvignon and Chardonnay grow on gentle slopes. It was renamed in its current form to distinguish it from Sonoma's Green Valley.","rslug":"north-coast"},"us-sonoita":{"name":"Sonoita","cat":"AVA","region":"Southwest and Mountain West","subregion":"Arizona","created":"1984","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Syrah, Grenache, Tempranillo, Malvasia Bianca, Cabernet Sauvignon","summary":"Arizona's first AVA (1984), a grassland basin southeast of Tucson at about 1,500 m. Dr. Gordon Dutt of the University of Arizona planted test vineyards in the 1970s and founded Sonoita Vineyards in 1983. Red, iron-rich soils and monsoon rains in July and August; Callaghan Vineyards and Dos Cabezas made it known.","producers":["Sonoita Vineyards","Callaghan","Dos Cabezas","Rune"],"rslug":"southwest-and-mountain-west"},"us-sonoma-coast":{"name":"Sonoma Coast","cat":"AVA","region":"Sonoma County","subregion":"North Coast","created":"1987","styles":["red","white","sparkling"],"grapes":"Pinot Noir, Chardonnay, Syrah","summary":"A huge AVA (1987) of more than half a million acres that runs from the Mendocino line to San Pablo Bay and reaches well inland. It was drawn so broadly that it lumps cold coastal ridges with warm inland flats, and its creation is usually traced to Sonoma-Cutrer, whose vineyards needed a single AVA to qualify for 'estate bottled' labels. For decades the name promised more coolness than many wines delivered, which is why growers on the true coast pushed for Fort Ross-Seaview (2011) and West Sonoma Coast (2022).\n\nAt its best Sonoma Coast Pinot Noir and Chardonnay are lean, saline and intensely perfumed.\n\nTTB drew Sonoma Coast in 1987 around a 'marine climate' line, but because it reached far inland to the edge of the Sonoma Valley and Russian River, it included warm sites as well as cold ones. That is why growers on the true coast pushed for Fort Ross-Seaview (2011) and West Sonoma Coast (2022). The best Sonoma Coast wines still come from ridgetops near the ocean and from the cool southern hills around Petaluma, where winds and fog keep sugars low and acidity high.","producers":["Sonoma-Cutrer","Flowers","Littorai","Peay","Failla","Hirsch"],"nerd_note":"Sonoma Coast contains the Russian River Valley, Green Valley, Petaluma Gap, Fort Ross-Seaview and West Sonoma Coast AVAs.","rslug":"sonoma-county"},"us-sonoma-mountain":{"name":"Sonoma Mountain","cat":"AVA","region":"Sonoma County","subregion":"Sonoma Valley","created":"1985","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, Pinot Noir, Zinfandel, Chardonnay","summary":"A mountain AVA (1985) on the western side of the Sonoma Valley, above Glen Ellen, including Jack London's Beauty Ranch. Vineyards above the fog get sun and a long season, and thin volcanic soils keep yields low. Cabernet Sauvignon is fine-grained and savoury; Patrick Campbell's Laurel Glen (from 1981) defined it.","producers":["Laurel Glen","Benziger","Kamen (nearby)","Repris"],"rslug":"sonoma-county"},"us-sonoma-valley":{"name":"Sonoma Valley","cat":"AVA","region":"Sonoma County","subregion":"North Coast","created":"1981","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, Zinfandel, Merlot, Chardonnay, Pinot Noir, Syrah","summary":"The 'Valley of the Moon' (1982), running from Carneros north to Santa Rosa between the Mayacamas and Sonoma Mountain. This is where Sonoma wine began: Mission San Francisco Solano (1823), General Vallejo's vineyards, Haraszthy's Buena Vista (1857) and Gundlach Bundschu's Rhinefarm (1858). Jack London ranched at Glen Ellen.\n\nThe south is cooled by San Pablo Bay; the north around Kenwood is warm, and its hillsides grow powerful Cabernet and Zinfandel. Old vineyards such as Old Hill Ranch (planted in the 1880s) carry Zinfandel field blends; Hanzell (planted 1953) is a Chardonnay and Pinot Noir landmark.","producers":["Hanzell","Gundlach Bundschu","Buena Vista","Kamen","Arrowood","Kunde","Ravenswood","Bedrock"],"contains":["Sonoma Mountain","Bennett Valley","Moon Mountain District Sonoma County"],"rslug":"sonoma-county"},"us-south-coast":{"name":"South Coast","cat":"AVA","region":"Southern California","subregion":"California","created":"1985","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Syrah, Cabernet Sauvignon, Tempranillo, Viognier, Zinfandel","summary":"Umbrella AVA (1985) of about 2.1 million acres covering the coastal portions of Orange, Riverside and San Diego counties, including Temecula Valley, Ramona Valley, San Pasqual Valley and San Luis Rey. Two further AVAs inside it were proposed by TTB: Rancho Guejito (2024) and Rancho Santa Fe (August 2026).","contains":["Temecula Valley","Ramona Valley","San Pasqual Valley","San Luis Rey"],"rslug":"southern-california"},"us-southeastern-new-england":{"name":"Southeastern New England","cat":"AVA","region":"New England","subregion":"Connecticut, Rhode Island, Massachusetts","created":"1984","styles":["white","sparkling","red"],"grapes":"Chardonnay, Vidal, Riesling, Cabernet Franc, Pinot Noir","summary":"A coastal AVA (1983) of about 2 million acres across Connecticut, Rhode Island and Massachusetts, including Cape Cod and the islands. The ocean extends the season by a few weeks compared with inland New England. Sakonnet Vineyards (now Carolyn's Sakonnet) in Little Compton, Rhode Island, petitioned for it; Westport Rivers in Massachusetts makes fine sparkling wine.","producers":["Westport Rivers","Carolyn's Sakonnet","Stonington Vineyards","Newport Vineyards"],"contains":["Martha's Vineyard"],"rslug":"new-england"},"us-southern-oregon":{"name":"Southern Oregon","cat":"AVA","region":"Southern Oregon","subregion":"Oregon","created":"2004","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Pinot Noir, Syrah, Tempranillo, Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot, Chardonnay, Viognier","summary":"Umbrella AVA (2004) joining the Umpqua and Rogue valleys, created so wines blended across them could carry a regional name. At approval it had about 120 vineyards, 3,000 acres and 17 wineries.","contains":["Umpqua Valley","Rogue Valley"],"rslug":"southern-oregon"},"us-spring-mountain-district":{"name":"Spring Mountain District","cat":"AVA","region":"Napa Valley","subregion":"Napa Valley","created":"1993","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot, Cabernet Franc, Chardonnay, Riesling","summary":"Mountain AVA (1993) on the Mayacamas slopes directly west of St. Helena, from about 120 m to almost 800 m. Steep, terraced vineyards on a mix of volcanic and sedimentary soils give Cabernet Sauvignon with firm tannin, bright acidity and a herbal, forest-floor note, and they hold some of Napa's most interesting whites: Stony Hill's taut, unoaked Chardonnay (first vintage 1952) and Smith-Madrone's dry Riesling.\n\nThe district has deep history (Tiburcio Parrott's Miravalle mansion of the 1880s later starred as the house in the TV soap Falcon Crest) and recent scars: the 2020 Glass Fire destroyed or damaged several estates here, including Newton and Cain.","terroir":"Volcanic and uplifted marine sedimentary soils, steep slopes, fog-free afternoons and cool mountain nights.","producers":["Stony Hill","Smith-Madrone","Pride Mountain","Cain","Spring Mountain Vineyard","Keenan","Ritchie Creek"],"nerd_note":"Pride Mountain Vineyards straddles the Napa-Sonoma county line, so it keeps separate records for grapes grown on each side. Spring Mountain Vineyard's 1973 Chardonnay finished fourth among the whites at the 1976 Paris tasting.","rslug":"napa-valley"},"us-squaw-valley-miramonte":{"name":"Squaw Valley-Miramonte","cat":"AVA","region":"Central Valley","subregion":"Central Valley","created":"2015","styles":["red"],"grapes":"Syrah, Cabernet Sauvignon, Zinfandel","summary":"A foothill AVA (2015) of about 44,690 acres in eastern Fresno County, at 450 to 1,000 m on the western slope of the Sierra, much cooler than the San Joaquin Valley floor. Three wineries and a few vineyards at approval.","rslug":"central-valley"},"us-st-helena":{"name":"St. Helena","cat":"AVA","region":"Napa Valley","subregion":"Napa Valley","created":"1995","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot, Cabernet Franc, Sauvignon Blanc, Zinfandel","summary":"Mid-valley AVA (1995) around the town of St. Helena, where the valley floor is narrowest and the heat of the north meets the last of the bay's influence. It is the historic heart of Napa: Charles Krug founded the valley's first commercial winery here in 1861, Beringer followed in 1876, and Heitz, Spottswoode, Duckhorn and Corison all sit within a few kilometres.\n\nCabernet Sauvignon from St. Helena tends to be plush and ripe, with the warmth of the upper valley, while the benchland vineyards on the west side (Spottswoode, Corison's Kronos) give more perfume and finesse. Merlot and Sauvignon Blanc also do well.","terroir":"Alluvial fans from the Mayacamas on the west, heavier clay and volcanic soils toward the Vaca side; warm days with modest fog.","producers":["Spottswoode","Corison","Beringer","Charles Krug","Heitz Cellar","Duckhorn","Grace Family"],"nerd_note":"Beringer made sacramental wine through Prohibition and claims to be Napa's oldest continuously operating winery; its 1884 Rhine House is a Victorian copy of the family home on the Rhine.","rslug":"napa-valley"},"us-sta-rita-hills":{"name":"Sta. Rita Hills","cat":"AVA","region":"Santa Barbara County","subregion":"Santa Ynez Valley","created":"2001","styles":["red","white","sparkling"],"grapes":"Pinot Noir, Chardonnay, Syrah","summary":"The cold western end of the Santa Ynez Valley (2001, expanded 2016), where the Santa Rosa Road and Highway 246 corridors run between hills of sand, clay and diatomaceous earth straight toward the Pacific. It is one of the coolest places in California to grow red grapes, and its Pinot Noirs combine dark fruit with high acidity, herbs and a saline edge; Chardonnay is equally tense.\n\nRichard Sanford and Michael Benedict planted the Sanford & Benedict vineyard in 1971, proving the area could ripen Pinot Noir. The AVA was approved as 'Santa Rita Hills' and shortened to 'Sta. Rita Hills' in 2005 after Chile's Viña Santa Rita objected to the use of its name.","terroir":"Marine sediments, sand, chert and diatomaceous earth; steady afternoon winds; many growing-season days under 25 degrees Celsius.","producers":["Sanford","Sea Smoke","Domaine de la Côte","Sandhi","Brewer-Clifton","Melville","Babcock","Ken Brown"],"nerd_note":"The 2016 expansion drew 41 objections arguing that the added land sat on a different landmass, a rare public fight over a few hundred acres.","rslug":"santa-barbara-county"},"us-stags-leap-district":{"name":"Stags Leap District","cat":"AVA","region":"Napa Valley","subregion":"Napa Valley","created":"1989","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot, Petit Verdot, Cabernet Franc, Sauvignon Blanc","summary":"A tiny AVA (1989) of about 1,200 ha below the rocky palisades of the Vaca Range on the Silverado Trail. The bare volcanic cliffs soak up and radiate heat, while afternoon wind from San Pablo Bay funnels through and cools the vines, a combination that gives Cabernet Sauvignon of ripe, silky texture over a firm core, the 'iron fist in a velvet glove' of local cliché.\n\nThis is where Warren Winiarski planted the S.L.V. vineyard in 1970, next to Nathan Fay's pioneering Cabernet. His 1973 S.L.V. Cabernet won the red flight at the Paris tasting of 1976. Stag's Leap Wine Cellars (S.L.V., FAY, Cask 23) has been wholly owned by Marchesi Antinori since 2023. Shafer's Hillside Select, Clos du Val (founded 1972) and Pine Ridge are also here.","terroir":"Volcanic rock and gravelly loam fans at the foot of the palisades; heat reflected from bare rock, cooled by afternoon bay breezes.","producers":["Stag's Leap Wine Cellars","Shafer","Clos du Val","Pine Ridge","Chimney Rock","Stags' Leap Winery","Cliff Lede","Quixote"],"nerd_note":"The AVA name carries no apostrophe at all: Stag's Leap Wine Cellars and Stags' Leap Winery fought for years over the name, a settlement let each keep its own punctuation, and TTB sidestepped the issue.","rslug":"napa-valley"},"us-suisun-valley":{"name":"Suisun Valley","cat":"AVA","region":"North Coast","subregion":"North Coast","created":"1982","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Petite Sirah, Cabernet Sauvignon, Zinfandel, Chardonnay","summary":"A valley (1982) just east of Napa across the Vaca Range near Fairfield, among the first AVAs approved in California. Afternoon winds from the Suisun Bay cool it, and it grows Petite Sirah, Cabernet Sauvignon and Zinfandel. Wooden Valley and Ledgewood Creek are among its wineries.","producers":["Wooden Valley","Ledgewood Creek","Sunset Cellars"],"rslug":"north-coast"},"us-swan-creek":{"name":"Swan Creek","cat":"AVA","region":"Southeast","subregion":"Yadkin Valley","created":"2008","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Cabernet Franc, Merlot, Chardonnay, Sangiovese","summary":"A small sub-AVA (2008) of the Yadkin Valley in Wilkes, Yadkin and Iredell counties, with three wineries and 75 acres at approval; sandy-loam soils of the Brushy Mountains foothills.","producers":["Raffaldini","Shadow Springs"],"rslug":"southeast"},"us-tehachapi-mountains":{"name":"Tehachapi Mountains","cat":"AVA","region":"Southern California","subregion":"Southern California","created":"2020","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, Syrah, Pinot Noir","summary":"A high mountain AVA (2020) of about 58,000 acres in Kern County, at roughly 1,000 to 1,500 m, between the San Joaquin Valley and the Mojave. Cool nights and big temperature swings; six small vineyards and one winery at approval.","rslug":"southern-california"},"us-temecula-valley":{"name":"Temecula Valley","cat":"AVA","region":"Southern California","subregion":"South Coast","created":"1984","styles":["red","white","sparkling"],"grapes":"Syrah, Tempranillo, Sangiovese, Viognier, Cabernet Sauvignon","summary":"Southern California's main wine district (1984), in Riverside County about 35 km from the Pacific. Cool air comes through the Rainbow Gap and Santa Margarita River gap each afternoon, and elevations of 300 to 450 m moderate the heat. Ely Callaway planted here in 1969; today there are dozens of wineries, mostly tourist-oriented, growing Rhône, Italian and Spanish varieties. Pierce's disease, spread by the glassy-winged sharpshooter, devastated vineyards around 1999 to 2000.","producers":["Callaway","South Coast Winery","Wilson Creek","Hart"],"rslug":"southern-california"},"us-templeton-gap-district":{"name":"Templeton Gap District","cat":"AVA","region":"Paso Robles","subregion":"Paso Robles","created":"2014","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Zinfandel, Syrah, Cabernet Sauvignon, Pinot Noir","summary":"The southwestern corner of Paso Robles (2014), where gaps in the Santa Lucia Range let ocean air and fog reach the vineyards. It is the coolest of the districts, with calcareous soils; Zinfandel has been grown here by Italian families since the early 20th century.","producers":["Turley (Pesenti Vineyard)","Castoro","Peachy Canyon"],"rslug":"paso-robles"},"us-texas-davis-mountains":{"name":"Texas Davis Mountains","cat":"AVA","region":"Texas","subregion":"Texas","created":"1998","styles":["red"],"grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot, Syrah","summary":"A high, remote AVA (1998) in the Davis Mountains of West Texas, around Fort Davis, with vineyards at about 1,500 m on volcanic soils, among the highest in the state.","rslug":"texas"},"us-texas-high-plains":{"name":"Texas High Plains","cat":"AVA","region":"Texas","subregion":"Texas","created":"1993","styles":["red","white","rose"],"grapes":"Tempranillo, Mourvèdre, Tannat, Cabernet Sauvignon, Viognier, Roussanne, Sangiovese","summary":"The engine of Texas wine (1993), about 8.9 million acres on the Llano Estacado around Lubbock, at 900 to 1,200 m. It grows the large majority of Texas wine grapes. Hot days, cool nights, wind and low humidity keep disease down, though hail and spring freeze are serious risks; soils are sandy loam over caliche. Tempranillo, Mourvèdre, Tannat, Viognier and Roussanne lead.","producers":["Llano Estacado","McPherson","Pheasant Ridge","William Chris (sourcing)","Lost Draw"],"rslug":"texas"},"us-texas-hill-country":{"name":"Texas Hill Country","cat":"AVA","region":"Texas","subregion":"Texas","created":"1991","styles":["red","white","rose"],"grapes":"Tempranillo, Mourvèdre, Sangiovese, Viognier, Blanc du Bois, Cabernet Sauvignon","summary":"By area the largest AVA wholly within one state (1991), about 9.3 million acres (some 14,500 square miles) west of Austin and San Antonio, centred on Fredericksburg. It is the heart of Texas wine tourism, with more than 100 wineries, though only a small share of the state's vineyards; many wineries use High Plains grapes. Limestone and granite soils; hot, humid summers. Two new AVAs, Llano Uplift and Hickory Sands District, were proposed inside it in 2026.","producers":["Becker","Fall Creek","Pedernales","Duchman","William Chris","Kuhlken","Ron Yates"],"contains":["Fredericksburg in the Texas Hill Country","Bell Mountain"],"rslug":"texas"},"us-texoma":{"name":"Texoma","cat":"AVA","region":"Texas","subregion":"Texas","created":"2005","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Blanc du Bois, Black Spanish, Cabernet Sauvignon, Munson hybrids","summary":"An AVA (2005) of about 3,350 square miles along the Red River on the Texas-Oklahoma border, around Lake Texoma and Denison, where T.V. Munson worked. Pierce's disease limits vinifera; Munson's hybrids and Blanc du Bois do better.","rslug":"texas"},"us-the-burn-of-columbia-valley":{"name":"The Burn of Columbia Valley","cat":"AVA","region":"Columbia Valley","subregion":"Columbia Valley","created":"2021","styles":["red"],"grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot, Syrah","summary":"An AVA (2021) of about 16,870 acres on a plateau above the Columbia River in eastern Klickitat County. The name is the one local residents have long used for the plateau; TTB declined to protect 'The Burn' on its own because the term is used elsewhere.","rslug":"columbia-valley"},"us-the-hamptons-long-island":{"name":"The Hamptons, Long Island","cat":"AVA","region":"Long Island","subregion":"Long Island","created":"1985","styles":["red","white","rose","sparkling"],"grapes":"Merlot, Chardonnay, Cabernet Franc","summary":"The southern prong (1985) of eastern Long Island, cooler and windier than the North Fork with heavier, loamy soils. Channing Daughters, Wölffer Estate and Duck Walk make Merlot, Chardonnay, sparkling wine and rosé for the summer crowd.","producers":["Wölffer Estate","Channing Daughters","Duck Walk"],"rslug":"long-island"},"us-the-rocks-district-of-milton-freewater":{"name":"The Rocks District of Milton-Freewater","cat":"AVA","region":"Walla Walla Valley","subregion":"Walla Walla Valley","created":"2015","styles":["red"],"grapes":"Syrah, Grenache, Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot, Tempranillo","summary":"The only AVA in the United States defined almost entirely by a single soil feature (2015). It sits on the Oregon side of the Walla Walla Valley, on the alluvial fan of the Walla Walla River, where the ground is a jumble of rounded basalt cobbles (Freewater cobbly loam) that store heat and radiate it at night. At approval it had about 250 acres of vines on 3,770 acres.\n\nChristophe Baron, a Champenois, spotted the stones in 1996 and planted Cayuse; the Syrahs from The Rocks have a savoury, olive, smoked-meat and iron character often compared to the northern Rhône, sometimes called 'funk' by fans.","producers":["Cayuse","Horsepower","Hors Catégorie","Reynvaan","Saviah","Delmas","Rotie"],"nerd_note":"The AVA was the first in the US whose petition rested almost wholly on soil; it is entirely within Oregon, yet nearly all the wineries that use it are across the state line in Walla Walla, Washington.","rslug":"walla-walla-valley"},"us-tip-of-the-mitt":{"name":"Tip of the Mitt","cat":"AVA","region":"Great Lakes","subregion":"Michigan","created":"2016","styles":["white","red"],"grapes":"Marquette, Frontenac, La Crescent","summary":"A cold AVA (2016) of about 2,760 square miles (1.75 million acres) at the northern tip of Michigan's Lower Peninsula, between Lakes Michigan and Huron. Cold-hardy hybrids dominate; 41 vineyards (94 acres) and 8 wineries at approval.","rslug":"great-lakes"},"us-tracy-hills":{"name":"Tracy Hills","cat":"AVA","region":"Central Valley","subregion":"Central Valley","created":"2006","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot, Chardonnay","summary":"An AVA (2006) of about 39,200 acres in the foothills southwest of Tracy, in San Joaquin and Stanislaus counties, with about 1,005 acres of vines at approval; afternoon winds from the Altamont Pass cool the area.","rslug":"central-valley"},"us-trinity-lakes":{"name":"Trinity Lakes","cat":"AVA","region":"Far North California","subregion":"Trinity County","created":"2005","styles":["red"],"grapes":"Pinot Noir, Merlot","summary":"A remote mountain AVA (2005) of about 96,000 acres around Lewiston and Trinity Lake in Trinity County, high in the Klamath Mountains. Vines are few and the climate is cool and continental.","rslug":"far-north-california"},"us-tryon-foothills":{"name":"Tryon Foothills","cat":"AVA","region":"Southeast","subregion":"North Carolina","created":"2025","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, Petit Verdot, Viognier, Chambourcin","summary":"A new AVA (effective October 2025) of about 176 square miles in Polk County, North Carolina, around Tryon and Columbus, on the escarpment where the Blue Ridge drops to the Piedmont. Its key feature is a thermal belt: on calm nights, cold air sinks to the valley floors and warmer air settles on the mid-slopes, so frost is rarer there. The petition placed 95 percent of the area in Winkler Region V; at approval it had five vineyards (about 78 acres) and four wineries.","producers":["Overmountain","Parker-Binns","Mountain Brook"],"rslug":"southeast"},"us-tualatin-hills":{"name":"Tualatin Hills","cat":"AVA","region":"Willamette Valley","subregion":"Willamette Valley","created":"2020","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Pinot Noir, Chardonnay, Riesling, Pinot Gris","summary":"The northwestern corner of the Willamette Valley (2020), about 144,000 acres in the hills west of Portland around Forest Grove, Gaston and Hillsboro. Laurelwood loess soils, higher rainfall and cooler temperatures than further south. At approval it had 33 vineyards and 21 wineries, including David Hill and Ernest Reuter's historic Forest Grove plantings.","producers":["Ruby Vineyard","David Hill","Montinore","Elk Cove (part)"],"rslug":"willamette-valley"},"us-ulupalakua":{"name":"Ulupalakua","cat":"AVA","region":"Hawaii","subregion":"Hawaii","created":"2021","styles":["red","white","sparkling"],"grapes":"Syrah, Malbec, Grenache, Chenin Blanc, Viognier","summary":"The smallest AVA in the United States (2021), about 70 acres on the leeward slopes of Haleakalā on Maui, at around 600 m, with about 16 acres of vines at approval. It is essentially MauiWine's estate on the historic Ulupalakua Ranch, where Emil Tedeschi planted grapes in 1974; volcanic soils and trade winds suit Syrah, Malbec and Chenin Blanc, and MauiWine also makes pineapple wine.","producers":["MauiWine"],"rslug":"hawaii"},"us-umpqua-valley":{"name":"Umpqua Valley","cat":"AVA","region":"Southern Oregon","subregion":"Southern Oregon","created":"1984","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Pinot Noir, Tempranillo, Syrah, Albariño, Riesling","summary":"The 'hundred valleys of the Umpqua' (1984) around Roseburg, a tangle of small valleys where the Klamath Mountains, Cascades and Coast Range meet. It has the most varied geology in Oregon and a climate between the cool Willamette and the warm Rogue. Richard Sommer's HillCrest (1961) restarted Oregon wine here, and Abacela planted the Pacific Northwest's first Tempranillo in 1995.","producers":["Abacela","HillCrest","Reustle","Girardet"],"contains":["Elkton Oregon","Red Hill Douglas County, Oregon"],"rslug":"southern-oregon"},"us-upper-cumberland":{"name":"Upper Cumberland","cat":"AVA","region":"Southeast","subregion":"Tennessee","created":"2024","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Chambourcin, Cabernet Franc, Vidal, Muscadine","summary":"A large AVA (2024) of about 2.19 million acres on the Highland Rim and Cumberland Plateau in Middle Tennessee, with 55 small vineyards (about 71 acres) and nine wineries at approval. The petition stressed limestone and sandstone geology, elevation, soils and climate.","rslug":"southeast"},"us-upper-hiwassee-highlands":{"name":"Upper Hiwassee Highlands","cat":"AVA","region":"Southeast","subregion":"Georgia and North Carolina","created":"2014","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Cabernet Franc, Chardonnay, Chambourcin, Traminette","summary":"A mountain AVA (2014) of about 690 square miles in the Hiwassee River basin straddling Georgia and North Carolina. At approval 26 vineyards grew about 54 acres of hybrids, natives and vinifera, and five wineries were operating.","producers":["Crane Creek","Hightower Creek"],"rslug":"southeast"},"us-upper-hudson":{"name":"Upper Hudson","cat":"AVA","region":"New York","subregion":"New York","created":"2018","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Marquette, Frontenac, La Crescent, Seyval Blanc","summary":"An AVA (2018) north of the Hudson River Region around Saratoga and Albany, defined by its colder climate, which favours cold-hardy hybrids like Marquette, Frontenac and La Crescent; about 1,500 square miles.","rslug":"new-york"},"us-upper-lake-valley":{"name":"Upper Lake Valley","cat":"AVA","region":"Lake County","subregion":"Clear Lake","created":"2022","styles":["white","red"],"grapes":"Sauvignon Blanc, Cabernet Sauvignon","summary":"A valley (2022) at the north end of Clear Lake around the town of Upper Lake, about 17,360 acres. The petition stressed hydrogeology: a shallow water table fed by streams from the Mendocino National Forest, plus soils and a climate that differ from the rest of Clear Lake. Sauvignon Blanc and Cabernet Sauvignon dominate.","rslug":"lake-county"},"us-upper-mississippi-river-valley":{"name":"Upper Mississippi River Valley","cat":"AVA","region":"Midwest","subregion":"Illinois, Iowa, Minnesota, Wisconsin","created":"2009","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Marquette, Frontenac, La Crescent, St. Croix, Edelweiss","summary":"The second-largest AVA by area (2009), about 19 million acres along the Mississippi in Illinois, Iowa, Minnesota and Wisconsin, including the unglaciated 'Driftless Area'. Cold winters limit growers to hybrids bred at the University of Minnesota and by Elmer Swenson, such as Marquette, Frontenac, La Crescent and Brianna.","contains":["Lake Wisconsin"],"rslug":"midwest"},"us-van-duzer-corridor":{"name":"Van Duzer Corridor","cat":"AVA","region":"Willamette Valley","subregion":"Willamette Valley","created":"2018","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Pinot Noir, Chardonnay, Pinot Gris","summary":"A low gap through the Coast Range (2018) about 59,900 acres in size, through which cold Pacific air flows into the Willamette Valley every summer afternoon, dropping temperatures fast. The AVA covers the valley floor and foothills where that wind hits hardest; at approval it had 17 vineyards on about 1,000 acres. Pinot Noir from here has thick skins, dark colour and high acidity.","producers":["Johan","Cherry Hill","Left Coast"],"nerd_note":"The wind is so reliable that growers call it the 'Van Duzer winds'; TTB clarified Eola-Amity Hills' boundary when it created this AVA.","rslug":"willamette-valley"},"us-verde-valley":{"name":"Verde Valley","cat":"AVA","region":"Southwest and Mountain West","subregion":"Arizona","created":"2021","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Syrah, Grenache, Malvasia Bianca, Tempranillo","summary":"A valley AVA (2021) of about 200 square miles along the Verde River in Yavapai County, near Cottonwood and Jerome, at roughly 1,000 to 1,500 m. Maynard James Keenan of Caduceus Cellars helped lead its rise. At approval it had 24 vineyards (about 125 acres) and 11 wineries.","producers":["Caduceus","Page Springs","Alcantara"],"rslug":"southwest-and-mountain-west"},"us-virginia-peninsula":{"name":"Virginia Peninsula","cat":"AVA","region":"Virginia","subregion":"Virginia","created":"2021","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot, Chardonnay, Petit Verdot","summary":"A coastal AVA (2021) of about 673,000 acres between the James and York rivers, including Williamsburg and Jamestown, where Acte 12 ordered colonists to plant vines in 1619. It was petitioned by The Williamsburg Winery; at approval it had five vineyards on about 112 acres.","producers":["The Williamsburg Winery"],"rslug":"virginia"},"us-virginias-eastern-shore":{"name":"Virginia's Eastern Shore","cat":"AVA","region":"Virginia","subregion":"Virginia","created":"1991","styles":["white","red"],"grapes":"Chardonnay, Merlot, Cabernet Franc, Petit Verdot","summary":"A coastal AVA (1990) on the Delmarva Peninsula between Chesapeake Bay and the Atlantic. Water on both sides moderates temperatures and extends the season; sandy soils. Chatham Vineyards on Church Creek is the leading winery.","producers":["Chatham Vineyards"],"rslug":"virginia"},"us-wahluke-slope":{"name":"Wahluke Slope","cat":"AVA","region":"Columbia Valley","subregion":"Columbia Valley","created":"2005","styles":["red"],"grapes":"Merlot, Cabernet Sauvignon, Syrah, Grenache","summary":"A broad, south-facing alluvial fan (2006) of about 81,000 acres on the north bank of the Columbia, east of Mattawa, one of the warmest and driest places in Washington. Huge vineyards supply many wineries with ripe Merlot, Cabernet and Syrah.","producers":["Milbrandt","Sagemoor (sourcing)"],"rslug":"columbia-valley"},"us-walla-walla-valley":{"name":"Walla Walla Valley","cat":"AVA","region":"Walla Walla Valley","subregion":"Columbia Valley","created":"1984","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot, Syrah, Cabernet Franc, Grenache","summary":"A celebrated AVA (1984, realigned 2001) shared by Washington and Oregon, around the town of Walla Walla at the foot of the Blue Mountains. Rainfall rises sharply toward the mountains (from about 300 mm in the west to more than 500 mm near the foothills), so some vineyards can be dry-farmed. Soils are wind-blown loess over Missoula flood deposits, plus basalt cobbles in The Rocks District.\n\nLeonetti (1977), Woodward Canyon (1981) and L'Ecole No 41 (1983) built the valley's name for Cabernet, Merlot and Syrah; it now has well over 100 wineries. TTB proposed a Mill Creek-Walla Walla Valley sub-AVA of about 4,900 acres in August 2026.\n\nWalla Walla is Washington's wine town par excellence: a small, historic farming town with over 100 wineries and tasting rooms in a valley that also grows its famous sweet onions. The valley shares its AVA with Oregon, and the Oregon side's cobbled Rocks District gives the Syrahs that made the area famous among sommeliers. A severe freeze in January 2024 damaged many vines, and growers continue to experiment with burying canes and frost protection.","producers":["Leonetti","Woodward Canyon","L'Ecole No 41","Pepper Bridge","Gramercy Cellars","Cayuse","Seven Hills","Long Shadows"],"contains":["The Rocks District of Milton-Freewater"],"rslug":"walla-walla-valley"},"us-warren-hills":{"name":"Warren Hills","cat":"AVA","region":"Mid-Atlantic","subregion":"New Jersey","created":"1988","styles":["white","red"],"grapes":"Chardonnay, Cabernet Franc, Vidal, Chambourcin","summary":"An AVA (1988) in the valleys of Warren County in northwestern New Jersey, where limestone valleys run between forested ridges of the Highlands. Cold winters favour hybrids such as Vidal and Chambourcin alongside cool-climate vinifera such as Chardonnay and Cabernet Franc.","producers":["Villa Milagro","Brook Hollow"],"rslug":"mid-atlantic"},"us-west-elks":{"name":"West Elks","cat":"AVA","region":"Southwest and Mountain West","subregion":"Colorado","created":"2001","styles":["white","red"],"grapes":"Pinot Noir, Riesling, Gewürztraminer, Chardonnay","summary":"One of the highest AVAs in the US (2001), along the North Fork of the Gunnison River around Paonia and Hotchkiss, at about 1,700 to 1,900 m. Cool and high, it grows Pinot Noir, Riesling and Gewürztraminer; Stone Cottage and Azura are among its small wineries.","producers":["Stone Cottage","Black Bridge","Terror Creek"],"rslug":"southwest-and-mountain-west"},"us-west-sonoma-coast":{"name":"West Sonoma Coast","cat":"AVA","region":"Sonoma County","subregion":"Sonoma Coast","created":"2022","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Pinot Noir, Chardonnay, Syrah","summary":"Approved in 2022, West Sonoma Coast (about 141,800 acres) is the 'true' Sonoma Coast that growers had wanted since the 1990s: the band nearest the Pacific, from the Gualala River to the Petaluma Gap area, including the ridges around Annapolis, Fort Ross, Freestone and Occidental. TTB accepted that topography, geology and the marine climate set it apart from the inland parts of the old Sonoma Coast AVA.\n\nVineyards sit on steep ridges between redwood forest, often above the fog, in cold, windy conditions with long hang time. Pinot Noir and Chardonnay are tense and aromatic, Syrah is peppery and lean.","terroir":"Marine sediments of the Franciscan complex and sandstone; strong marine influence with fog in the hollows and sun on ridgetops.","producers":["Flowers","Hirsch","Peay","Littorai","Wayfarer","Marcassin","Ceritas","Red Car"],"contains":["Fort Ross-Seaview"],"rslug":"sonoma-county"},"us-western-connecticut-highlands":{"name":"Western Connecticut Highlands","cat":"AVA","region":"New England","subregion":"Connecticut","created":"1988","styles":["white","red"],"grapes":"Seyval Blanc, Vidal, Chardonnay, Cabernet Franc","summary":"An upland AVA (1988) in western Connecticut's Litchfield Hills, with cold winters and a short season; Hopkins Vineyard and others grow hybrids and cool-climate vinifera.","producers":["Hopkins Vineyard","Haight-Brown"],"rslug":"new-england"},"us-white-bluffs":{"name":"White Bluffs","cat":"AVA","region":"Columbia Valley","subregion":"Columbia Valley","created":"2021","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot, Syrah, Riesling","summary":"An AVA (2021) of about 93,700 acres on a plateau east of the Columbia near Pasco, named for the pale cliffs of ancient lake sediments (the Ringold Formation) along the river. The plateau's flat top and south-facing slopes are cut by the Ringold and Koontz coulees.","rslug":"columbia-valley"},"us-wild-horse-valley":{"name":"Wild Horse Valley","cat":"AVA","region":"Napa Valley","subregion":"Napa Valley","created":"1988","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Pinot Noir, Chardonnay","summary":"A small, cool upland valley (1988) on the ridge east of Napa city that straddles the Napa-Solano county line, with most of it in Solano County. At around 300 to 600 m and open to wind from the bay, it is one of the coolest corners of the Napa Valley AVA, suited to Pinot Noir and Chardonnay. Plantings are tiny and the name rarely appears on labels.","terroir":"Volcanic soils on a windy upland; cool, marine-influenced climate.","nerd_note":"It is one of the few Napa sub-AVAs that lies mostly outside Napa County.","rslug":"napa-valley"},"us-willamette-valley":{"name":"Willamette Valley","cat":"AVA","region":"Willamette Valley","subregion":"Oregon","created":"1983","styles":["red","white","sparkling","rose"],"grapes":"Pinot Noir, Chardonnay, Pinot Gris, Pinot Blanc, Riesling, Gamay","summary":"Oregon's flagship AVA (1983, expanded 2016) of about 3.4 million acres, with most of the state's wineries and vineyards. Cool, wet winters and dry summers, with a long, mild ripening period in September and October, give Pinot Noir of red-fruit perfume, silk and bright acidity. Chardonnay has become a second calling card since the 2010s, made in a leaner, reductive style.\n\nThe valley's story is one of outsiders proving experts wrong. In the 1960s UC Davis advised that Oregon was too cold and wet for vinifera; David Lett, Dick Erath, David Adelsheim and Dick Ponzi planted anyway, choosing hillsides above the frost-prone floor. Today vintages swing between cool, rainy years that give translucent, high-acid Pinot and warm years (2014, 2015, 2018, 2022) that give richer, darker wines. Burgundian investment (Drouhin from 1987, Louis Jadot's Résonance, Lafon's Lingua Franca partnership, Roserock) and Champagne interest in sparkling wine have made the Willamette the New World's closest analogue to the Côte d'Or in spirit, though its soils (volcanic, marine sediment, loess) are quite different.","producers":["The Eyrie Vineyards","Domaine Drouhin Oregon","Beaux Frères","Bergström","Cristom","Evening Land","Antica Terra","Adelsheim","Ponzi","Domaine Serene"],"contains":["Dundee Hills","Eola-Amity Hills","Chehalem Mountains","Ribbon Ridge","Laurelwood District","Yamhill-Carlton","McMinnville","Van Duzer Corridor","Tualatin Hills","Lower Long Tom","Mount Pisgah, Polk County, Oregon"],"rslug":"willamette-valley"},"us-willcox":{"name":"Willcox","cat":"AVA","region":"Southwest and Mountain West","subregion":"Arizona","created":"2016","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Syrah, Grenache, Mourvèdre, Tempranillo, Malvasia Bianca, Viognier","summary":"Arizona's main growing area (2016), about 526,000 acres in a high basin around a dry lake bed (Willcox Playa) at about 1,300 to 1,600 m. At approval it had 21 vineyards on about 454 acres and 18 wineries; it now grows the majority of Arizona's wine grapes, mostly Rhône and Spanish varieties.","producers":["Bodega Pierce","Sand-Reckoner","Carlson Creek","Aridus"],"rslug":"southwest-and-mountain-west"},"us-willow-creek":{"name":"Willow Creek","cat":"AVA","region":"Far North California","subregion":"Humboldt and Trinity counties","created":"1983","styles":["red"],"grapes":"Zinfandel, Pinot Noir, Cabernet Sauvignon","summary":"A small AVA (1983) along the Trinity River in eastern Humboldt and western Trinity counties, where coastal fog meets inland heat. A handful of growers make Zinfandel and Pinot Noir.","rslug":"far-north-california"},"us-winters-highlands":{"name":"Winters Highlands","cat":"AVA","region":"Sacramento Valley","subregion":"Yolo and Solano counties","created":"2023","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, Petite Sirah, Tempranillo","summary":"A small upland AVA (2023) of about 7,300 acres in the foothills west of Winters, straddling Yolo and Solano counties near the Napa line. The petition cited higher humidity and less rainfall than surrounding areas, plus distinctive soils; vineyards grow Bordeaux and Mediterranean varieties.","rslug":"sacramento-valley"},"us-wisconsin-ledge":{"name":"Wisconsin Ledge","cat":"AVA","region":"Midwest","subregion":"Wisconsin","created":"2012","styles":["white","red"],"grapes":"Marquette, La Crescent, St. Pepin, Frontenac","summary":"An AVA (2012) of about 3,800 square miles (2.4 million acres) in northeastern Wisconsin along the Niagara Escarpment, including the Door Peninsula. Lake Michigan and Green Bay moderate the climate; cold-hardy hybrids dominate.","rslug":"midwest"},"us-yadkin-valley":{"name":"Yadkin Valley","cat":"AVA","region":"Southeast","subregion":"North Carolina","created":"2002","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Cabernet Franc, Merlot, Chardonnay, Viognier, Chambourcin","summary":"North Carolina's first AVA (2002), about 1.4 million acres in the Piedmont along the Yadkin River northwest of Winston-Salem. Former tobacco farmers replanted with vinifera and hybrids; more than 40 wineries make Cabernet Franc, Merlot, Chardonnay and Viognier. Shelton Vineyards (1999) led the expansion.","producers":["Shelton","Raffaldini","Jones von Drehle","RagApple Lassie"],"contains":["Swan Creek"],"rslug":"southeast"},"us-yakima-valley":{"name":"Yakima Valley","cat":"AVA","region":"Yakima Valley","subregion":"Columbia Valley","created":"1983","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot, Syrah, Chardonnay, Riesling","summary":"Washington's first AVA (1983), along the Yakima River from Union Gap to near the Tri-Cities. It is the historical centre of Washington grape growing: Concord for juice, then wine grapes from the 1960s. Today about a third of the state's wine grapes grow here. The west end is cooler, the east end warmer; famous vineyards include Red Willow (planted 1973, an early source of Syrah) and Boushey.","producers":["Columbia Winery (Red Willow)","Owen Roe","Cote Bonneville","Boushey (grower)","DeLille (sourcing)"],"contains":["Red Mountain","Rattlesnake Hills","Snipes Mountain","Candy Mountain","Goose Gap"],"rslug":"yakima-valley"},"us-yamhill-carlton":{"name":"Yamhill-Carlton","cat":"AVA","region":"Willamette Valley","subregion":"Willamette Valley","created":"2004","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Pinot Noir, Chardonnay, Pinot Gris","summary":"A horseshoe of low foothills (2004, boundary refined 2010) around the towns of Yamhill and Carlton, limited to elevations between 200 and 1,000 feet. Coarse-grained, ancient marine sediments (Willakenzie) drain quickly, and vines ripen early; the Pinot Noirs are dark-fruited and broad-shouldered. The AVA was approved as 'Yamhill-Carlton District' and later shortened.","producers":["Ken Wright","Soter","Shea Wine Cellars","Elk Cove","Anne Amie","WillaKenzie"],"nerd_note":"Ken Wright's Carlton winery helped turn the town into a tasting-room hub; Shea Vineyard is one of Oregon's most bottled single vineyards.","rslug":"willamette-valley"},"us-york-mountain":{"name":"York Mountain","cat":"AVA","region":"San Luis Obispo County","subregion":"Central Coast","created":"1983","styles":["red"],"grapes":"Pinot Noir, Zinfandel, Syrah","summary":"A tiny, cool AVA (1983) in the Santa Lucia Range between Paso Robles and the coast, at about 450 m. Andrew York founded a winery here in 1882, one of the oldest in the county; marine air makes it far cooler than Paso Robles, which is why TTB kept it separate.","nerd_note":"York Mountain sits outside the Paso Robles AVA even though it is almost surrounded by it.","rslug":"san-luis-obispo-county"},"us-yorkville-highlands":{"name":"Yorkville Highlands","cat":"AVA","region":"Mendocino County","subregion":"Mendocino","created":"1998","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Sauvignon Blanc, Pinot Noir, Cabernet Sauvignon, Syrah","summary":"Rolling, rocky hills (1998) along Highway 128 between Cloverdale and the Anderson Valley, at about 300 to 760 m. Gravelly, low-fertility soils and a climate between the warm Alexander Valley and the cool Anderson Valley suit Sauvignon Blanc, Pinot Noir and Bordeaux and Rhône reds.","producers":["Yorkville Cellars","Maple Creek"],"rslug":"mendocino-county"},"us-yountville":{"name":"Yountville","cat":"AVA","region":"Napa Valley","subregion":"Napa Valley","created":"1999","styles":["red","white","sparkling"],"grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot, Cabernet Franc, Petit Verdot, Chardonnay","summary":"Named for George C. Yount, who planted the valley's first vines in the late 1830s, Yountville (1999) sits where the valley floor starts to feel the bay: cooler and windier than Oakville, with clay-rich and gravelly soils around a few low volcanic knolls. Cabernet here is firmer and more savoury, and Merlot does well.\n\nDominus Estate is the flagship: Christian Moueix of Pomerol leased and later bought the historic Napanook vineyard (once owned by Inglenook's John Daniel Jr.), made his first Dominus in 1983, and in 1997 built a winery by Herzog & de Meuron whose walls are wire cages filled with local basalt. Domaine Chandon, Moët & Chandon's California outpost (1973), is also here.","terroir":"Mixed alluvial and clay soils with volcanic knolls; marine air from San Pablo Bay moderates afternoons.","producers":["Dominus Estate","Domaine Chandon","Kapcsándy","Cliff Lede (Yountville vineyards)"],"nerd_note":"Dominus's gabion walls let air and light filter through the stones, cooling the cellar without much air conditioning.","rslug":"napa-valley"},"us-yucaipa-valley":{"name":"Yucaipa Valley","cat":"AVA","region":"Southern California","subregion":"Southern California","created":"2024","styles":["red"],"grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, Syrah, Zinfandel","summary":"A new AVA (2024) of 36,467 acres in San Bernardino County, in the foothills of the San Bernardino Mountains, with about 23 vineyards and two wineries at approval. Elevations of 600 to 1,500 m and cool canyon winds set it apart from the hot inland valleys.","rslug":"southern-california"}}};
